WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:21.720
In the year 1815, the English colonial&nbsp;
official Thomas Stamford Raffles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:27.360
would travel across the island of&nbsp;
Java in what is today Indonesia.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:39.680
At this time, the world was torn by war.&nbsp;
In Europe, the Napoleonic Wars were raging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:48.200
and the Earth's oceans had become just another&nbsp;
battlefront. The lush tropical island of Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:55.080
had been until recently a Dutch colony, but the&nbsp;
Netherlands had been conquered by Napoleon in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:03.560
advance across Europe. And so the Dutch colonies&nbsp;
had fallen under the control of the French. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:11.960
the British desired to take Java for themselves&nbsp;
and strike a blow at Napoleon's overseas empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:22.171
In 1811, British Marines launched an invasion&nbsp;
of Java, landing among its coral reefs and sky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.171 --> 00:01:31.000
blue oceans, clambering through its shady mangrove&nbsp;
forests and up its beaches of black volcanic sand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:38.120
climbing the steep cliffs in the shadow&nbsp;
of its towering volcanoes. From there they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:44.920
overwhelmed the inexperienced Dutch defenders&nbsp;
and took the island for the British Empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:53.680
With them came the Englishman Thomas Stamford&nbsp;
Raffles who later wrote about these events.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.760
Holland had ceased to be an&nbsp;
independent nation and in the year 1811&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:07.400
the French flag was hoisted over Java. But&nbsp;
on the 17th of September of the same year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:13.280
a capitulation was entered into by which all the&nbsp;
dependencies fell into the hands of Great Britain.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:23.360
Raffles was a lifelong sailor and had actually&nbsp;
been born at sea on a ship commanded by his father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:31.200
off the coast of Jamaica. He became a clerk in the&nbsp;
British East India Company at the age of 14 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:38.800
from there became a true company man. He had spent&nbsp;
the best part of the last decade furthering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:46.040
interests of British colonialism in East Asia and&nbsp;
had even become fluent in the language of Malay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:54.320
When Java was captured by the British, partly&nbsp;
as a result of his skill at languages, Raffles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:02.760
was granted the position of left tenant governor.&nbsp;
At the age of just 30, Raffles found himself in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:09.920
charge of an island a thousand kilometers&nbsp;
across, home to more than 6 million people.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:25.560
Raffles was fascinated by Java. He remarked&nbsp;
on its dramatic landscape of soaring volcanoes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:32.280
and steamy rainforests, its fertile&nbsp;
rice terraces and remarkable culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:41.400
as he wrote home to King George III. The gracious&nbsp;
permission which I have received affords me an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:47.080
opportunity of interesting your royal highness&nbsp;
in favor of the amiiable and ingenious people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:53.640
whose country they describe. A chain of very high&nbsp;
mountains runs along the whole length of Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:58.360
Even in July and August, the atmosphere&nbsp;
is refreshed by occasional showers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:02.680
and the landscape is at all times of the&nbsp;
year covered with the brightest green.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.360
But most of all, he was&nbsp;
fascinated by Java's antiquities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:17.720
Everywhere among its forests and shaded&nbsp;
rice terraces could be seen the remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:24.600
of the countless temples, palaces, and&nbsp;
even whole ancient cities left in ruins.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:34.360
A few years later, around 1815, he&nbsp;
embarked on a journey around the island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:39.040
with the aim of surveying&nbsp;
these ruins and writing a book.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:49.160
Although by then Java was an overwhelmingly Muslim&nbsp;
land, Raffles found everywhere the remains of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:57.320
older culture hiding just beneath the surface.&nbsp;
As he writes in his work, the history of Java,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:05.440
the Muhammadan religion, as it present exists on&nbsp;
Java, seems only to have penetrated the surface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:10.880
and to have taken but little root in the heart&nbsp;
of Javans. But the antiquities of Java consist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:15.800
of ruins of edififices and in particular&nbsp;
of temples sacred to the former worship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:21.520
Images of deities found within them and scattered&nbsp;
throughout the country, either sculpted in stone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:28.280
or cast in metal. inscriptions on stone and&nbsp;
copper in ancient characters and ancient coins.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:37.840
Raffles was fascinated by these remains&nbsp;
which seemed to confirm stories he had heard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:42.800
Stories of a great empire that&nbsp;
had risen up on that island.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:50.600
A thriving combination of Hinduism and Buddhism&nbsp;
that had flourished for centuries, spread its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:57.240
influence across the sea to countless other&nbsp;
islands in the Indonesian archipelago and built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:06.400
what may have been one of the largest empires in&nbsp;
Southeast Asia. This was the empire of Majapahit.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:15.800
The numerous and interesting remains of former art&nbsp;
and grandeur which exist in the ruins of temples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:21.840
and other edifices. The abundant treasures&nbsp;
of sculpture and statuary either lay entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:27.200
buried under rubbish or were but partially&nbsp;
examined. Nothing therefore of the ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:32.480
history of the people of their institutions&nbsp;
prior to the introduction of Muhammadanism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:37.680
of their magnificence and power could be&nbsp;
accurately known or fully relied on. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:43.640
grandeur of their ancestors sounds like a fable&nbsp;
and it is only when it can be traced in monuments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:48.280
which cannot be falsified that we are led to&nbsp;
give credit to their traditions concerning it.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:58.160
Raffles became convinced that he could find&nbsp;
the remnants of the civilization of Majapahit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:03.880
and so he traveled the length and breadth of&nbsp;
the island and visited every ruined site he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:12.600
could along the way. He stopped at the ancient&nbsp;
cities of Kadiri and Singasari and found there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:21.520
the crumbling remains of city walls, palaces and&nbsp;
carved statues of the Buddha alongside Hindu gods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:30.920
But when he got to the valley of the Brontis&nbsp;
River in East Java, the locals advised him not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:39.080
go near the ruins. They told him that a terrible&nbsp;
curse still lingered over those crumbling stones.

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:46.040
The natives had a superstition that the&nbsp;
site of this ancient capital cannot be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:50.200
visited without some misfortune attaching&nbsp;
to the party who undertake the visit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:54.520
Those whom I had with some difficulty&nbsp;
induced to accompany me to the spot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:58.280
did not fail to assure me that I should&nbsp;
lose my government within the year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:07.920
Finally, Raffles visited the great capital of&nbsp;
Majapahit, which his guides told him lay buried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:16.840
near the town of Trrowan. At Trrowan, Raffles&nbsp;
hoped to find a magnificent ruined city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:25.400
testifying to the former greatness of this power.&nbsp;
But when he arrived, he would be disappointed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:32.760
He would find little more than a scatter&nbsp;
of broken brick work and assorted sculpture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:40.400
littering the landscape, buried in volcanic&nbsp;
ash and shaded beneath the towering teak trees.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:47.800
A village adjacent is called&nbsp;
Trulan or the light of the moon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:52.640
The ruins of the palace and several&nbsp;
gateways of burnt brick are to be seen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:58.680
but the whole country for many miles is thickly&nbsp;
covered with a stately teak forest which appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:03.160
to have been the growth of ages so that it is&nbsp;
difficult to trace the outline of this former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:10.000
capital. Ruins of temples mostly executed in brick&nbsp;
are scattered about the country for many miles and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:17.280
attest to the extent and grandeur of this pride&nbsp;
of Java. The temples are beautifully decorated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:23.080
with representations of flowers and other peculiar&nbsp;
ornaments which it would be difficult to describe.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:33.120
In fact, the local people turned out to be right&nbsp;
and perhaps Raffles should have listened to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:42.760
warnings. Raffles would only govern the island of&nbsp;
Java for another year. The Napoleonic Wars soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:50.280
came to an end and the British returned the colony&nbsp;
to the now independent Dutch, relieving raffles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:57.720
of his post. And he would go on to suffer&nbsp;
more misfortune in the years that followed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:04.680
Just 10 years later, he would have a stroke&nbsp;
likely caused by an underlying condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:12.760
in the blood vessels of his brain. This stroke&nbsp;
would kill him the day before his 45th birthday.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:22.040
As Raffles wandered about those ruins of crumbling&nbsp;
red brick at the sight of the ancient city of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:29.240
Majapahit, he had no idea that his fate was&nbsp;
already sealed by the birth defect lurking in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:36.960
his head. But perhaps that day he thought about&nbsp;
life and death as he walked beneath the shade of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:44.120
that teak forest and listened to the calling&nbsp;
of koels and minor birds high in the trees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:52.080
Perhaps he thought about the rise and fall of&nbsp;
empires and the changing fortunes of the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:57.720
Perhaps he wondered how such a power&nbsp;
had risen up on the island of Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:04.880
and left such a mark on the memories and&nbsp;
imaginations of that land's people. Perhaps he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:12.640
asked himself how its people had constructed these&nbsp;
temples and palaces, these walls and reservoirs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:20.720
carved such beautiful and haunting monuments,&nbsp;
and then fallen all at once into ruin.

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:04.000
My name's Paul Cooper and you're listening to the&nbsp;
fall of civilizations podcast. Each episode I look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:10.200
at a civilization of the past that rose to glory&nbsp;
and then collapsed into the ashes of history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:16.480
I want to ask what did they have&nbsp;
in common? What led to their fall?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:23.360
and what did it feel like to be a person alive&nbsp;
at the time who witnessed the end of their world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:31.560
In this episode, I want to tell the story of&nbsp;
the Majapahit Empire of medieval Indonesia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:39.120
I want to show how this empire of the&nbsp;
islands grew up on the volcanic land of Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:45.040
and how it beat the odds to survive&nbsp;
in a world of medieval superpowers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:52.040
I want to show how Majapahit expanded&nbsp;
its influence and dominion to other lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:58.040
over the seas and undertook voyages&nbsp;
that have few precedents in history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:05.440
And I want to show what happened to bring this&nbsp;
Empire of the Islands finally crashing down.

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:24.800
The surface of planet Earth&nbsp;
is a thin crust of solid rock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:29.040
which sits over the softer and&nbsp;
much hotter mantle beneath it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:38.920
This crust can be as much as 80 km thick&nbsp;
in places and as thin as 10 km in others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:48.560
and it is divided into seven major tectonic&nbsp;
plates. The largest of these is the Pacific plate.

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:58.400
It is a vast sheet of solid rock more&nbsp;
than 100 million square km in size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:06.080
that makes up much of the floor of the Pacific&nbsp;
Ocean and which alone accounts for about a fifth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:15.960
of Earth's total surface. The Pacific plate is&nbsp;
made up almost entirely of oceanic crust which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:24.360
is different to the continental crust that we all&nbsp;
live on. Oceanic crust is typically much thinner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:32.840
but made of denser bassalt rock. For this reason,&nbsp;
whenever it impacts with other tectonic plates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:38.760
the Pacific plate is pushed downwards into&nbsp;
the Earth's upper mantle, creating a zone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:46.480
of subduction. And these subduction zones are&nbsp;
renowned for their violent volcanic activity.

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:56.200
Due to its immense size and density, the&nbsp;
Pacific plate has the most subduction zones of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.200 --> 00:15:04.040
any tectonic plate. And as a result, its borders&nbsp;
are the most volcanically active areas on Earth.

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:20.600
This ring of raging volcanoes stretches in a&nbsp;
vast horseshoe shape for more than 40,000 km&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:27.120
or about the circumference of the earth from&nbsp;
the southern tip of South America all around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:33.720
the western shore of the Americas and then&nbsp;
through the sea down the eastern coast of Asia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:39.320
To geologists, it is called&nbsp;
the CircumPacific Belt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:47.200
but in popular usage, it has come to be known&nbsp;
by another name, the Pacific Ring of Fire.

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:06.680
The 19th century English&nbsp;
geologist George P. Scrope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:11.240
was one of the first to describe&nbsp;
this far-ranging phenomenon.

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:19.000
A striking and constant&nbsp;
delineation of volcanic spiracles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:24.080
is displayed in the numerous archipelos&nbsp;
which bordered the eastern coast of Asia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:30.920
Beginning from the peninsula of Kamchatka,&nbsp;
Japan, the Luchu, Philippines and Malocas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:37.760
form a scarcely interrupted linear series of&nbsp;
volcanic islands amongst which many contain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:44.120
vents in habitual activity and bearing marks&nbsp;
of recent and extremely violent eruptions.

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:54.320
The Pacific Ring of Fire is home&nbsp;
to 3/4 of all the world's volcanoes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.880 --> 00:17:03.240
and 90% of the earthquakes on Earth take place&nbsp;
along this line. The Ring of Fire has given us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:11.560
some of the world's most iconic peaks, including&nbsp;
the elegant cone of Mount Fuji in Japan and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:19.480
violently explosive Mount St. Helens in the United&nbsp;
States as well as the volcanoes of Mount Tambora&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:28.000
and Krakatoa whose 1815 and 1883 eruptions&nbsp;
were among the largest in recorded history

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:41.600
as it is dragged down into the depths of&nbsp;
the planet. The subducting Pacific plate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:48.520
has also created some of the lowest places on&nbsp;
the Earth's surface, giving rise to a series of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:56.600
dramatic oceanic trenches. The deepest&nbsp;
of these, known as the Marana Trench,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.600 --> 00:18:05.440
reaches a sea depth of 11 km, deeper than Mount&nbsp;
Everest is tall. And in the areas where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:13.360
ring of fire passes through open ocean, it has&nbsp;
also created a huge number of volcanic islands.

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:21.360
As volcanoes erupt under the sea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:29.600
magma spews out of the planet's mantle and cools&nbsp;
on contact with cold seaater into solid rock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:38.720
Over time, whole islands form around these&nbsp;
volcanoes. And on these islands, the lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:46.400
and deaths and dramas and tragedies of millions&nbsp;
of human lives have played out throughout history.

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:55.640
Perhaps the region where the Pacific&nbsp;
Ring of Fire has had the most effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.640 --> 00:19:02.200
in sculpting the landscape is in the area&nbsp;
of what is today Malaysia and Indonesia.

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:11.320
Here the Ring of Fire has lifted thousands&nbsp;
of islands large and small from the sea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:19.520
And this archipelago has come to be known&nbsp;
by a name that in old Javanese meant simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.520 --> 00:19:24.800
the outer lands. This name is Nusan Tara.

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:40.680
The archipelago today called Nusantara lies&nbsp;
across an area of ocean wider than the continental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:49.760
United States and laid over a map of Europe would&nbsp;
stretch from France as far east as northern India.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.320 --> 00:20:00.640
The archipelago is today home to more than 700&nbsp;
languages, six major religions, and more than 300&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:08.800
ethnicities. And since modern humans first arrived&nbsp;
on these islands, they have given rise to a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:18.640
variety of different peoples. One Chinese Buddhist&nbsp;
monk named Faran who sailed to India in the 4th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:26.280
century in search of rare religious manuscripts&nbsp;
describes the perils and beauties of this ocean.

00:20:29.440 --> 00:20:34.480
In this sea there are many pirates.&nbsp;
The sea is boundless in extent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:42.840
It is impossible to know east or west and one can&nbsp;
only advance by observing the sun, moon or stars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:48.880
If it is dark rainy weather, you have to&nbsp;
follow the wind in perfect uncertainty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:55.640
During the darkness of night, one only sees&nbsp;
the great waves striking each other and shining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.640 --> 00:21:01.840
like fire, whilst shors of sea monsters&nbsp;
of every description surround the ship.

00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:12.640
Today, the complex geography of this region can&nbsp;
prove confusing to many. As you listen, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.640 --> 00:21:18.880
find yourself getting lost in the different names&nbsp;
of islands and seas referenced in this episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.880 --> 00:21:26.240
it's worth remembering that you're not alone.&nbsp;
This is by far the densest region for islands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:33.120
anywhere in the world. And in fact, the government&nbsp;
of Indonesia itself has struggled to come up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:38.640
a definitive answer to exactly how many&nbsp;
islands there are within its borders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:48.520
One 2002 study using the satellite images&nbsp;
Landsat identified more than 18,000 islands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:57.520
while another 2010 study gave a lower figure,&nbsp;
noting that only around 9,000 even have names.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:06.280
Nearly 90% of Indonesia's islands are tiny,&nbsp;
coming in at less than a kilometer squared.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.800 --> 00:22:15.640
Other islands are massive. The archipelago&nbsp;
contains the world's second largest island of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.640 --> 00:22:26.040
Papua or New Guinea, the third largest of Borneo,&nbsp;
and the sixth largest of Sumatra. In land area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.040 --> 00:22:35.400
each of these is larger than Japan or the size&nbsp;
of the United Kingdom and France combined. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:42.880
while this archipelago is home to some enormous&nbsp;
islands, these tend to be rocky and mountainous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:49.880
their interiors covered in impenetrable&nbsp;
rainforest, and generally sparssely populated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.920 --> 00:22:57.840
It is the fifth largest island in the archipelago&nbsp;
that has had perhaps the greatest impact of all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:05.920
And this is the place where our story&nbsp;
begins. the long narrow island of Java.

00:23:20.280 --> 00:23:29.120
Java is a volcanic island shaped like a cigar&nbsp;
running just south of and roughly parallel to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.120 --> 00:23:39.320
the equator. It's little over 100 km wide north to&nbsp;
south, but stretches for more than a thousand km&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:47.440
east to west, and the center of the island is&nbsp;
dominated by a rocky spine of conical volcanoes.

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:55.520
A medieval Javanese creation&nbsp;
myth called the Tanu Pangalaran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.520 --> 00:24:03.880
describes how the island was once floating free in&nbsp;
the ocean before the gods decided to nail it down.

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:14.680
The island of Java was unsteady,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:21.880
constantly moving up and down as it had no&nbsp;
great mountains, not to mention human beings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.920 --> 00:24:29.560
For this reason, Lord Jagat Prammana arose and&nbsp;
promptly performed yoga on the island of Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.760 --> 00:24:38.640
The deity stood facing east. He churned the water&nbsp;
and it turned into foam and turned into mountains.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.800 --> 00:24:44.240
For this reason, we have what is&nbsp;
called the land above the clouds. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:50.440
now the gods Brahma and Vishnu&nbsp;
agreed and made human beings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:56.960
They needed lumps of clay and formed&nbsp;
them into humans of perfect beauty.

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:11.640
Today, partly as a result of its rich volcanic&nbsp;
soil and its heavy rainfall and strong sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.640 --> 00:25:14.920
Java is the world's most populous island.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:23.440
It is home to more than 150 million people.&nbsp;
despite its land area being only about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.440 --> 00:25:30.040
size of England, making it one of the&nbsp;
most densely populated places on Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:39.440
More people live on Java than in the whole of&nbsp;
Russia, despite it being about 130 times smaller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:47.400
and its capital of Jakarta recently took the&nbsp;
top spot as the world's most populated city.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:54.640
This reputation for abundance&nbsp;
goes back to ancient times.

00:25:57.400 --> 00:26:03.280
The Hindu epic poem, the Ramayana,&nbsp;
written around 2,000 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:13.400
already mentions Java as a place of wealth. The&nbsp;
island of Yava is decorated with seven kingdoms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:22.200
gold and silver islands rich in gold mines and a&nbsp;
cold mountain that touches the sky with its peak.

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:31.080
Even in ancient times, the&nbsp;
island had a global reputation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:39.080
It was prominent enough in the 2n century that&nbsp;
the Greek writer and geographer Claudius Tommy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.080 --> 00:26:45.760
included it in his work geographia right&nbsp;
at the farthest edge of the known world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:54.680
He describes an island named Yabadu, a name&nbsp;
which he says means the island of Bali,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:55.240 --> 00:27:01.520
possibly referring to the hardy grain millet,&nbsp;
which has been an important food stuff there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.520 --> 00:27:10.720
for centuries before the introduction of rice.&nbsp;
As a result of its inclusion in Tomy's work, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:18.360
island would also appear in a number of medieval&nbsp;
and early modern mapundi or maps of the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:27.680
Although few Europeans could agree on where&nbsp;
exactly the island was. In these maps, it usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:35.960
appears somewhere wedged between Africa and Asia&nbsp;
and always as far away as it was possible to get.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:37.800 --> 00:27:43.880
But for the people who lived on the island&nbsp;
of Java, it was the very center of the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.480 --> 00:27:51.760
According to Javanese legend, it was&nbsp;
where the gods had first created humanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:51.760 --> 00:27:58.400
and taught them the secrets of civilization.&nbsp;
As the Tantu Pangaladan recounts,

00:28:01.040 --> 00:28:09.480
such were the instructions of Lord Guru to all&nbsp;
the gods. They descended to Java. The god Brahma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:17.240
became a blacksmith. The earth served as his&nbsp;
anvil, water as his tongs, light as his fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:17.240 --> 00:28:26.760
and wind as his bellows and the sky as his&nbsp;
hammer. Lord Wiswakarma descended as a carpenter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:26.760 --> 00:28:34.520
and made a house and people followed him and made&nbsp;
houses. The Lord Ishwara descended and taught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:34.520 --> 00:28:42.120
beneficial words in particular about the 10 kinds&nbsp;
of good conduct and the five fields of learning.

00:28:45.480 --> 00:28:52.920
Since it sits virtually on the equator, Java&nbsp;
experiences very little variation in sunlight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:52.920 --> 00:29:00.800
throughout the year. In fact, its shortest day&nbsp;
is only 48 minutes shorter than its longest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:09.000
The western side of the island also enjoys very&nbsp;
dependable monsoon rains from the Indian Ocean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:16.720
which combine with the extremely fertile volcanic&nbsp;
soil so that farmers could grow all year round&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:26.080
and could often harvest three whole crops of&nbsp;
rice each year. The 14th century Chinese source,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.080 --> 00:29:33.120
the Sunshi or History of the Sun Dynasty&nbsp;
describes the great abundance of this island.

00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:42.480
The country is flat and fit for agriculture.&nbsp;
Its products are rice, hemp, and peas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:48.800
there being no wheat. Salt is obtained by boiling&nbsp;
sea water and there is an abundance of fish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.800 --> 00:29:54.360
turtles, poultry, ducks, goats and cattle&nbsp;
which they kill for the purpose of eating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:55.120 --> 00:30:00.400
Their fruit are papaya, coconuts,&nbsp;
plantains, sugarcane and taro.

00:30:03.240 --> 00:30:09.920
From about the fifth century on, this vast&nbsp;
agricultural potential would give birth to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:09.920 --> 00:30:16.760
thriving societies and these would go on to&nbsp;
construct elaborate monumental architecture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:18.800 --> 00:30:25.440
For nearly the next thousand years, most of&nbsp;
these societies would arise in the west of Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:27.080 --> 00:30:35.600
One king of this time named Pur Navaman left one&nbsp;
of the earliest stone inscriptions carved into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:35.600 --> 00:30:44.960
large boulder in the Chiruten River. The powerful,&nbsp;
illustrious and brave king, the famous Por Navaran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.960 --> 00:30:51.520
of the Taruma Nagara Kingdom whose print of the&nbsp;
foot souls are the same as those of God Vishnu.

00:30:57.760 --> 00:31:04.160
The inscription is accompanied by a&nbsp;
carved imprint of both of the king's feet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:08.640
symbolically stamping his&nbsp;
ownership on the territory.

00:31:12.720 --> 00:31:20.200
By the sixth or seventh century, a power known&nbsp;
as Kling seems to have risen up in central Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:20.920 --> 00:31:27.200
and a description of it was included in the&nbsp;
Chinese source known as the Sin Tang Shu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:27.760 --> 00:31:30.560
or the new history of the Tong Dynasty.

00:31:33.720 --> 00:31:41.600
Carling is also called Java. It is situated in the&nbsp;
southern ocean. The people make fortifications of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:49.000
wood and even the largest houses are covered with&nbsp;
palm leaves. They have couches of ivory and mats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:56.400
of the outer skin of bamboo. The land produces&nbsp;
tortois shell, gold and silver, rhinoceros&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:56.400 --> 00:32:03.840
horns and ivory. The country is very rich. They&nbsp;
have letters and are acquainted with astronomy.

00:32:06.880 --> 00:32:12.560
These early kingdoms seem to have met&nbsp;
with great success on the island of Java,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:19.280
but little has survived of their records,&nbsp;
their literature, or their architecture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:20.960 --> 00:32:27.080
But all that would change with the&nbsp;
founding of a kingdom known as Mataram.

00:32:40.280 --> 00:32:46.000
The name Martam comes from the&nbsp;
Sanskrit word martr meaning mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:46.680 --> 00:32:54.560
and likely meant something like motherland.&nbsp;
This kingdom grew up not in the west&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:54.560 --> 00:33:02.960
but in the highlands of central Java right in the&nbsp;
shadow of the great volcanoes Merbu and Marappi.

00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:12.640
The people of Mataram were the first to truly&nbsp;
take advantage of this mountainous landscape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:19.400
and cultivated the steep slopes of this area&nbsp;
using concentric terraces of rice fields&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:19.920 --> 00:33:26.560
fed and drained by intricate systems&nbsp;
of canals and stonelined channels.

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:36.920
This kingdom of the mountains was originally&nbsp;
Hindu and worshiped primarily the god Shiva,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.920 --> 00:33:39.760
the god of creation and destruction.

00:33:42.720 --> 00:33:49.480
Living as they did on a land constantly torn&nbsp;
apart and remade by the destructive power of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.480 --> 00:33:56.680
the volcanoes, it's no mystery why this god&nbsp;
would have held sway in the kingdom of Martam.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:57.720 --> 00:34:03.200
But with the rise of a dynasty known&nbsp;
as the Shilendras in the 8th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:09.080
Martam began to drift further&nbsp;
towards a new religion, Buddhism.

00:34:16.920 --> 00:34:23.363
In this period, Mataram would become what's&nbsp;
known to historians as an Indianized Hindu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:23.363 --> 00:34:29.880
Buddhist kingdom with elements of both&nbsp;
religions combined into a syncratic whole.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:31.480 --> 00:34:37.600
These religions became so intertwined over the&nbsp;
centuries that followed that the epic Tantu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:37.600 --> 00:34:45.280
Pangaladan even recounts the story of one god who&nbsp;
came down to earth and divided himself into two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:45.280 --> 00:34:52.880
holy sages, one Buddhist and one Hindu so that&nbsp;
he could spread both religions at the same time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:55.200 --> 00:35:01.600
Let us tell about Maampu Paleat.&nbsp;
He returned to the island of Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:02.520 --> 00:35:08.160
He divided his body into two and&nbsp;
it became a shyite and a Buddhist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:14.920
Off they went and arrived in Java. They headed&nbsp;
for Gira and set up a monastery together.

00:35:17.680 --> 00:35:23.600
The kings of Java would even come to&nbsp;
be known by the title Bura Siwa Buddha&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:24.160 --> 00:35:32.360
meaning the divine Lord Shiva Buddha. a royal&nbsp;
figure in whom the two sacred beings were fused.

00:35:34.760 --> 00:35:39.280
This was in general a period&nbsp;
of coexistence and unity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:46.400
And it's in this era that some of Java's&nbsp;
most remarkable monuments would be built.

00:35:59.560 --> 00:36:05.120
The kings of the mountain kingdom&nbsp;
of Mataram were ambitious builders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:06.920 --> 00:36:15.080
Around the year 850, they completed construction&nbsp;
of a great temple to Shiva, the towering Pramanan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.080 --> 00:36:22.600
temple, which still sends its eight sturdy&nbsp;
spires up into the skies of southern Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:24.880 --> 00:36:32.960
It is the second largest Hindu structure in&nbsp;
Southeast Asia behind only anchor watt in Cambodia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.520 --> 00:36:37.240
and it left its mark not just on the landscape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:37.240 --> 00:36:42.280
but in the name of the year in which&nbsp;
its construction was completed.

00:36:45.160 --> 00:36:52.800
By this time, the Javanese had adopted a unique&nbsp;
system of naming each year, giving them what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.800 --> 00:37:03.800
are known as moon chronogs or in Javanese Chundra&nbsp;
sanka. This system used the Indian Shaka calendar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:03.800 --> 00:37:13.520
which assigned numbers to the years the&nbsp;
way we do today like 1066, 1939 or 2026.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:14.480 --> 00:37:20.280
But the Javanese developed this Indian&nbsp;
calendar with their own poetic flourish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:22.160 --> 00:37:31.360
They gave each number from 0 to 10 an array&nbsp;
of possible symbols like the sun, moon, fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:31.360 --> 00:37:37.920
and other elements which they could choose from to&nbsp;
give the year a name that seemed the most fitting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:39.200 --> 00:37:47.320
For example, the 18th century Balan chronicle&nbsp;
named the Babadgumi gives one year the name Gaja&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:47.320 --> 00:37:56.720
Aappi Anin Watu or elephant, fire, wind, stone due&nbsp;
to a particularly violent eruption of the volcano&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:56.720 --> 00:38:06.400
Mount Aong that took place in East Bali. Another&nbsp;
year in which a fierce battle took place was named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:06.400 --> 00:38:15.480
Paksha Shakara Babumi or the force of violence&nbsp;
moving upon the earth. Due to this system, we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:15.480 --> 00:38:21.760
sometimes get a sense from the names that years&nbsp;
were given of the overriding mood in the kingdom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:30.240
Sometimes a mood of joy and celebration,&nbsp;
at others of despair and sorrow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:32.400 --> 00:38:36.960
On the year that the kings of Mataram&nbsp;
completed the Prammanan temple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:37.480 --> 00:38:44.400
they were clearly proud of their achievement and&nbsp;
because of its eight towering main shrines, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:44.400 --> 00:38:53.760
named the year in honor of it. In our calendar it&nbsp;
was 856 but in the Shaka calendar it was called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:53.760 --> 00:39:01.360
eight mountains of the holy monks as recorded&nbsp;
in a text known as the shavra inscription.

00:39:04.040 --> 00:39:10.600
In the time of the Shaka year, eight mountains of&nbsp;
the holy monks, the statue of the god was finished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.600 --> 00:39:17.440
and inaugurated. After the shiva sanctuary&nbsp;
had been completed in its divine splendor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:17.440 --> 00:39:21.920
the course of the river was changed&nbsp;
so that it rippled along the grounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:22.920 --> 00:39:27.280
Then the grounds were inaugurated&nbsp;
as temple grounds with the gods.

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:37.560
Other temples built by the kings of Mataram,&nbsp;
such as the sprawling temple at Seu, are thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:37.560 --> 00:39:44.480
to have been Buddhist since they were built in&nbsp;
the form of a mandala, an important geometrical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:44.480 --> 00:39:53.400
concept in Buddhism that is used to conceptualize&nbsp;
the shape of the universe. But chief among their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:53.400 --> 00:40:00.600
works is the structure that would become what is&nbsp;
still the largest Buddhist temple in the world.

00:40:03.360 --> 00:40:09.160
It's thought that the Javanese of the&nbsp;
time called it Bumi Shambara Budara&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:09.160 --> 00:40:15.000
or the mountain of combined virtues&nbsp;
of the 10 stages of Buddhahood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:15.880 --> 00:40:21.560
But today it is known by a name meaning&nbsp;
only the great Buddha or Boro Buddhur.

00:40:21.560 --> 00:40:22.600
Boro Budur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.720 --> 00:40:35.640
rises like a mountain from the river plains right&nbsp;
in the shadow of the great volcano Mount Marappi.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:37.640 --> 00:40:42.400
This vast complex consists&nbsp;
of nine stacked platforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:42.920 --> 00:40:50.120
rising like a wedding cake up to a height&nbsp;
of 35 m and topped by a central dome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:51.240 --> 00:40:58.360
One heritage worker Gerard Bolla describes&nbsp;
the appearance of this incredible temple.

00:41:01.040 --> 00:41:08.440
Orodor the mountain of the virtues is first of all&nbsp;
a landscape. The landscape which over a thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:08.440 --> 00:41:14.280
years ago met the eyes of those who came on&nbsp;
pilgrimage here to seek inner peace. It has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:14.280 --> 00:41:21.440
hardly changed. In the distance, two volcanoes,&nbsp;
one ever menacing, glows red in the night.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:22.240 --> 00:41:29.120
All around in the middle distance, a ring of&nbsp;
mountains built on this same volcanic stone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:29.120 --> 00:41:34.440
Borobodor emerges from a delicate green&nbsp;
background of tropical vegetation.

00:41:37.920 --> 00:41:46.480
The design of Bora Budur like the temple at&nbsp;
Su is an enormous mandala visible from above.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:47.400 --> 00:41:53.920
But this mandala covers the area of two&nbsp;
football pitches and guides its pilgrims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:53.920 --> 00:42:01.320
up through a series of concentric walkways and&nbsp;
stairways carved out of black volcanic stone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:02.680 --> 00:42:11.360
These are all lined with nearly 3,000 carved&nbsp;
freezes and 500 Buddha statues created by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:11.360 --> 00:42:20.080
the finest craftsmen the kingdom could find. The&nbsp;
archaeologist, Professor Raden Sokmano, who was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:20.080 --> 00:42:27.680
charge of the project to restore the great temple&nbsp;
in modern times, describes its spiritual function.

00:42:30.960 --> 00:42:37.480
For the ardent Buddhist, it is the highest wisdom&nbsp;
that leads to the ultimate salvation. And for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:37.480 --> 00:42:44.560
technician, the highest degree of expertise that&nbsp;
leads to the appropriate fulfillment of his duty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:45.160 --> 00:42:54.360
In both cases, Boro Budor is the embodiment of&nbsp;
such a deeply felt wish. It is a prayer in stone.

00:43:03.200 --> 00:43:09.680
The symbolism of this structure shaped like&nbsp;
a mountain would likely have been obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:09.680 --> 00:43:18.440
to the early medieval Javanese. The gods had&nbsp;
once placed the mountains of Java to secure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.440 --> 00:43:27.480
the island in place and bring stability to the&nbsp;
land. Now their king did the same thing. placing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:27.480 --> 00:43:34.480
his own mountain on the landscape to act as a&nbsp;
testament to the lasting stability of his reign.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:35.960 --> 00:43:44.840
And Bora Budor was built to last. It was&nbsp;
constructed entirely without mortar or cement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:45.360 --> 00:43:52.040
carving its 2 million blocks of volcanic&nbsp;
stone into intricate interlocking shapes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:52.040 --> 00:43:59.600
to hold them together. This design has meant that&nbsp;
it has largely survived the countless earthquakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:59.600 --> 00:44:06.160
that have taken place over the last millennium in&nbsp;
one of the world's most seismically active zones,

00:44:08.560 --> 00:44:15.720
as well as the shifting ground beneath their feet.&nbsp;
The builders also had the heavy rainfall of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:15.720 --> 00:44:23.480
Javanese monsoon to contend with. For this&nbsp;
reason, they equipped the site of Bora Budur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:23.480 --> 00:44:31.560
with more than 100 spouts for drainage, each&nbsp;
carved in the shape of a giant or sea dragon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:31.560 --> 00:44:39.080
so that when it rained, water would have poured&nbsp;
from their mouths. But the most remarkable and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:39.080 --> 00:44:46.680
haunting feature of this monument is the thousands&nbsp;
of reliefs carved all around its galleries.

00:44:54.760 --> 00:45:00.720
Many of these carvings show stories from&nbsp;
the lives of the Buddha ascending through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:00.720 --> 00:45:08.200
kilometers of galleries. And in this way, the&nbsp;
monument is a story brought to life in stone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:10.120 --> 00:45:16.280
But alongside this religious narrative,&nbsp;
others show scenes of simple daily life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:16.800 --> 00:45:23.400
for all the different levels of&nbsp;
Javanese society. In these carvings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:23.400 --> 00:45:32.480
we see people carrying woven baskets for winnowing&nbsp;
and steaming rice and stirring milk rice in pots.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:33.360 --> 00:45:39.120
We see people working in the fields and&nbsp;
shopping in a bustling urban marketplace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:40.040 --> 00:45:47.040
People carrying baskets of tropical fruit&nbsp;
and nets full of fish. All of their faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:47.040 --> 00:45:54.440
looking out from the stone, frozen in a moment&nbsp;
that passed by more than a thousand years ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:56.760 --> 00:46:00.080
The Chinese history of Leyang describes the dress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:00.640 --> 00:46:07.440
and appearance of the people of Java which&nbsp;
can be seen in the reliefs of Boro Buddhur.

00:46:10.120 --> 00:46:12.880
Men and women have the upper&nbsp;
part of the body naked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:12.880 --> 00:46:17.560
Their hair hangs loosely down and around their&nbsp;
lower limbs they only use a sarong of cotton.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:18.480 --> 00:46:23.280
The king and the nobles have a thin flowered&nbsp;
cloth for covering the upper part of their body.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:24.040 --> 00:46:27.520
They wear a girdle of gold and&nbsp;
golden rings in their ears.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:28.400 --> 00:46:32.480
Young girls cover themselves with a cloth&nbsp;
of cotton and wear an embroidered girdle.

00:46:35.800 --> 00:46:40.000
In these carvings, we also&nbsp;
get a sense for courtly life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:41.120 --> 00:46:47.800
We see the king coming and going on his&nbsp;
elephant, his courtiers and the palace women.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:49.760 --> 00:46:57.400
In some carvings, we see the king and his nobles&nbsp;
enjoying a splendid feast known as a mahammansa.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:06.240
One 12th century Javanese epic&nbsp;
poem known as the bulman taka or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:12.040
death of Bulma describes the sumptuous&nbsp;
sight of one of these feasts.

00:47:20.480 --> 00:47:29.280
The dishes were salted eggs, the pork neck cuts,&nbsp;
ayong ayong and paban, vegetables in hot sauce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:29.280 --> 00:47:36.120
lickin and chzecha, red mints of tripe with its&nbsp;
various red salads and sizzling dripping of turtle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:37.320 --> 00:47:44.000
and the boiled vegetables. Languban with marrow&nbsp;
and tongue in rolls. Mushrooms steamed in leaves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:44.000 --> 00:47:49.480
Crisp flakes of jellyfish. Liver with&nbsp;
rump accompanied by roasted lungs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:49.480 --> 00:47:58.440
Breast bone. Intestines on skewers with fat and&nbsp;
hot kebabs. The banana heart was sweet. And there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:58.440 --> 00:48:05.520
was no lack of prawns, crabs, salted fish, and&nbsp;
muscles. Pickled meats, grilled meats, wuku,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:05.520 --> 00:48:13.360
and various skewered meats, doves, puk birds, and&nbsp;
wild pigeon made them clutch in all directions.

00:48:13.360 --> 00:48:22.280
ring aarab.

00:48:22.280 --> 00:48:30.280
Some of these 9th century carvings even depict&nbsp;
recipes that are still enjoyed in Indonesia today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:31.160 --> 00:48:38.440
Among them, a dish called tonang, a tall cone&nbsp;
of rice surrounded by side dishes which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:38.440 --> 00:48:47.560
still prepared for special occasions. But one&nbsp;
particular feature of these carvings has always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:47.560 --> 00:48:55.880
captured the imagination of those who visit the&nbsp;
ruins. These are the so-called Boro Budor ships.

00:49:06.480 --> 00:49:14.440
In among the scenes of daily life are carved&nbsp;
the shapes of five large seagoing vessels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:14.440 --> 00:49:21.000
graceful and high-sided, which show just&nbsp;
how accomplished the Javanese had become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:21.000 --> 00:49:29.480
as shipbuilders and sailors. And so it's&nbsp;
no surprise that it was around this time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:30.120 --> 00:49:37.480
that the people of Java began to gain a reputation&nbsp;
around the world as traders and pirates.

00:49:41.240 --> 00:49:48.960
The Javanese of the Mataram period sailed in&nbsp;
large ships that were often built from teik.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:50.240 --> 00:49:58.200
This kind of wood was prized for its strength&nbsp;
and its high oil content which gives it a natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:58.200 --> 00:50:06.120
water resistance even without varnishing and&nbsp;
gives it a distinct rich aroma like fine leather.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:07.360 --> 00:50:15.040
These ships were built without nails using&nbsp;
only interlocking joints and wooden dowels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:15.040 --> 00:50:25.600
and with ingenious arrangements of sails. The 3rd&nbsp;
century Chinese writer Wan Chen in his work Nanju&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:25.600 --> 00:50:33.840
Yiu J or strange things of the southern lands&nbsp;
describes the sight of these majestic vessels.

00:50:36.880 --> 00:50:45.360
The people sometimes rig as many as four sails&nbsp;
which they carry in a row from bow to stern. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:45.360 --> 00:50:53.320
four sails do not face directly forward, but are&nbsp;
set obliquely and so arranged that they can all be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:53.320 --> 00:51:00.640
fixed in the same direction to receive the wind&nbsp;
and to spill it. If it is violent, the sailors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:00.640 --> 00:51:08.480
diminish or augment the surface of the sails&nbsp;
according to the conditions. Thus these ships sail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:08.480 --> 00:51:15.360
without avoiding strong winds and dashing waves&nbsp;
by the aid of which they can make great speed.

00:51:18.080 --> 00:51:26.440
To Wan Chen and other Chinese writers, these&nbsp;
Javanese ships were so remarkable because China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:26.440 --> 00:51:35.480
itself would not develop large oceangoing vessels&nbsp;
until around the 9th century or 600 years later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:36.640 --> 00:51:42.520
In fact, the Chinese held the ships of&nbsp;
these islanders in such reverence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:42.520 --> 00:51:50.760
no foreign craft were permitted to sail within&nbsp;
range of the main port of Guangong or Canton.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:50.760 --> 00:51:56.200
As the 16th century Portuguese&nbsp;
diplomat Tom Perez recounts,

00:51:59.960 --> 00:52:04.640
"They say that the Chinese made this law&nbsp;
about not being able to go to Canton for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:04.640 --> 00:52:10.080
fear of the Japanese and Malays, for it&nbsp;
is certain that one of these people ships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:10.080 --> 00:52:16.120
would route 20 Chinese ships. Such is&nbsp;
their fear of Malays and Japanese."

00:52:20.080 --> 00:52:28.520
This fear appears to have been warranted. The&nbsp;
inscriptions of the Cham people of Vietnam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:28.520 --> 00:52:36.520
and the Camair in Cambodia frequently reference&nbsp;
the arrival of fearsome seagoing raiders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:36.520 --> 00:52:41.360
who are thought to have sailed&nbsp;
from islands like Sumatra and Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:43.160 --> 00:52:52.240
One inscription in the Po Nagar temple in&nbsp;
Vietnam describes one such raid in the year 774.

00:52:55.480 --> 00:53:00.440
Ferocious, pitiles, dark-skinned&nbsp;
men born in other countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:00.440 --> 00:53:07.160
whose food was more horrible than corpses and&nbsp;
who were vicious and furious came in ships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:07.160 --> 00:53:11.360
took away the temple statue&nbsp;
and set fire to the temple.

00:53:14.680 --> 00:53:19.920
The Javanese were in part able&nbsp;
to become such successful sailors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:20.480 --> 00:53:26.480
due to the extremely predictable wind patterns&nbsp;
that take place in this part of the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:28.280 --> 00:53:35.080
During the rainy season from December to March,&nbsp;
the winds blow in over Java from the Asian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:35.080 --> 00:53:41.880
mainland in the northwest, picking up moisture&nbsp;
over the South China Sea and Pacific Ocean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:41.880 --> 00:53:47.960
and bringing the monsoon. But during&nbsp;
the dry season from April to September,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:48.480 --> 00:53:54.840
the winds reverse direction and blow from&nbsp;
the southeast and the deserts of Australia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:55.480 --> 00:54:02.960
bringing dry air and a lower probability of&nbsp;
rain. This meant you could travel whichever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:02.960 --> 00:54:08.160
direction you wanted so long as you&nbsp;
wisely chose your season to sail.

00:54:12.120 --> 00:54:15.080
As their confidence grew on the waves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:15.080 --> 00:54:21.640
we have evidence that the Javanese also&nbsp;
undertook some even more ambitious voyages.

00:54:30.200 --> 00:54:39.400
One Italian explorer who arrived in Java&nbsp;
around 1505 named Ludovvic Deva of Bologna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:39.400 --> 00:54:47.120
sailed to the island of Java and was amazed to&nbsp;
see the captain of the ship navigate even when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:47.120 --> 00:54:53.920
the North Star was no longer visible. When the&nbsp;
chartered vessel was supplied with provisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:53.920 --> 00:54:59.720
we took our way towards the beautiful island&nbsp;
called Java at which we arrived in 5 days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:59.720 --> 00:55:05.160
sailing towards the south. My companion asked the&nbsp;
Christians, "Now that we have lost the North Star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:05.160 --> 00:55:09.840
how does he steer us? Is there any other&nbsp;
North Star than this by which we steer?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:10.360 --> 00:55:15.400
The Christians asked the captain of the ship this&nbsp;
same thing, and he showed us four or five stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:15.400 --> 00:55:19.640
among which there was one which he said&nbsp;
was the opposite to our north star.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:22.000 --> 00:55:28.360
This was the socalled southern cross, the&nbsp;
distinctive pattern of stars that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:28.360 --> 00:55:32.960
be seen in the southern hemisphere&nbsp;
in the direction of the South Pole.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:34.200 --> 00:55:41.520
The Europeans were amazed at this discovery.&nbsp;
But the captain then told him that the Javanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:41.520 --> 00:55:49.920
had sailed into the south following these stars&nbsp;
and had there come upon an icy and frozen land.

00:55:53.160 --> 00:55:57.960
He also told us that on the other side of the&nbsp;
said island towards the south, there are some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:57.960 --> 00:56:04.240
other races who navigate by the said four or&nbsp;
five stars opposite to ours. And moreover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:04.240 --> 00:56:09.720
they gave us to understand that beyond the said&nbsp;
island, the day does not last more than 4 hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:09.720 --> 00:56:13.520
and that there it was colder than&nbsp;
in any other part of the world.

00:56:16.640 --> 00:56:23.840
If this is true, it means that the Javanese&nbsp;
had already sailed to and around Australia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:23.840 --> 00:56:30.440
and set out into the Antarctic&nbsp;
Ocean. But tantalizingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:30.440 --> 00:56:38.240
we have only this secondhand story to go by, and&nbsp;
the rest will have to be left to our imaginations.

00:56:40.440 --> 00:56:46.960
But as the centuries of the first millennium&nbsp;
went on, there is a great deal more evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:46.960 --> 00:56:52.120
that the people of Java were making&nbsp;
their mark in even further lands.

00:56:58.760 --> 00:57:08.760
7,000 km away on the shores of East Africa, the&nbsp;
10th century Persian writer Bazour Iben Shari&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:08.760 --> 00:57:17.240
gives an account of a series of raids undertaken&nbsp;
by a people from a faraway land that he calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:17.240 --> 00:57:25.720
the Wacwack. The Wacwack set out with a&nbsp;
thousand ships to launch a determined attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:25.720 --> 00:57:34.120
upon the town of Kbala. But they were unable to&nbsp;
capture it for the town was strongly fortified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:34.120 --> 00:57:41.480
and was surrounded by an arm of the sea in the&nbsp;
midst of which Kbarlo rose like a fortress.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:43.120 --> 00:57:48.640
When the people of the African coast&nbsp;
eventually came to terms with the attackers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:49.280 --> 00:57:54.960
they learned more about why they had&nbsp;
come and how far they had traveled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:56.960 --> 00:58:03.560
The people of that country demanded why they had&nbsp;
come to Gmbalo rather than to any other place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:03.560 --> 00:58:08.520
They replied that it was because the country&nbsp;
possessed merchandise of value in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:08.520 --> 00:58:16.000
Wacwack country and in China such as ivory,&nbsp;
tortoise shell, panther skins, and amberes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:16.000 --> 00:58:22.200
And also because they wanted to obtain some&nbsp;
of the Zenge people who being strong men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:22.200 --> 00:58:29.400
are able to stand heavy labor. Their voyage,&nbsp;
they said, had lasted a year. If the story told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:29.400 --> 00:58:36.640
by these people be true, this proves that the&nbsp;
Wacwack Islands are situated opposite to China.

00:58:39.920 --> 00:58:44.880
The location of these Wacwack&nbsp;
Islands has long been debated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:45.560 --> 00:58:51.200
One clue can be found in the&nbsp;
Persian chronicler Iben Kodadbe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:51.200 --> 00:58:58.720
whose description has made many historians believe&nbsp;
that they may have been the islands of Indonesia.

00:59:01.040 --> 00:59:06.120
East of China are the lands of Wacwack, which&nbsp;
are so rich in gold that the inhabitants make the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:06.120 --> 00:59:12.000
chains for their dogs and the collars for their&nbsp;
monkeys of this metal. They manufacture tunics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:12.000 --> 00:59:19.240
woven with gold. Excellent. Ebony wood is found&nbsp;
there. Gold and ebony are exported from Wacwack.

00:59:23.040 --> 00:59:30.320
This detail of dogs wearing collars made of&nbsp;
gold was also mentioned by early European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:30.320 --> 00:59:37.720
explorers who visited Java, leading many to&nbsp;
conclude that the Wacwack really were Javanese.

00:59:40.360 --> 00:59:46.200
If this story does relate an attack on&nbsp;
eastern Africa by the people of Java,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:46.200 --> 00:59:51.520
then it shows the extent of their&nbsp;
ship building and seafaring prowess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:51.520 --> 00:59:58.040
and that they have become a formidable threat for&nbsp;
all the peoples who lived around the Indian Ocean.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:00.160 --> 01:00:06.000
From these raids, they would have brought&nbsp;
back gold, ivory, and slaves to Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:06.920 --> 01:00:11.160
But in other places, they didn't&nbsp;
simply raid and sail away.

01:00:19.080 --> 01:00:23.960
In the large island of Madagascar,&nbsp;
just off the coast of Eastern Africa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:23.960 --> 01:00:31.840
the people's language has always puzzled&nbsp;
linguists. The Madagascan language of Malagasi&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:31.840 --> 01:00:37.640
shares little in common with those of the African&nbsp;
mainland. And it was only when the grammar and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:37.640 --> 01:00:44.240
syntax were compared with Indonesian languages&nbsp;
that a remarkable similarity was noticed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:45.240 --> 01:00:51.840
Although separated by the entire Indian Ocean,&nbsp;
the language of Malagasi shares a grammatical&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:51.840 --> 01:00:58.560
structure with several Indonesian languages like&nbsp;
Javanese. And they even have a number of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:58.560 --> 01:01:08.400
words in common. Just one example is the word for&nbsp;
island, nosi in Malagasi and Nusa in Japanese.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:10.160 --> 01:01:15.823
In fact, the closest living relative of Malagasi&nbsp;
is thought to be a language called Manan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:16.800 --> 01:01:24.560
which originated on the south coast of Borneo.&nbsp;
The most modern analysis of mitochondrial DNA in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:24.560 --> 01:01:30.640
Madagascar has confirmed this connection and&nbsp;
shows that the people there can trace their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:30.640 --> 01:01:36.880
lineage back to about 30 mothers who arrived&nbsp;
on the island from what is now Indonesia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:38.840 --> 01:01:44.800
This suggests a single boatload of settlers&nbsp;
who had traveled across one of the world's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:44.800 --> 01:01:51.960
widest oceans to find a new home. They&nbsp;
also brought plants and crops with them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:53.360 --> 01:02:01.640
Botists estimate that a full 7% of the plant&nbsp;
life on Madagascar today originated in Indonesia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:02.960 --> 01:02:07.160
These include bananas, plantains, taro, and yams.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:07.680 --> 01:02:15.480
And all of these were already well established&nbsp;
there by the medieval period. In602,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:15.480 --> 01:02:23.880
another Portuguese explorer, Dio Duto, described&nbsp;
these far-reaching journeys of the Javanese.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:25.880 --> 01:02:31.720
The Javanese are all men very experienced in the&nbsp;
art of navigation to the point that they claim&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:31.720 --> 01:02:37.320
to be the most ancient of all. It is certain&nbsp;
that they formally navigated to Africa's Cape&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:37.320 --> 01:02:42.880
of Good Hope and were in communication with&nbsp;
the east coast of the island of Madagascar&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:42.880 --> 01:02:48.520
where there are many brown and Javaneseike&nbsp;
natives who say they are descended from them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:50.480 --> 01:02:56.840
The Javanese of this period navigated&nbsp;
using the stars but also magnetic compasses&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:56.840 --> 01:03:00.760
and maps written on the bark&nbsp;
of the paper Malbury tree.

01:03:04.400 --> 01:03:12.480
In 1512, only 20 years after Columbus had first&nbsp;
reached the Americas and 12 years after the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:12.480 --> 01:03:19.920
Europeans set foot in Brazil, a Portuguese&nbsp;
concistador fighting on the Malay Peninsula&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:19.920 --> 01:03:23.480
captured a sea chart from a Javanese sailor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:24.280 --> 01:03:30.680
He was amazed to find that it had already been&nbsp;
updated with all the new discoveries that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:30.680 --> 01:03:38.640
been made on the other side of the world. as he&nbsp;
wrote in one letter to King Manuel I of Portugal.

01:03:41.640 --> 01:03:47.640
We found a large map of a Javanese pilot&nbsp;
containing the Cape of Good Hope, Portugal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:47.640 --> 01:03:54.240
and the land of Brazil, the Red Sea and the Sea&nbsp;
of Persia, the Clove Islands, the navigation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:54.240 --> 01:04:00.080
the Chinese and Japanese, and how the kingdoms&nbsp;
border on each other. It seems to me, sir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:00.080 --> 01:04:05.480
that this was the best thing I have ever seen.&nbsp;
and your highness will be very pleased to see it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:07.000 --> 01:04:15.080
In 2003, a modern team was able to pilot&nbsp;
a replica of an old Japanese sailing ship&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:15.080 --> 01:04:22.080
from Indonesia to Madagascar and around&nbsp;
the Cape of Good Hope in South Africa.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:22.080 --> 01:04:28.240
Thus proving that this journey was possible&nbsp;
with the technology available at the time.

01:04:30.920 --> 01:04:38.000
But despite its remarkable achievements in&nbsp;
architecture and its voyages of discovery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:38.000 --> 01:04:42.800
the Chileendra dynasty of&nbsp;
Mataram would eventually decline&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:42.800 --> 01:04:46.680
and all of its grand monuments&nbsp;
would decline with it.

01:04:56.520 --> 01:05:04.560
The reasons for the fall of Mataram are unclear&nbsp;
since no true chronicles have survived from this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:04.560 --> 01:05:12.640
time. But the kingdom's decline may have been&nbsp;
related to a series of dramatic volcanic eruptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:12.640 --> 01:05:17.480
that occurred sometime in the&nbsp;
10th and early 11th centuries.

01:05:20.960 --> 01:05:26.160
The people of Java have always&nbsp;
lived with a particular paradox&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:27.080 --> 01:05:33.840
that the immense productivity of their land&nbsp;
also comes from its most destructive force.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:35.320 --> 01:05:43.320
Many landscapes that experience a monsoon season&nbsp;
as heavy as Java can suffer from poor soil quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:43.320 --> 01:05:52.680
as the seasonal rains wash away top soil and&nbsp;
leech nutrients away. But in much of Java,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:52.680 --> 01:06:00.960
the hundreds of active volcanoes counteract this&nbsp;
effect. Due to the island's particular geology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:00.960 --> 01:06:07.640
its volcanoes are especially beneficial for the&nbsp;
soil, filling it with minerals like magnesium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:07.640 --> 01:06:15.640
potassium, and phosphorus that plants thrive on,&nbsp;
all blasted out of the depths of the earth itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:17.400 --> 01:06:23.640
Volcanic soil is also uniquely porous&nbsp;
and retains moisture exceptionally well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:23.640 --> 01:06:33.040
while allowing air circulation for plant roots.&nbsp;
But of course, a volcano is a violent phenomenon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:34.520 --> 01:06:41.000
When they erupt, they scorch the earth&nbsp;
with pyrolastic flows, spew lava,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:41.000 --> 01:06:51.040
and rain huge boulders down from the sky across a&nbsp;
large area. This poses an obvious problem that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:51.040 --> 01:06:57.560
areas of the greatest agricultural productivity&nbsp;
on the island were also the most dangerous.

01:07:01.720 --> 01:07:07.560
This meant that while the volcano's overall&nbsp;
effect on the island was positive, a year in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:07.560 --> 01:07:14.720
which a great eruption took place could wipe out&nbsp;
a good proportion of any kingdom's food supplies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:16.000 --> 01:07:20.120
And of all these volcanoes, by far the most active&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:20.120 --> 01:07:28.120
was the one that the Javanese call by a name&nbsp;
which means the mountain of fire or merap.

01:07:38.960 --> 01:07:46.600
Rising to nearly 3,000 m or 10,000 ft. Mount&nbsp;
Marappi is the most active volcano in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:46.600 --> 01:07:52.400
whole of Indonesia and is considered one of&nbsp;
the most active and dangerous in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:53.600 --> 01:07:57.240
It has erupted regularly for&nbsp;
at least the last 500 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:57.800 --> 01:08:04.600
with small eruptions occurring every 2 to&nbsp;
3 years and larger ones every 10 to 15.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:05.640 --> 01:08:12.480
Then every few hundred years the mountain&nbsp;
explodes in a truly catastrophic fashion.

01:08:16.040 --> 01:08:23.920
The Javanese Chronicle, the Babad Tan Jawi,&nbsp;
records the devastation caused by one eruption&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:23.920 --> 01:08:28.600
of the great Mount Marappi that&nbsp;
took place in the 16th century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:30.800 --> 01:08:39.480
Then Mount Marappi erupted with fire. It made a&nbsp;
terrifying thundering noise. A rain of ash fell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:39.480 --> 01:08:48.280
A volcanic mud flow ran down the OAC river. Big&nbsp;
rocks rose from the flow and landed on the land.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:48.880 --> 01:08:53.880
When the piles of wood on the Ganang&nbsp;
GD mountains were set on fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:54.480 --> 01:09:01.680
the mountains became a blaze of fire. The&nbsp;
Gong Ki Bichchuk was beaten ceaselessly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:02.520 --> 01:09:06.440
All tried to save their lives&nbsp;
and ran in all directions.

01:09:09.280 --> 01:09:15.960
Added to this obvious physical destruction,&nbsp;
the Javanese also held the volcanoes in great&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:15.960 --> 01:09:24.720
spiritual reverence and viewed eruptions as&nbsp;
signs and portants from the gods. After the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:24.720 --> 01:09:31.920
deaths of great kings, Javanese chronicles often&nbsp;
describe the mountains rumbling in mourning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:33.760 --> 01:09:39.000
At the death of his majesty, the wailing&nbsp;
in the palace was as loud as thunder.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:39.920 --> 01:09:44.960
Mount Marappi rumbled, joining&nbsp;
in with the sound of a rainstorm.

01:09:48.040 --> 01:09:54.000
It was believed that the eruptions,&nbsp;
earthquakes, and various rumblings of the land&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:54.000 --> 01:10:01.200
were not just seismic events, but actually one&nbsp;
of the primary ways that the gods weighed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:01.200 --> 01:10:09.000
on the affairs of humanity. For this reason, their&nbsp;
timing could have real life or death consequences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:10.560 --> 01:10:18.920
One chronicle recounts the story of a lucky&nbsp;
prisoner held by a 17th century Javanese king.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:21.040 --> 01:10:27.160
The king wanted to kill the prisoner Raden&nbsp;
Masuya Kasuma. But this did not happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:27.160 --> 01:10:32.680
because every time they wanted to kill him,&nbsp;
Mount Marrapy flared up with a terrifying noise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:33.480 --> 01:10:40.480
In the end, the man was set free and promoted&nbsp;
and given the lordship of 1,000 rice fields.

01:10:47.680 --> 01:10:54.440
A particularly devastating eruption would not&nbsp;
only cause immense economic damage to the kingdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:54.440 --> 01:11:00.280
it could also be considered a clear sign that&nbsp;
the gods were angry with the current ruler.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:01.240 --> 01:11:07.480
For any king who didn't have a firm grasp&nbsp;
on power, this could be a devastating blow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:08.760 --> 01:11:15.920
From some inscriptions left in Java, we&nbsp;
know that as the 9th century came to an end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:15.920 --> 01:11:23.680
the kingdom of Mataram descended into civil&nbsp;
war with three kings ruling in only two years.

01:11:26.200 --> 01:11:33.920
When the dust of this civil war settled in&nbsp;
the year 929, a king named Rakhino Pusindok&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:33.920 --> 01:11:42.200
took to the throne. But curiously, it seems&nbsp;
the kingdom of Mataram had by this point moved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:43.800 --> 01:11:49.080
All of the inscriptions issued by this&nbsp;
king would be written in East Java,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:49.080 --> 01:11:54.480
far away from its original heartlands&nbsp;
around the volcano Mount Marappi.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:55.920 --> 01:12:02.360
It seems that around the beginning of the 10th&nbsp;
century, the entire Mataram Kingdom simply upped&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:02.360 --> 01:12:10.960
sticks and fled its original wealthy province,&nbsp;
leaving its towering temples and cities abandoned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:12.080 --> 01:12:17.600
And what's perhaps most significant is&nbsp;
that no other power seems to have swept&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:17.600 --> 01:12:24.080
in to the empty lands. This suggests that&nbsp;
some kind of cataclysm took place there.

01:12:32.320 --> 01:12:38.440
Archaeological evidence shows that the early&nbsp;
10th century layer of human occupation at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:38.440 --> 01:12:46.000
great temple of Boro Budor, for instance, is&nbsp;
sealed by up to half a meter of volcanic ash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:46.000 --> 01:12:54.800
suggesting a major eruption. The peoples of this&nbsp;
land were no strangers to erupting volcanoes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:55.440 --> 01:13:02.440
So we can imagine that it must have taken one&nbsp;
of particular violence or a series of eruptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:02.440 --> 01:13:09.720
taking place in frighteningly quick succession to&nbsp;
cause the wholesale abandonment of this kingdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:10.240 --> 01:13:14.680
and its reestablishment hundreds&nbsp;
of kilometers to the east.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:16.880 --> 01:13:25.080
One inscription known as the Puchangan stone&nbsp;
describes a kind of disaster sweeping the land.

01:13:28.240 --> 01:13:32.320
In the time of the great catastrophe&nbsp;
that overwhelmed the island of Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:32.320 --> 01:13:37.960
in the Saka year 928 when King&nbsp;
Warari came forth from Waram,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:38.480 --> 01:13:42.320
the whole of Java seemed transformed&nbsp;
into a single raging sea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:43.240 --> 01:13:49.200
Many of the foremost men of the realm perished.&nbsp;
Chief among the dead was the divine king.

01:13:52.280 --> 01:13:57.800
Unfortunately, the exact dating of&nbsp;
volcanic eruptions is difficult,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:57.800 --> 01:14:02.720
and it's hard to say whether this was&nbsp;
truly the cause of Mataram's downfall.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:03.760 --> 01:14:09.760
But whether from volcanic causes or not,&nbsp;
it's clear that for one reason or another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:10.280 --> 01:14:15.600
life had become unlivable for the&nbsp;
people of Matarang in central Java.

01:14:18.280 --> 01:14:24.160
The kingdom eventually reached a level of&nbsp;
stability in its new home in East Java,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:24.720 --> 01:14:27.360
away from the destruction it had faced.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:28.200 --> 01:14:35.640
But its confidence in itself and in the god's&nbsp;
approval of its rulers had been fatally reduced.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:36.840 --> 01:14:45.080
Within a century, the remains of Martam was facing&nbsp;
destruction, now at the hands of human enemies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:46.200 --> 01:14:53.080
An invasion by a neighboring king wiped out&nbsp;
what was left of it, and the kingdom of Mataram&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:53.080 --> 01:15:00.920
disappeared, living on only in the stone carvings&nbsp;
left lining the walls of its great temples.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:01.520 --> 01:15:09.680
Thousands of moments of everyday life now&nbsp;
sealed beneath a thick layer of volcanic ash.

01:15:18.680 --> 01:15:24.760
After the fall of Mataram, a number&nbsp;
of successor kingdoms would rise up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:26.520 --> 01:15:34.040
In the 11th century, much of Java was reunited&nbsp;
again under the rule of a king named Ayalanga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:34.680 --> 01:15:42.520
whose name meant he who leapt over the water.&nbsp;
He was born on the small island of Bali&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:43.040 --> 01:15:50.760
and crossed the sea to Java as a young&nbsp;
man. The Puchangan stone describes how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:50.760 --> 01:15:57.240
this king was spared from the destruction that&nbsp;
swept the land during the fall of Mataram.

01:15:59.960 --> 01:16:05.800
At that time, his majesty was only 16 years old,&nbsp;
still too young to have mastered the art of war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:06.480 --> 01:16:09.280
Yet because he was the very&nbsp;
manifestation of Vishnu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:09.800 --> 01:16:13.640
all the gods protected him from being&nbsp;
swept away by the great destruction.

01:16:16.600 --> 01:16:24.000
Arlanga would rule successfully for several&nbsp;
decades. But when he died, the land divided again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:25.480 --> 01:16:31.920
The kingdom that would truly give birth&nbsp;
to Java's golden era was named Singasari.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:33.800 --> 01:16:39.520
Singari derived its name from the&nbsp;
Sanskrit word simha meaning lion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:40.480 --> 01:16:46.320
And this lion kingdom was founded&nbsp;
by a king named Ken Angro.

01:16:53.280 --> 01:16:58.400
Ken Angro's name means the&nbsp;
man who overturned everything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:59.280 --> 01:17:09.640
and he was a revolutionary force on Java. In the&nbsp;
centuries since he lived, the legend of Ken Angro&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:09.640 --> 01:17:17.680
has grown to mythic proportions and he has&nbsp;
inspired theater, song and poetry in Indonesia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:19.720 --> 01:17:27.920
The story of his life is largely told in a&nbsp;
fragmentaryary chronicle known as the Paraton&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:28.440 --> 01:17:38.360
or the book of kings written sometime later in&nbsp;
the 16th century. According to this chronicle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:38.360 --> 01:17:45.400
Ken Angraok was actually the son of the&nbsp;
Hindu god Brahma who appeared to his mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:45.920 --> 01:17:52.560
a poor woman named Ken Endok, and&nbsp;
informed her of her immaculate conception.

01:18:00.240 --> 01:18:07.520
The god Brahma descended there and lay with&nbsp;
Ken Endoc. This meeting took place in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:07.520 --> 01:18:15.440
fields of Lalaten. The god made a vow with the&nbsp;
woman. You must not go to your husband again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:16.040 --> 01:18:24.960
If you lie with him, he will die and my child&nbsp;
will be corrupted. That child is named Ken Anbrook&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:25.600 --> 01:18:31.640
and he will one day rule the land&nbsp;
of Java. With that, Brahma vanished.

01:18:39.080 --> 01:18:49.960
The woman held true to her word and soon Ken&nbsp;
Angro was born. But the family was extremely poor&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:49.960 --> 01:18:57.520
and couldn't provide for the little boy. His&nbsp;
parents abandoned him in a grave. But he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:57.520 --> 01:19:05.680
found by a common thief named Lemong who was&nbsp;
perhaps plundering the graveyard for valuables.

01:19:12.040 --> 01:19:20.320
When her time was full, she gave birth to&nbsp;
a boy. Ken Endok abandoned him in a burial&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:20.320 --> 01:19:27.280
ground for children. Not long after, a thief&nbsp;
named Len Bong wandered into the same place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:28.040 --> 01:19:34.000
There he saw something glowing. Drawn to&nbsp;
it, he heard the sound of a child crying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:34.840 --> 01:19:40.160
When he came closer, he saw that the&nbsp;
light was coming from the child himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:40.840 --> 01:19:46.480
Lemong picked him up, carried him&nbsp;
home, and raised him as his own son.

01:19:55.360 --> 01:20:01.640
Brought up by this thief, Ken Angro&nbsp;
would learn all the tricks of his trade&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:02.160 --> 01:20:05.720
until he was the most accomplished thief in Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:08.000 --> 01:20:14.720
But one day he met an Indian holy man&nbsp;
who identified him as the son of Brahma&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:15.360 --> 01:20:25.920
and an embodiment of the god Vishnu. This holy man&nbsp;
convinced Ken Angro to give up his sinful life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:27.320 --> 01:20:36.120
As a reward, Ken Angro gained a magical&nbsp;
weapon, a kind of Javanese knife known as ais.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:38.160 --> 01:20:44.000
These are long daggers often created&nbsp;
with a distinctive rippled blade&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:44.560 --> 01:20:52.480
and they were worn by Javanese men as a sign&nbsp;
of status as a talisman against various evils&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:52.480 --> 01:21:00.000
and of course as a practical sidearm for&nbsp;
self-defense. One Chinese visitor to Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:00.000 --> 01:21:07.200
at the start of the 15th century named Mahuan&nbsp;
writes about the importance of these blades.

01:21:09.880 --> 01:21:16.800
From little boys of 3 years to old men of&nbsp;
a hundred years, they all have these knives&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:16.800 --> 01:21:22.280
which are all made of steel with most intricate&nbsp;
patterns drawn in very delicate lines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:23.200 --> 01:21:28.800
For the handles, they use gold or rhinoceros&nbsp;
horn or elephant's teeth engraved with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:28.800 --> 01:21:35.880
representations of human forms or devil's faces.&nbsp;
The craftsmanship being very fine and skillful.

01:21:40.320 --> 01:21:48.240
With this magical sword by his side, Ken Angro&nbsp;
rose to prominence in Java and eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:48.240 --> 01:21:56.680
through the overwhelming force of his various&nbsp;
virtues became its king as the paraton describes.

01:22:03.280 --> 01:22:12.960
Not long afterwards, Ken Anro was acclaimed as&nbsp;
king of Tamapel. His realm was called Singasari.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:13.760 --> 01:22:20.920
His enthronement was witnessed by learned men of&nbsp;
both the Shyite and Buddhist traditions of Keri.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:21.840 --> 01:22:28.200
Those who had once shown Ken Angarok kindness&nbsp;
in his days of hardship were summoned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:28.200 --> 01:22:32.200
granted protection, and generously&nbsp;
rewarded for their loyalty.

01:22:37.400 --> 01:22:38.400
Bilazani.

01:22:38.400 --> 01:22:47.800
Not everyone easily accepted the rule of this&nbsp;
commoner. In the nearby rival kingdom of Kediri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:47.800 --> 01:22:55.400
the king was named Dandang Gendis and he&nbsp;
refused to accept Ken Angro as his king.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:57.120 --> 01:23:02.480
Dandang Gendis gathered his armies&nbsp;
and marched to fight Ken Angro.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:03.360 --> 01:23:08.720
But he was no match for the&nbsp;
divine power of the God made man.

01:23:15.080 --> 01:23:22.600
He fell because he was attacked on all sides by&nbsp;
the forces of Singasari who came on like a flood&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:22.600 --> 01:23:30.600
pouring down from the mountains. The army of Keri&nbsp;
scattered like a swarm of bees, fleeing in panic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:30.600 --> 01:23:39.800
abandoning the field like startled goats, casting&nbsp;
aside their parasolles. King Dandang Dendis&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:39.800 --> 01:23:45.400
withdrew from the battle and fled into the realm&nbsp;
of the gods, rising up into the air with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:45.400 --> 01:23:52.760
horse, his grooms, his parasolbearers, and those&nbsp;
who carried his drinking vessels and his mats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:52.760 --> 01:24:00.800
All of them ascending into the sky. Thus,&nbsp;
Kendiri was utterly defeated by Ken Angbrook.

01:24:14.240 --> 01:24:21.720
Needless to say, this section of the Paraton&nbsp;
Chronicle is considered to be largely mythical&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:21.720 --> 01:24:26.400
and reflects the propaganda&nbsp;
of this king's new regime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:28.280 --> 01:24:35.960
But this story would be repeated across the towns&nbsp;
and villages of Java by trained storytellers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:36.520 --> 01:24:45.520
who together wo a new national myth around&nbsp;
the figure of Ken Angro. One of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:45.520 --> 01:24:53.200
important mediums in which this story was&nbsp;
told was that of the Javanese shadow puppets.

01:24:59.320 --> 01:25:07.160
The exact origin of Javanese shadow puppetry&nbsp;
is a mystery, but it's clear from a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:07.160 --> 01:25:13.880
oblique references in Javanese literature that&nbsp;
it was already a wellestablished practice by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:13.880 --> 01:25:20.800
at least the 10th century. This form&nbsp;
of shadow theater is known as Wyang.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:22.480 --> 01:25:29.000
Wang in Javanese means shadow or ghost,&nbsp;
but it can also mean imagination.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:29.880 --> 01:25:37.560
And these plays would retell stories from the&nbsp;
Hindu epics like the Mahabarat, local Javanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:37.560 --> 01:25:45.240
stories such as how rice came to the earth&nbsp;
and the stories of their kings and ancestors.

01:25:47.840 --> 01:25:52.560
The Chinese visitor to Java at the&nbsp;
beginning of the 15th century, Marwan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:53.600 --> 01:25:57.480
describes witnessing these&nbsp;
performances. [screaming]&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:59.320 --> 01:26:04.800
Each time the performer unrolls and exposes a&nbsp;
section of the picture, he thrusts it forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:04.800 --> 01:26:09.840
toward the audience and speaking with a loud&nbsp;
voice in the foreign language, he explains&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:09.840 --> 01:26:15.840
the derivation of this section. The crowd sits&nbsp;
around and listens to him, sometimes laughing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:15.840 --> 01:26:21.240
sometimes crying, exactly as if the narrator&nbsp;
were reciting one of our popular romances.

01:26:24.080 --> 01:26:30.280
These shadow plays would typically&nbsp;
start at nightfall and go on until dawn&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:31.040 --> 01:26:36.880
and would be conducted behind a screen of&nbsp;
white cotton fabric using a lamp burning&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:36.880 --> 01:26:43.720
with coconut oil, usually cast in metal&nbsp;
in the form of the sacred bird Gauda.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:44.720 --> 01:26:49.320
The puppets were typically made&nbsp;
of goat hide or buffalo skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:49.960 --> 01:26:56.720
mounted on bamboo canes and placed in a&nbsp;
trunk of banana palm lying behind the screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:58.320 --> 01:27:05.040
The puppets had jointed limbs and were punctured&nbsp;
all over with intricate patterns of holes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:05.040 --> 01:27:10.800
that allowed the light through so that their&nbsp;
shadows could then be cast onto a screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:12.600 --> 01:27:19.880
These puppet shows were often accompanied&nbsp;
by music. In small village performances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:19.880 --> 01:27:25.160
this could simply be the drumming of the&nbsp;
puppeteer on the box where the puppets were kept.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:26.240 --> 01:27:32.880
But in the royal court, a puppeteer would&nbsp;
perform alongside a full orchestra of musicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:32.880 --> 01:27:38.240
known as a gamalan with a&nbsp;
whole host of 15 instruments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:40.320 --> 01:27:48.600
These night-long plays would unfold according to&nbsp;
strict traditions. In the hours between sunset&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:48.600 --> 01:27:55.280
and midnight, the puppeteer introduced the&nbsp;
characters and set up the conflict. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:55.280 --> 01:28:02.280
at the stroke of midnight, the drama burst out&nbsp;
into a frenzied clash between good and evil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:03.560 --> 01:28:10.800
The Portuguese traveler and apothecary&nbsp;
Tomé Perez, who visited Java around 1513,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:11.320 --> 01:28:16.000
wrote about seeing these forms&nbsp;
of theater and shadow puppetry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:18.360 --> 01:28:24.200
The land of Java is a land of performers&nbsp;
and masks of various Japanese kinds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:24.200 --> 01:28:30.920
and both men and women do thus. They have&nbsp;
entertainments of dancing and stories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:30.920 --> 01:28:37.800
They mime. They wear actors dresses and all their&nbsp;
clothes. They are certainly graceful. They have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:37.800 --> 01:28:44.360
music of bells. The sound of all of them playing&nbsp;
together is like an organ. These actors show a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:44.360 --> 01:28:51.560
thousand graces like these by day and night.&nbsp;
At night, they make shadows of various shapes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:53.680 --> 01:28:59.640
Over time, the performance of these&nbsp;
shadow plays took on a spiritual meaning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:01.200 --> 01:29:08.080
The puppeteer or dalang was thought to become&nbsp;
possessed by the power of the gods as he worked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:08.680 --> 01:29:15.640
And in the performance all the holy elements were&nbsp;
represented. The banana palm representing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:15.640 --> 01:29:23.600
earth, the cloth screen, the sky, the fire in the&nbsp;
coconut oil lamp, and of course, the interplay of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:23.600 --> 01:29:31.600
light and darkness, good and evil, as the stories&nbsp;
of gods and demons played out across the screen.

01:29:34.360 --> 01:29:37.720
These performances were&nbsp;
considered to have real power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:38.400 --> 01:29:45.480
Some such as the Bratayuta or a Javanese&nbsp;
version of the Indian epic the Mahabarat were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:45.480 --> 01:29:53.560
considered too potent to ever perform in their&nbsp;
entirety. As Thomas Stamford Raffles records,

01:29:56.080 --> 01:30:00.200
although the Wyang may represent&nbsp;
any portion of the Bratuda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:00.200 --> 01:30:04.040
there is a prevalent superstition that&nbsp;
a great war will be the inevitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:04.040 --> 01:30:08.400
consequence of performing at one&nbsp;
sitting the whole of this poem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:08.920 --> 01:30:14.560
It is gravely asserted that a chief of Kendall&nbsp;
tried this experiment about 50 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:14.560 --> 01:30:20.360
but that the performance was no sooner completed&nbsp;
than his country was laid to waste and destroyed.

01:30:23.400 --> 01:30:29.160
Other of these shadow plays were performed&nbsp;
at auspicious times in order to bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:29.160 --> 01:30:36.240
about certain effects with stories of the&nbsp;
goddess of rice told during the harvests&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:36.800 --> 01:30:44.960
and the great myths told during times of&nbsp;
trouble and strife. One particular shadow play&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:44.960 --> 01:30:51.000
was reserved solely for times when a plague&nbsp;
of mice or rats had descended on a village,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:51.560 --> 01:30:59.160
and its goal was to convince the rodents to&nbsp;
depart and dwell in the kingdom of vermin instead.

01:31:12.040 --> 01:31:12.280
Over

01:31:12.280 --> 01:31:19.560
the next centuries, the story of Ken Angro,&nbsp;
the first king of Singer Sari, would be told&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:19.560 --> 01:31:26.880
again and again in these kinds of performances and&nbsp;
would take on more and more fantastical elements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:27.960 --> 01:31:33.920
And for that reason, it's impossible to say&nbsp;
definitively which parts may have a grain of truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:33.920 --> 01:31:43.160
to them. But it's likely that some elements are&nbsp;
true. That Ken Angro was a common man of uncertain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:43.160 --> 01:31:50.760
birth and lineage. That he had been a thief or&nbsp;
brigand of some kind. That he rose to power in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:50.760 --> 01:31:58.280
the kingdom in a violent upheaval and crushed&nbsp;
the nearby kingdom of Kadiri in open battle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:59.960 --> 01:32:06.000
But after he comes to the throne, the tone&nbsp;
of the chronicle shifts and becomes more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:06.000 --> 01:32:13.960
grounded in historical reality. Many events&nbsp;
recorded during his reign are dated. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:13.960 --> 01:32:20.640
this second half of the chronicle is considered to&nbsp;
contain more historically verifiable information.

01:32:27.520 --> 01:32:32.480
What we know for sure is that over&nbsp;
the course of the 13th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:32.480 --> 01:32:40.760
Ken Angro's new kingdom of Singari would&nbsp;
gradually expand its power, wealth, and prestige.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:41.840 --> 01:32:47.960
But it was under the reign of his&nbsp;
greatgrandson, the fifth king of Singari,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:47.960 --> 01:32:56.560
that it would grow into a true empire. His name&nbsp;
was King Keranagara. And during his time on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:56.560 --> 01:33:03.760
throne, he would order expeditions to extend the&nbsp;
kingdom's influence not just over most of Java,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:04.320 --> 01:33:11.560
but across the sea to the large island of Borneo&nbsp;
to the north, the small island of Bali to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:11.560 --> 01:33:19.920
east, and even conduct a successful invasion of&nbsp;
Java's sister island of Sumatra in the northwest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:21.040 --> 01:33:26.680
Now the kingdom of Singari had&nbsp;
grown to be a true seagoing empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:27.400 --> 01:33:32.240
and it controlled virtually all of&nbsp;
the region's most crucial waterways.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:33.160 --> 01:33:40.800
And the most important of these was the narrow&nbsp;
sea corridor known as the straight of Malaka.

01:33:48.560 --> 01:33:56.160
The Malay Peninsula is a long body of land&nbsp;
that stretches southeast from the southern&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:56.160 --> 01:34:04.920
edge of the Asian continent where Thailand meets&nbsp;
Malaysia. And just off its southernmost point&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:04.920 --> 01:34:11.480
and following the line of the continental&nbsp;
shelf is the island of Sumatra and then Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:13.120 --> 01:34:21.800
Together, these form a sea wall of more than&nbsp;
3,000 km that stands between the Indian Ocean&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:21.800 --> 01:34:32.200
and the South Pacific and South China Sea. In this&nbsp;
massive wall, there are only two narrow gates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:32.920 --> 01:34:40.400
The southerntherly one known as the Straits of&nbsp;
Sunda and the northern one, the Straits of Malaka.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:42.680 --> 01:34:49.200
The American sailor and writer Herman&nbsp;
Melville writing in the mid 19th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:49.200 --> 01:34:58.040
describes the sight of these straits. Those&nbsp;
narrow straits of Sunda stand midway in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:58.040 --> 01:35:06.240
that vast rampart of islands like the central&nbsp;
gateway opening into some vast walled empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:07.120 --> 01:35:16.200
the inexhaustible wealth of spices and silks and&nbsp;
jewels and gold and ivory with which the thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:16.200 --> 01:35:25.400
islands of that oriental sea are enriched are&nbsp;
thus guarded from the all grasping western world.

01:35:28.400 --> 01:35:35.560
For much of history, it has been the wider and&nbsp;
deeper straight of Malaca that has been used most&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:35.560 --> 01:35:44.360
heavily. In fact, today it is the busiest shipping&nbsp;
channel in the world, seeing nearly 100,000 ships&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:44.360 --> 01:35:53.320
pass through it each year. Around a quarter of all&nbsp;
global trade passes through this narrow waterway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:53.920 --> 01:36:03.320
where the massive cities of Singapore and Koala&nbsp;
Lumpur are today located. And in the 13th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:03.320 --> 01:36:11.120
during the time of the singari kingdom this&nbsp;
waterway was no less crucial or lucrative.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:13.120 --> 01:36:18.720
Around the year 1515, the&nbsp;
Portuguese diplomat to Perez&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:18.720 --> 01:36:25.200
passed through the Yemen port of Aiden&nbsp;
on the Red Sea and wrote that goods&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:25.200 --> 01:36:31.440
transported thousands of miles from Malaca&nbsp;
featured prominently in the city's markets&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:32.040 --> 01:36:37.320
where merchants would carry them on&nbsp;
to Egypt and from there into Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:38.920 --> 01:36:44.600
The merchants from Cairo bring the merchandise&nbsp;
which comes from Italy and Greece and Damascus&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:44.600 --> 01:36:52.200
to Aiden. While the merchants of Aiden take back&nbsp;
with them all the products of Malaca, cloves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:52.200 --> 01:37:01.200
nutmeg, mace, sandalwood, Brazil wood, silks,&nbsp;
seed pearls, musk, porcelain, and other things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:03.560 --> 01:37:11.160
Control of the narrow waterway of the Malaca&nbsp;
strait gave King Keranagardada immense power&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:11.720 --> 01:37:20.080
despite the relatively small size of the kingdom&nbsp;
of Singasari. Now, anyone wishing to sail between&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:20.080 --> 01:37:27.440
China and India or the rest of the world either&nbsp;
did so with Kerta Nagada's permission or had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:27.440 --> 01:37:36.280
to take a detour of at least 2,000 km through&nbsp;
rough and stormy seas around this island wall.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:37.560 --> 01:37:44.520
As a result of this control, the power and&nbsp;
wealth of Singer Sari grew dramatically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:45.920 --> 01:37:52.680
But there were much larger empires in the region&nbsp;
who had a strong interest in making sure that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:52.680 --> 01:38:00.600
flow of goods by sea could pass through these&nbsp;
narrow straits. And as the 13th century came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:00.600 --> 01:38:09.440
an end, the kingdom of Singari came into direct&nbsp;
conflict with one of these much larger powers.

01:38:30.840 --> 01:38:39.920
In the year 1289, King Keranagara of the Javanese&nbsp;
Kingdom of Singari received a group of ambassadors&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:39.920 --> 01:38:49.280
at his court. They came finely dressed, horty,&nbsp;
and proud, and said that they had come to speak&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:49.280 --> 01:38:59.000
on behalf of the world's most powerful man.&nbsp;
That was the Mongol Emperor of China, Kubla Khn.

01:39:07.760 --> 01:39:14.640
As we saw in our 19th episode, from their&nbsp;
humble beginnings on the Mongolian step,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:14.640 --> 01:39:22.360
over the last century, the Mongols had embarked&nbsp;
on a remarkable series of campaigns to conquer all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:22.360 --> 01:39:30.120
of China, Persia, and the Middle East, along with&nbsp;
much of what is today Russia, and Eastern Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:31.640 --> 01:39:38.920
Founded by the warlord Chingis Khan, the Mongol&nbsp;
Empire was the largest contiguous land empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:38.920 --> 01:39:46.360
in history and combined the speed and power of&nbsp;
Mongol horsemen with the craftsmanship of Persia&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:46.920 --> 01:39:57.040
and the ingenuity and manpower of China. By this&nbsp;
point, the enormous Mongol Empire had split into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:57.040 --> 01:40:04.440
four separate domains. But the largest of these&nbsp;
was still the world's biggest empire at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:05.520 --> 01:40:13.200
That was the one ruled from China by the&nbsp;
grandson of Chingis Khan, the emperor Kubla.

01:40:15.800 --> 01:40:22.480
This hybrid Chinese Mongolian power&nbsp;
was known as the Yuan dynasty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:24.480 --> 01:40:32.960
But by this time, the empire had begun to&nbsp;
struggle. The emperor Kubli was now well into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:32.960 --> 01:40:42.320
his 70s and his domain was plagued by political&nbsp;
and economic problems. The Mongol Empire had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:42.320 --> 01:40:51.800
built on conquest, but now its terrifying engine&nbsp;
seemed to have run out of fuel. 8 years earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:51.800 --> 01:40:58.760
Kubla's forces had for the second time failed&nbsp;
to capture a foothold on the islands of Japan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:59.520 --> 01:41:06.560
His invasions of Burma and Vietnam had also&nbsp;
fizzled out with the Mongol forces struggling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:06.560 --> 01:41:15.160
in the tropical heat. The aging Mongol Emperor&nbsp;
desperately needed a victory, and he had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:15.160 --> 01:41:23.160
increasingly casting his gaze southwards across&nbsp;
the sea and towards the wealthy island of Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:24.800 --> 01:41:33.720
The Chinese chronicle, the Yuani, or History of&nbsp;
Yuan, describes this strange and farway land.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:35.600 --> 01:41:41.600
The customs and products of this land are not&nbsp;
much known, but as a rule, the barbarian countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:41.600 --> 01:41:48.080
over the sea produce many rare and valuable&nbsp;
things which fetch a high price in China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:48.080 --> 01:41:53.120
The inhabitants are strange and their nature&nbsp;
and speech are not understood by the Chinese.

01:41:55.640 --> 01:42:03.080
By that time, Java's kingagara had been&nbsp;
defying Kubla Khan for nearly a decade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:04.440 --> 01:42:11.640
Two previous ambassadorial visits to his court had&nbsp;
been sent home without any tribute for the Mongol&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:11.640 --> 01:42:18.280
Khn, and the defiance of this small island&nbsp;
was beginning to make the empire look weak.

01:42:22.840 --> 01:42:29.920
For this reason, in 1289, Kubla&nbsp;
sent a new party of envoys to Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:29.920 --> 01:42:34.200
to meet with the king and&nbsp;
give him a final ultimatum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:35.880 --> 01:42:43.240
They demanded that King Kerton swear an oath of&nbsp;
feelalty to the Mongol Emperor, pay him a hefty&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:43.240 --> 01:42:50.600
tribute, and send members of his royal family&nbsp;
to the Mongol court in China to act as hostages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:51.400 --> 01:42:59.160
But when these ambassadors arrived, they would&nbsp;
not find King Keranagara in an agreeable mood.

01:43:02.920 --> 01:43:09.560
The Javanese of the time considered repeated&nbsp;
requests of this kind to be exceedingly rude.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:10.600 --> 01:43:15.720
To Perez writes about the proud&nbsp;
nature of the Javanese people.

01:43:18.480 --> 01:43:23.240
The Japanese are men who if they&nbsp;
write once and do not get a reply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:23.240 --> 01:43:27.720
do not write again. Although it&nbsp;
may be of great importance to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:28.520 --> 01:43:36.120
the Japanese are daring men and determined to&nbsp;
die. They are gamblers and play for high stakes.

01:43:39.600 --> 01:43:45.920
King Keranagara was no different and&nbsp;
he would now make a tremendous gamble&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:46.440 --> 01:43:53.840
for the highest stakes possible. He ordered&nbsp;
that the Mongol ambassadors be punished.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:55.000 --> 01:44:01.360
By some accounts, their faces were branded&nbsp;
with hot irons and their ears cut off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:02.240 --> 01:44:06.840
a punishment typically reserved&nbsp;
for bandits and pirates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:08.200 --> 01:44:15.240
We can only imagine the rage of the Mongol&nbsp;
Emperor Kubla Khn upon hearing this news&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:15.240 --> 01:44:24.440
and seeing the mutilated faces of his ambassadors.&nbsp;
The history of Yuan describes what happened next.

01:44:27.520 --> 01:44:32.960
In the second month of the 29th year of the&nbsp;
Yuan dynasty, the emperor issued an order&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:32.960 --> 01:44:38.120
to the governor of Fujen, directing&nbsp;
him to send an army to subdue Java,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:38.120 --> 01:44:43.400
to collect soldiers to the number of&nbsp;
20,000, to send out a thousand ships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:43.400 --> 01:44:48.600
and to equip them with provisions for&nbsp;
a year and with 40,000 bars of silver.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:49.640 --> 01:44:55.160
The emperor said to them, "When you arrive&nbsp;
at Java, you must clearly proclaim that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:55.160 --> 01:45:01.240
have lately cut the face of the imperial envoy,&nbsp;
Mongchi, and that you have come to punish them."

01:45:05.000 --> 01:45:14.640
This expedition set sail 3 years later in the&nbsp;
year 1292, but it was to be a difficult voyage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:15.720 --> 01:45:21.880
The history of Yuan describes the&nbsp;
troubles they encountered on the Java Sea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:23.400 --> 01:45:29.680
The wind was strong and the sea very rough so&nbsp;
that the ships rolled heavily and the soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:29.680 --> 01:45:36.520
could not eat for many days. The forces to attack&nbsp;
Java were driven by the wind to one island and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:36.520 --> 01:45:43.280
many ships were wrecked. Over a hundred men who&nbsp;
were ill from the long beating about in the storm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:43.280 --> 01:45:47.240
were unable to leave with the rest of&nbsp;
the expedition and were left there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:47.760 --> 01:45:51.640
And today the Chinese live mixed&nbsp;
up with the native families.

01:45:54.240 --> 01:46:01.160
Despite their troubles on the voyage, the Mongol&nbsp;
forces eventually arrived on the shores of Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:01.160 --> 01:46:08.600
in the next year of 1293, ready to punish&nbsp;
King Keranagara for defying the empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:08.600 --> 01:46:15.320
and mistreating its ambassadors. But&nbsp;
when they arrived, they would find their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:15.320 --> 01:46:22.760
enthusiasm for revenge had been somewhat&nbsp;
deflated. In fact, in the preceding year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:23.280 --> 01:46:33.200
the defiant kingagara had been overthrown&nbsp;
and killed by a usurper named Ja Katon.

01:46:42.920 --> 01:46:51.520
Ja Katon was the ruler of a city called Kediri,&nbsp;
which controlled a province covering much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:51.520 --> 01:47:00.880
central Java. For a long time, Kadiri had actually&nbsp;
been the predominant power on the island. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:00.880 --> 01:47:08.120
after its king, Dandan Gendis had been defeated&nbsp;
in battle by the hero Ken Angro, Kadiri had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:08.120 --> 01:47:18.960
eclipsed by the rise of Singari. Now Kadiri was&nbsp;
a vassel of King Keranagara but it was by far&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:18.960 --> 01:47:28.240
the most powerful of his subjects and as a result&nbsp;
never sat comfortably within his kingdom. The epic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:28.240 --> 01:47:37.800
poem the Deshavanana describes the new ruler&nbsp;
of Kadiri Ja Katon as cunning and malevolent.

01:47:45.760 --> 01:47:53.800
There was a vassal king, a villain known by the&nbsp;
name of Ja Katong, who there in the land of Kadiri&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:53.800 --> 01:47:58.320
desired to replace the king and was&nbsp;
outstandingly clever at laying schemes.

01:48:07.040 --> 01:48:14.000
At the time, the majority of King&nbsp;
Keranagara's forces were away from Java,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:14.000 --> 01:48:22.240
fighting to increase his territory across the&nbsp;
sea in Sumatra. And so, Ja Katon saw his chance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:24.080 --> 01:48:30.520
According to the Paraton or Book of&nbsp;
Kings, Jaakong was encouraged to seize&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:30.520 --> 01:48:37.160
the opportunity by one of his allies&nbsp;
who sent him a thinly veiled message.

01:48:45.960 --> 01:48:51.320
The secret letter said, "My&nbsp;
lord, I respectfully tell you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:51.880 --> 01:49:00.040
if his majesty has any consideration and desire to&nbsp;
hunt. Now his majesty has the chance to so hunt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:00.880 --> 01:49:07.000
It will be the best and the most opportune&nbsp;
moment. There is no danger. There are no tigers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:07.000 --> 01:49:14.840
wild buffaloos, nor snakes, or even thorns.&nbsp;
There is only one tiger, but it has no teeth.

01:49:23.800 --> 01:49:29.640
Urged on by this coded message,&nbsp;
King Jayak rose in rebellion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:30.840 --> 01:49:37.160
His city of Kadiri was not far from the&nbsp;
capital of Singari, but it was on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:37.160 --> 01:49:45.120
other side of a number of towering volcanic&nbsp;
cones, mounts Kellud, Arjuna, and Kawi.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:46.560 --> 01:49:53.920
to strike at Singari and depose the king. He could&nbsp;
either march north through the easier territory&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:53.920 --> 01:50:02.120
of the northern plains or south through the&nbsp;
rugged hill country riven by gorges and ravines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:03.560 --> 01:50:10.200
The cunning Ja Katon considered his&nbsp;
options and came up with a plan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:11.640 --> 01:50:20.640
In 1292, alarming news reached King Kerton's&nbsp;
court. Messengers would have burst into his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:20.640 --> 01:50:26.320
throne room and breathlessly told him&nbsp;
that an army had been seen rampaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:26.320 --> 01:50:33.240
through the farmland to the north, flying the&nbsp;
distinctive white and red banners of Kadiri.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:34.520 --> 01:50:40.520
They had taken the toothless tiger&nbsp;
King Keranagara completely by surprise.

01:50:46.320 --> 01:50:53.520
Now, King Jayak Katang set out to attack. His army&nbsp;
coming from the north consisted of bad people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:54.160 --> 01:50:59.920
They were full of flags and sounds.&nbsp;
The area to the north was devastated.

01:51:06.280 --> 01:51:11.200
Dismayed at the destruction, the&nbsp;
king appointed one of his son-in-laws&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:11.200 --> 01:51:18.160
to lead what forces he had remaining&nbsp;
in Java. A man named Raden Vijaya.

01:51:20.480 --> 01:51:28.160
This young man marched north with the remains&nbsp;
of the royal army and met Jaakatong's rampaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:28.160 --> 01:51:36.600
forces in battle. As one Javanese inscription&nbsp;
carved at a place called Kudadu describes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:38.560 --> 01:51:45.520
when King Jaya Katon's troops reached&nbsp;
the village of Jasun Wong Kal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:45.520 --> 01:51:52.240
King Kerta Nagara sent his son-in-law&nbsp;
Raden Wijaya to fight the enemy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:53.080 --> 01:52:01.080
After the battle, the enemy fled while suffering&nbsp;
a great loss. Seemingly they disappeared forever.

01:52:05.320 --> 01:52:11.720
Raden Vagaya had succeeded in the north&nbsp;
and defended his father-in-law's kingdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:11.720 --> 01:52:20.680
which he was set to one day inherit. But&nbsp;
now the true strategy of Jaong was revealed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:22.280 --> 01:52:28.400
While this loud northern force had been burning&nbsp;
and sacking their way through the north of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:28.400 --> 01:52:36.440
kingdom, a much larger second force had also&nbsp;
advanced in the south. This army moved much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:36.440 --> 01:52:44.800
more stealthily through the ravines and valleys&nbsp;
of the central highlands as the paraton describes.

01:52:50.520 --> 01:52:56.760
Then a large army descended from Kadiri&nbsp;
coming from the banks of the Axa River&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:56.760 --> 01:53:03.000
heading towards Lour. They were not&nbsp;
allowed to carry flags or make any noise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:04.240 --> 01:53:09.000
Upon arrival at Sidabana, they&nbsp;
headed straight for Singasari.

01:53:09.000 --> 01:53:18.800
be son.

01:53:18.800 --> 01:53:22.520
This force would go completely unnoticed and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:22.520 --> 01:53:30.120
apparently arrived at the kingdom's capital&nbsp;
of Coutaraja while King Keranagara was drunk.

01:53:33.960 --> 01:53:41.160
Drinking in Java was a serious business and a&nbsp;
large number of alcoholic beverages were consumed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:41.160 --> 01:53:50.240
for pleasure, especially on special occasions.&nbsp;
One Javanese poem known as the Sumana Santa&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:50.240 --> 01:53:57.840
describes the drinks available at an&nbsp;
idealized wedding. The drinks poured&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:57.840 --> 01:54:05.000
continuously from rock crystal pitches like&nbsp;
fountains of palm syrup. Sugar cane, wine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:05.000 --> 01:54:12.640
and rice beer were drunk one after the other like&nbsp;
holy water and meat welling up from glass vessels.

01:54:16.920 --> 01:54:22.720
But the influence of tantric Buddhism meant&nbsp;
that drinking was more than just for fun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:23.480 --> 01:54:29.160
In fact, it was at times a spiritual&nbsp;
practice and even a royal duty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:30.480 --> 01:54:37.680
The king was required to take part in ceremonies&nbsp;
known as chakra puja which involved the drawing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:37.680 --> 01:54:44.920
of geometric designs on the earth with colored&nbsp;
powder after which the ritual could take place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:46.720 --> 01:54:52.880
The historian Miranda Shaw describes&nbsp;
the unfolding of one of these rituals.

01:54:55.800 --> 01:55:01.920
An elaborate array of offerings and foods are&nbsp;
laid out. The participants dawn special insignia&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:01.920 --> 01:55:08.920
like bone ornaments and crowns and use musical&nbsp;
instruments of archaic design such as skull drums,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:08.920 --> 01:55:15.280
thighbone trumpets, and conch horns for inducing&nbsp;
heightened awareness. Practitioners sit in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:15.280 --> 01:55:21.760
circle and partake of sacramental meat and wine&nbsp;
served in skull cups. The feast culminates in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:21.760 --> 01:55:27.040
the performance of tantric dances and music&nbsp;
that must never be disclosed to outsiders.

01:55:30.040 --> 01:55:37.720
While this ceremony was a crucial part of King&nbsp;
Keranagara's royal calendar, the timing couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:37.720 --> 01:55:46.360
have been worse. Jaakatong's concealed force&nbsp;
arrived outside the city walls without warning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:47.280 --> 01:55:52.240
They took the garrison by surprise&nbsp;
and poured over the walls of the city&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:52.240 --> 01:56:00.640
while the king was thus engaged. The city&nbsp;
was taken and King Keranagaram was killed.

01:56:08.440 --> 01:56:16.200
The king's son-in-law, Raden Vaya, was still&nbsp;
celebrating his victory in the north, and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:16.200 --> 01:56:24.360
must have been distraught when the news reached&nbsp;
him. He had won his battle, but lost the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:24.360 --> 01:56:32.760
and now everything he had stood to inherit&nbsp;
had also been taken by the usurper Ja Katon.

01:56:35.440 --> 01:56:43.520
Raden Vagaya now retreated to the rainforests of&nbsp;
the northeast and left the capital and the throne&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:43.520 --> 01:56:52.720
in the hands of the rebel Ja Katon. The city of&nbsp;
Kusarajjo, the heart of the Singosari kingdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:52.720 --> 01:56:59.280
was looted and depopulated and many&nbsp;
of its people taken back to Kadiri.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:00.880 --> 01:57:06.360
Others simply fled into the safety of&nbsp;
the countryside and never returned.

01:57:08.760 --> 01:57:14.880
Some stonemasons working on a new temple&nbsp;
in the city simply dropped their tools in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:14.880 --> 01:57:19.480
the middle of carving their reliefs&nbsp;
and never came back to finish them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:20.520 --> 01:57:27.640
After that, the city was left to crumble&nbsp;
and be taken over by the encroaching forest.

01:57:30.240 --> 01:57:38.160
Now the dispossessed prince Raden Vagaya&nbsp;
and the rebel usurper Ja Katon faced off&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:38.160 --> 01:57:45.200
against one another. But it seems neither side&nbsp;
of this civil war was confident in victory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:46.600 --> 01:57:52.560
Raden vagaya wasn't strong enough to march&nbsp;
south and take his enemy's capital of Kadiri.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:53.400 --> 01:58:00.400
And meanwhile, Jaakong couldn't leave his city&nbsp;
and risk losing his shaky grasp on the throne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:02.080 --> 01:58:07.520
Both parties entered into an&nbsp;
uneasy negotiation. And eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:07.520 --> 01:58:17.000
the rebel king settled for buying off Raden&nbsp;
Vaga. In exchange for laying down his arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:17.000 --> 01:58:23.160
the prince would be granted a piece of land,&nbsp;
and there he would be allowed to build a city.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:25.560 --> 01:58:32.760
Rod and Vaga, now completely out of other&nbsp;
options and stranded in the wild rainforest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:32.760 --> 01:58:41.760
had no choice but to agree. But it must have&nbsp;
been with no small amount of bitterness.

01:58:45.280 --> 01:58:51.560
The prince must have felt bitter too when he&nbsp;
reached the piece of land that Ja Katang had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:51.560 --> 01:59:00.480
granted him. It was a wild overgrown jungle in&nbsp;
the middle of a sparsely inhabited area known&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:00.480 --> 01:59:08.120
as the Tardic Forest. But his fate was&nbsp;
sealed, and he swallowed his bitterness&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:08.120 --> 01:59:13.320
and ordered his people to begin clearing&nbsp;
the land for construction and farming.

01:59:17.520 --> 01:59:25.120
The old growth forests of Java were&nbsp;
dense and wild, fed by the volcanic soil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:27.040 --> 01:59:33.760
Colorful hornvilles roosted in the treetops,&nbsp;
while hidden high among the branches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:33.760 --> 01:59:38.760
gibbons greeted the rising sun&nbsp;
with their long mournful songs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:40.440 --> 01:59:45.640
Troops of long-tailed macac&nbsp;
monkeys leapt through the trees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:46.240 --> 01:59:54.520
while sambar deer crept through the shade of the&nbsp;
forest. Great pythons lay coiled in the trees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:55.320 --> 02:00:03.680
and dangerous animals like the Javan leopard and&nbsp;
wild boar lurked hidden in the green undergrowth.

02:00:06.160 --> 02:00:12.120
Everywhere could be heard the creaking of&nbsp;
cicardas and frogs calling to one another&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:12.120 --> 02:00:21.400
from hidden pools. At sunset, fruit bats rose in&nbsp;
their thousands from their hidden caves, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:21.400 --> 02:00:30.040
fireflies would have been visible at night. The&nbsp;
trees were the most ancient life of the forest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:31.040 --> 02:00:36.600
Towering hardwoods like teik, ebony,&nbsp;
and tamarind would have risen here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:37.120 --> 02:00:45.040
interspersed with bamboo groves, strangler&nbsp;
figs, palms, and tangled lyana vines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:45.040 --> 02:00:53.600
all competing for every shaft of sunlight.&nbsp;
But when they began cutting down this forest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:53.600 --> 02:01:00.280
Raden Vaga's men found that a particular&nbsp;
kind of tree grew there in great quantities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:01.600 --> 02:01:09.600
This was the tree that grows a fruit which in&nbsp;
Indonesia is called maja and is known elsewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:09.600 --> 02:01:19.680
as a wood apple, elephant apple or Indian quint.&nbsp;
These maja fruits are pear-shaped and the size&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:19.680 --> 02:01:26.680
of a large grapefruit with a thick hard woody&nbsp;
rind that needs to be cracked with a hammer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:28.560 --> 02:01:36.560
Inside is an orangey yellow pulp that gives off&nbsp;
the scent of roses but tastes sweet and bitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:36.560 --> 02:01:43.480
a little like marmalade. The word&nbsp;
for bitter in Japanese is parit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:44.120 --> 02:01:49.320
And due to the large numbers of these&nbsp;
maja fruits growing in this region,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:49.320 --> 02:01:58.400
the people named this place Maja Parit or&nbsp;
Bitter Wood apple as the Paraton describes.

02:02:06.720 --> 02:02:13.800
When the mades were building the village, a hungry&nbsp;
man lacking supplies while cutting down the forest&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:13.800 --> 02:02:20.080
ate a marajger fruit. He found it bitter and&nbsp;
threw away all the mara fruit he had picked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:20.920 --> 02:02:27.440
The majger fruit there became known for its&nbsp;
bitter taste and the place was then named.

02:02:27.440 --> 02:02:28.560
abide

02:02:37.240 --> 02:02:44.680
for Raden Vajaya and his people. The name&nbsp;
Maja Pahit was a perfect encapsulation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:44.680 --> 02:02:54.240
the bitterness of their situation. They had lost a&nbsp;
kingdom and gained a plot of wild jungle in return&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:55.040 --> 02:03:00.160
and all because of the treachery of Ja Katon.

02:03:00.720 --> 02:03:09.400
They all must have shared a single desire to&nbsp;
throw out the rebel king and unite Java once more.

02:03:12.120 --> 02:03:20.880
Their chance would come in the year 1293 when the&nbsp;
sails of the Mongol fleet appeared on the horizon.

02:03:38.040 --> 02:03:43.240
The Mongol fleet that had been&nbsp;
sent to punish the people of Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:43.240 --> 02:03:52.120
was led by a commander named Sherbi. When&nbsp;
he heard of the death of King Keranagara,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:52.120 --> 02:03:58.160
he must have found himself in a&nbsp;
quandry. His emperor demanded vengeance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:58.160 --> 02:04:05.400
and he and his men had sailed for months to get&nbsp;
there. He must have feared leaving empty-handed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:06.960 --> 02:04:14.160
It was while he was puzzling over this situation&nbsp;
that Sherbi was approached by a messenger&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:14.160 --> 02:04:21.840
from the bitter lord of the new city of&nbsp;
Majapahit, the dispossessed prince Raden Vagaya.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:23.160 --> 02:04:29.200
The Chinese history of Yuan describes&nbsp;
the deal that the Mongols struck

02:04:32.320 --> 02:04:36.720
at that time. and Java carried on an&nbsp;
old feud with the neighboring country&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:37.320 --> 02:04:40.040
and the old king of Java had already been killed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:41.000 --> 02:04:48.080
The son-in-law of the former Raden Vijaya had&nbsp;
attacked Jaakaton but could not overcome him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:49.080 --> 02:04:55.560
He therefore retired to Maja Pahit. And when&nbsp;
he heard that Sheibi and his army had arrived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:55.560 --> 02:05:00.960
he sent envoys with an account of his rivers&nbsp;
and seap ports and a map of the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:01.480 --> 02:05:08.920
offering his submission and asking for&nbsp;
assistance. She then advanced with all his forces.

02:05:12.080 --> 02:05:17.120
For the Mongols, the arrangement was&nbsp;
a neat way to solve their problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:18.560 --> 02:05:25.640
They would help Raden Vaga overthrow the rebel Ja&nbsp;
Katang and place him on the throne as a kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:25.640 --> 02:05:35.280
puppet king, swearing allegiance to the Mongols&nbsp;
in China. Then Sherb could return to Kubla Khan&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:35.280 --> 02:05:40.680
and say that he had brought the defiant&nbsp;
little island back under control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:42.520 --> 02:05:48.640
But among Raden Vajaya's men, it must&nbsp;
have been a controversial proposal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:50.080 --> 02:05:58.520
The Javanese were a proud people, and few had any&nbsp;
desire to be ruled by the faroff Mongol Emperor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:00.520 --> 02:06:06.000
Some may even have called Raden&nbsp;
Vaga a coward and a traitor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:07.360 --> 02:06:11.960
But all this time he was&nbsp;
hatching a plot of his own.

02:06:14.960 --> 02:06:23.760
The later chronicle of the Paraton describes the&nbsp;
ensuing war referring to the Mongols as Tatas.

02:06:34.600 --> 02:06:40.520
The Tata army came out from the north while&nbsp;
the Majapahit armies came out from the east.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:41.480 --> 02:06:49.080
King Jayakong was confused, unsure which to&nbsp;
guard. Then he was attacked heavily from the north&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:49.080 --> 02:06:56.040
by the Tata army. King Jay Katang, who was&nbsp;
fighting to the north, armed with a shield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:56.040 --> 02:07:02.640
was attacked together by the Tata people and&nbsp;
was finally caught and imprisoned by the Tatas.

02:07:13.120 --> 02:07:21.640
The Mongols had now become kingmakers in Java.&nbsp;
The 13th century Persian historian Abdullah Wasaf&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:22.240 --> 02:07:26.960
describes how they put Radan&nbsp;
Vaya on the Javanese throne.

02:07:29.600 --> 02:07:35.920
His majesty Kublai put the old king's&nbsp;
son-in-law Vijaya on the steps of the throne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:35.920 --> 02:07:43.680
He bestowed on him a ceremonial dress of honor and&nbsp;
conferred on him much grace. And in exchange for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:43.680 --> 02:07:49.360
the payment of tribute and taxes of pearls&nbsp;
and gold, he left the island in his hands.

02:07:54.640 --> 02:07:59.440
The Mongol Emperor Kubli must have&nbsp;
been ecstatic when he heard the news.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:00.280 --> 02:08:07.280
Finally, here was a victory he could show to&nbsp;
his people. The wealthy island of Java would&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:07.280 --> 02:08:15.200
now be added to his imperial domain along with its&nbsp;
control of major shipping lanes. And perhaps now&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:15.200 --> 02:08:23.440
the fortunes of his teetering empire would finally&nbsp;
turn around. But that was not to be the case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:25.520 --> 02:08:32.880
That's because the new king Raden Vaya had no&nbsp;
intention of ruling Java as a vassel of Kubla&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:32.880 --> 02:08:40.640
Khan. And the moment he got to power, he turned&nbsp;
on the Mongol forces who had helped him seize&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:40.640 --> 02:08:49.480
the throne. The Chinese history of Yuan gives&nbsp;
a tur account of this disastrous turnaround.

02:08:52.280 --> 02:08:57.960
Raden Vagaya asked permission to return to&nbsp;
his country in order to prepare a new letter&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:57.960 --> 02:09:03.600
of submission to the emperor and to take the&nbsp;
precious articles in his possession back to court.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:04.680 --> 02:09:10.320
Shui consented to this and sent two&nbsp;
officers with 200 men to go with him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:11.480 --> 02:09:18.880
But on the way, Raden Vagaya killed the two&nbsp;
officers and revolted again. After which he&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:18.880 --> 02:09:25.880
availed himself of the circumstance that the main&nbsp;
army was returning to attack it from both sides.

02:09:29.560 --> 02:09:32.960
The Mongol army believed&nbsp;
their mission to be complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:32.960 --> 02:09:41.320
and were already packing their ships to leave.&nbsp;
The seasonal winds were about to change direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:41.320 --> 02:09:46.920
and if they didn't move fast, they might&nbsp;
become stranded on Java for another 6 months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:48.120 --> 02:09:54.480
When Raden Vaga's soldiers burst out&nbsp;
of the forest in ambush, firing arrows&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:54.480 --> 02:10:01.240
and darts from their blow guns, the Mongol&nbsp;
Yuan troops were taken completely by surprise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:02.880 --> 02:10:09.320
The Javanese epic poem Bulman Taka&nbsp;
describes a battle of this kind.

02:10:12.880 --> 02:10:19.280
They were thrown into disarray and in fear the&nbsp;
demon army fled and many of the demons and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:19.280 --> 02:10:25.760
leaders were struck dead. They withdrew in haste&nbsp;
to the shelter of the fort but were pursued.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:26.320 --> 02:10:33.080
Others shuddering sought a way of resisting&nbsp;
but lacked the strength and hit by arrows&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:33.080 --> 02:10:40.720
floundered about and all of them left in fear&nbsp;
and ran away. The whole fort was in uproar&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:40.720 --> 02:10:47.880
and all shouted in confusion. The fires glowed and&nbsp;
blazed up frightfully like an erupting volcano.

02:10:54.840 --> 02:11:01.840
Thousands were killed and the remains of the&nbsp;
Mongol army fled in disarray back to the ships&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:02.440 --> 02:11:05.680
as the history of Yuan describes.

02:11:10.960 --> 02:11:15.640
Sheep was left behind and was cut off from the&nbsp;
rest of the army. He was forced to fight his&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:15.640 --> 02:11:21.160
way back to the ships for a distance of more&nbsp;
than 100 miles. At last, he embarked again&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:21.160 --> 02:11:27.880
and reached China on a voyage of 68 days. Of&nbsp;
his soldiers, more than 3,000 men had died.

02:11:32.680 --> 02:11:40.240
When news of this betrayal reached the emperor&nbsp;
Kubla Khn, we can only imagine his rage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:41.480 --> 02:11:46.040
He ordered the general sherb to receive 17 lashes&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:46.640 --> 02:11:52.600
and commanded a third of his property to be&nbsp;
confiscated as punishment for his failure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:54.320 --> 02:12:01.080
He ordered his ministers to begin immediate&nbsp;
preparations for a fullscale invasion of Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:01.080 --> 02:12:09.080
with an even larger army of 100,000 men. But&nbsp;
these plans would never come to fruition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:10.880 --> 02:12:18.800
The disaster in Java would be the last campaign&nbsp;
of Kubla Khan's reign and he died the next year&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:18.800 --> 02:12:29.080
in February of 1294 at the age of 78. After&nbsp;
that, the Mongol Yuan dynasty would enter&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:29.080 --> 02:12:37.640
into a steady and irrevocable decline and would&nbsp;
collapse entirely little more than 70 years later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:39.280 --> 02:12:44.680
One great empire was coming to&nbsp;
an end. But in its collapse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:44.680 --> 02:12:51.200
it gave way for the rise of what was now the&nbsp;
undisputed major power on the island of Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:51.880 --> 02:12:59.200
and in the archipelago of Nusan&nbsp;
Tar, the new power of Maja Parahit

02:13:06.880 --> 02:13:13.640
on the 15th day of the month of Kartika,&nbsp;
the 8th month of the Hindu calendar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:13.640 --> 02:13:18.320
Raden Vagaya was crowned king&nbsp;
in the palace of Majapahit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:19.680 --> 02:13:26.480
What had once been a stretch of wild jungle&nbsp;
was now the beating heart of Java's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:26.480 --> 02:13:34.120
powerful kingdom. The Chinese history of Sun&nbsp;
describes the appearance of the kings of Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:34.120 --> 02:13:40.320
And from this we might imagine how this new&nbsp;
king looked on the day of his coronation.

02:13:43.800 --> 02:13:52.480
The king has hair in a knot upon the top of&nbsp;
his head. He wears golden bells, a silk robe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:52.480 --> 02:14:00.960
and shoes of leather. He sits on a square couch&nbsp;
and his functionaries who see him daily salute&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:00.960 --> 02:14:10.520
him three times on going away. When he goes out,&nbsp;
he rides on an elephant or sits in a carriage and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:10.520 --> 02:14:20.240
from 5 to 700 soldiers follow him. When the people&nbsp;
see the king, they squat down until he has passed.

02:14:29.800 --> 02:14:35.120
Raden vagaya would rule as the&nbsp;
king of Majapahit for 16 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:35.680 --> 02:14:38.560
But his reign would be a troubled one.

02:14:44.680 --> 02:14:49.080
From the earliest days of his&nbsp;
rule, he faced rebellions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:49.080 --> 02:14:55.840
many of them from men who had fought alongside&nbsp;
him against Ja Katang and the Mongols.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:57.520 --> 02:15:02.840
Many of these disagreements were sparked over&nbsp;
the question of who would become the king's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:02.840 --> 02:15:10.280
Maha party, a powerful and influential position&nbsp;
that combined elements of a prime minister and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:10.280 --> 02:15:19.720
head general and essentially acted as the king's&nbsp;
right hand. Whatever his skills as a diplomat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:19.720 --> 02:15:25.720
it seems Raden Vagaya struggled to manage the&nbsp;
ambitions of the men who served under him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:27.320 --> 02:15:32.840
For this reason, he spent much of his rule&nbsp;
putting down rebellions all over Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:34.280 --> 02:15:38.080
In an effort to cement the&nbsp;
legitimacy of his new dynasty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:38.640 --> 02:15:43.440
he would marry the remaining three daughters&nbsp;
of his dead father-in-law, Curtenagara,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:43.960 --> 02:15:49.480
and ensure that the royal line of&nbsp;
succession would pass entirely through him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:50.120 --> 02:15:56.960
But still the problems persisted and the new&nbsp;
kingdom teetered on the brink of destruction.

02:15:59.880 --> 02:16:09.040
When Raden Vagaya died in the year 1309, he must&nbsp;
have hoped that his only son Ja would set things&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:09.040 --> 02:16:18.280
right. But due to the apparently deficient nature&nbsp;
of Gygra's character, this would not be the case.

02:16:24.440 --> 02:16:29.640
King Gienegra would rule for nearly 20&nbsp;
years after the death of his father.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:30.480 --> 02:16:38.600
But he was a widely disliked character. So much&nbsp;
so that the author of the chronicle the paraton&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:38.600 --> 02:16:43.121
would give him the nickname&nbsp;
Kala Gt meaning weak villain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:44.120 --> 02:16:50.519
Meanwhile, the Javanese folk tradition has&nbsp;
remembered him by the name Bajang Ratu or&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:50.520 --> 02:16:58.241
dwarf king, referencing either his short stature&nbsp;
or simply the uninspiring nature of his rule.

02:17:01.120 --> 02:17:07.200
Gienegra faced multiple attempts on&nbsp;
his life and widespread rebellions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:07.200 --> 02:17:13.720
even worse than his fathers. The most serious&nbsp;
of which was led by a man named Kouti.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:15.360 --> 02:17:21.559
This uprising became so dangerous that the king&nbsp;
was forced to abandon his capital for a time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:22.320 --> 02:17:28.840
And it's during this time of upheaval that&nbsp;
a man would walk onto the historical stage&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:29.360 --> 02:17:34.040
who would turn around the fortunes&nbsp;
of this new and struggling kingdom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:35.240 --> 02:17:40.400
He was a soldier who led the king's&nbsp;
bodyguard during the Couti rebellion&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:40.920 --> 02:17:47.880
and gained a position of trust in the&nbsp;
royal court. His name was Gaja Mada.

02:17:56.440 --> 02:18:02.759
Gaja Mada's name came from the old&nbsp;
Javanese word gaja meaning elephant&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:03.320 --> 02:18:10.519
and ma which is a difficult to translate word&nbsp;
describing a state of furious intoxication.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:11.240 --> 02:18:18.080
This probably alluded to the period of a few&nbsp;
weeks every year when male elephants enter their&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:18.080 --> 02:18:27.800
breeding cycle called must and their testosterone&nbsp;
levels explode. During this time, a bull elephant&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:27.800 --> 02:18:34.640
seeps liquid from a gland around its ears and&nbsp;
becomes extremely aggressive and dangerous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:36.280 --> 02:18:43.800
Elephants had long been used for warfare in&nbsp;
Java. They formed the centerpiece of any army&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:43.800 --> 02:18:49.520
and allowed royals and generals to command&nbsp;
the battlefield from an elevated position.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:51.720 --> 02:18:56.800
Although prehistoric fossils of native&nbsp;
elephants have been found in Java,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:56.800 --> 02:19:00.080
this species had long been extinct by this point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:00.640 --> 02:19:05.441
And during the medieval period,&nbsp;
elephants were imported from other lands.

02:19:07.800 --> 02:19:14.680
They were enormous, expensive, and prestigious.&nbsp;
Their tusks sometimes adorned with metal&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:14.680 --> 02:19:21.361
implements to make them even more deadly and&nbsp;
their sides often oiled before going into battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:21.360 --> 02:19:28.719
to prevent the enemy from climbing on their backs.&nbsp;
So this commander's name would have generated&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:28.720 --> 02:19:37.920
images of strength, vitality, and an unstoppable&nbsp;
force. and by all accounts it would be a good fit.

02:19:43.240 --> 02:19:48.559
Although the elephant general Gaja Mada would&nbsp;
eventually rise through the new kingdom of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:48.560 --> 02:19:55.680
Majapahit to become one of its most influential&nbsp;
statesmen, his birth was relatively unexceptional.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:57.000 --> 02:20:02.600
We know little about his early life, but he&nbsp;
was likely born into a poor family in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:02.600 --> 02:20:09.880
area around the city of Majapi, and his father&nbsp;
was possibly killed in the war with the Mongols.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:11.560 --> 02:20:18.960
But he would prove his worth and become the head&nbsp;
of the king's elite royal bodyguard, the Bayara.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:20.320 --> 02:20:23.759
After successfully protecting the&nbsp;
king through the Kouti rebellion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:24.480 --> 02:20:28.679
Gajamada would be rewarded with the&nbsp;
governorship of a small province.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:29.560 --> 02:20:35.201
Finally, he would be named Maha&nbsp;
Parti or the right hand of the king.

02:20:38.440 --> 02:20:45.280
The epic poem the Desha Varana describes this&nbsp;
warrior statesman in the following terms.

02:20:52.480 --> 02:20:59.200
Gaja Mada is the excellent party of Maja,&nbsp;
an officer valiant, discerning in tactics&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:59.200 --> 02:21:05.240
with a firm foundation in loyalty and devotion&nbsp;
to the king. Eloquent and skilled in speech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:05.240 --> 02:21:08.880
polite and patient, constantly exerting himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:09.400 --> 02:21:14.360
unremitting, he watches over the king's&nbsp;
position as supreme ruler of the world.

02:21:23.120 --> 02:21:30.160
King Jane was clearly a man ills suited&nbsp;
to rule. But with Gaja Mada's help,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:30.160 --> 02:21:35.720
he was able to keep hold of his kingdom&nbsp;
by his fingernails. And it's in the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:35.720 --> 02:21:42.640
decade of his reign that a visitor from a&nbsp;
far off land arrived on the shores of Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:44.880 --> 02:21:49.360
He was a traveler from the&nbsp;
northern Italian region of Friulia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:50.000 --> 02:21:56.760
then a territory of the Holy Roman Empire.&nbsp;
And his name was Odorich of Pordinon.

02:22:07.080 --> 02:22:15.760
Around the year 1318, Odorich had departed from&nbsp;
Venice and spent the next decade or so traveling&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:15.760 --> 02:22:23.000
all over Asia, passing through Persia and seeing&nbsp;
the ruins of the ancient city of Percepilus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:24.240 --> 02:22:28.320
He then sailed through the&nbsp;
strait of Hormuz to India&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:28.320 --> 02:22:34.120
and from there through the straits of&nbsp;
Malaca to Borneo, Sumatra and Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:36.240 --> 02:22:42.200
When he returned home, he dictated the&nbsp;
memoirs of his travels in a work entitled&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:42.200 --> 02:22:51.320
the relatio de miraabilus oriental tatarorum or&nbsp;
the account of the marvels of the eastern tartas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:52.480 --> 02:23:00.200
In this text he provides a remarkable&nbsp;
snapshot of life in Java around the year 1320.

02:23:03.240 --> 02:23:12.800
There is an island which is called Java which is&nbsp;
a good 3,000 mi around. The king of this island&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:12.800 --> 02:23:20.680
has under him seven crowned kings as vassels.&nbsp;
This island is very populous and is the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:20.680 --> 02:23:28.840
best of all islands that be on the earth.&nbsp;
One finds there cloves, pepper, nutmeg, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:28.840 --> 02:23:35.720
many other precious spices that they grind there&nbsp;
and all manner of necessities in great quantity.

02:23:39.400 --> 02:23:44.320
Odric was particularly impressed&nbsp;
by the royal palace of Geneagra&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:44.320 --> 02:23:49.599
which was richly decorated in gold&nbsp;
mined from the volcanic mountain ranges.

02:23:52.280 --> 02:23:59.400
The king of this island lives in a marvelous&nbsp;
and very large palace. Its steps are of gold&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:59.400 --> 02:24:06.519
and silver alternately. The paving tiles&nbsp;
are likewise one gold and the next silver.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:07.160 --> 02:24:14.480
The walls are covered with plates of gold and&nbsp;
there are mounted men sculpted out of gold on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:14.480 --> 02:24:20.839
them with golden circles around their heads like&nbsp;
the diadems we make for the figures of saints.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:21.560 --> 02:24:26.000
And these circles are full of&nbsp;
precious stones. To put it briefly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:26.560 --> 02:24:31.760
this is the richest and loveliest&nbsp;
palace that there be in the whole world.

02:24:35.440 --> 02:24:41.879
Odorich also records that many still remembered&nbsp;
the war with the Mongol Yuan dynasty of China&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:42.400 --> 02:24:45.320
now nearly 30 years in the past.

02:24:47.960 --> 02:24:55.040
The great Khn of Cath who is the sovereign&nbsp;
emperor of all the Tartas has often made war&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:55.040 --> 02:25:01.120
with this king and often brought battle to&nbsp;
him. But this king has always defeated him.

02:25:04.200 --> 02:25:11.280
But despite the glowing report of Odorich and the&nbsp;
ongoing help of the elephant general Gaja Mada,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:11.920 --> 02:25:14.600
Jay Negra's reign was a poor one.

02:25:18.760 --> 02:25:25.040
He was so insecure in his rule that he at one&nbsp;
point seems to have considered marrying two of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:25.040 --> 02:25:33.800
his own sisters named Diar Wyatt and Diar Gitar&nbsp;
to ensure that no rivals could arise from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:33.800 --> 02:25:43.200
royal line. When opposition to this plan exploded&nbsp;
in the court and their mother loudly protested,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:43.200 --> 02:25:51.080
King Jane gave up on this idea. But he still&nbsp;
forbade his sisters from marrying anyone else.

02:25:59.520 --> 02:26:04.201
King Genegra had two sisters&nbsp;
born of the same mother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:05.120 --> 02:26:09.760
He did not permit them to be married to&nbsp;
others, wishing to keep them for himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:10.840 --> 02:26:19.361
Therefore, no prince dared remain openly at&nbsp;
Majapahit. Any man who appeared was driven away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:19.360 --> 02:26:25.519
lest he seek their hands, and the other&nbsp;
nobles kept themselves hidden in fear.

02:26:32.040 --> 02:26:33.040
The

02:26:33.040 --> 02:26:39.440
Genegra confined these two princesses for&nbsp;
years in the women's quarters of the palace&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:40.040 --> 02:26:47.240
and prevented anyone from seeing&nbsp;
them. It was a microcosm of a rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:47.240 --> 02:26:53.120
that seems to have been at every turn&nbsp;
paranoid, petty, and authoritarian.

02:26:55.680 --> 02:27:00.361
Over the course of his reign, the&nbsp;
kingdom of Majapahit would falter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:01.840 --> 02:27:08.401
The island of Bali, just a short hop over&nbsp;
the Sunda Strait, slipped out of his control&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:08.400 --> 02:27:14.679
around this time. And people must have feared&nbsp;
that the whole kingdom would now disintegrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:15.320 --> 02:27:23.000
and the land would return to destructive&nbsp;
civil war. But that was not to be the case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:24.600 --> 02:27:31.080
That's because of a woman who had made&nbsp;
her name standing up to King Gygra,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:31.760 --> 02:27:38.361
the mother of the two imprisoned&nbsp;
princesses. A woman named Dia Guyatry.

02:27:53.240 --> 02:28:01.240
Diar was a title that in old Javanese was given&nbsp;
to princes and princesses of the royal line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:02.120 --> 02:28:09.559
And diaratri had been the youngest daughter&nbsp;
of king kertonagara the last king of singari.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:11.240 --> 02:28:17.240
She had also been the most senior wife&nbsp;
of the founder of Majaet Raden Vagaya&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:17.920 --> 02:28:23.840
and all her life she had been a quiet&nbsp;
force behind the lives of these great men.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:25.640 --> 02:28:30.960
As a girl, she had watched her father&nbsp;
brand the face of the Mongol envoys&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:30.960 --> 02:28:37.040
and then saw her home of Singer Sari&nbsp;
destroyed by the rebellion of Ja Katang.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:38.360 --> 02:28:44.000
She had fled through the rice fields to&nbsp;
safety while her home burned behind her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:46.360 --> 02:28:50.320
As a young woman, she had watched&nbsp;
the war with the Mongols unfold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:50.880 --> 02:28:57.880
then seen her husband found the kingdom of&nbsp;
Majapahit, which now stood as his lasting legacy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:59.120 --> 02:29:06.080
By now, she was a widow in her 50s and had&nbsp;
retired to become a Buddhist nun in a forest&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:06.080 --> 02:29:13.640
monastery on the slope of a nearby mountain. But&nbsp;
she was still known by the title Raja Putney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:14.360 --> 02:29:21.679
a word which means wife of kings and which&nbsp;
gave her the prestige of a royal matriarch.

02:29:23.920 --> 02:29:31.640
The bumbling king Gienegra was her late&nbsp;
husband's son by another less senior wife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:32.400 --> 02:29:39.000
And we can only imagine how Diar Guyatri&nbsp;
felt now watching from her monastery&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:39.000 --> 02:29:46.720
as he veered the empire from crisis to crisis&nbsp;
and kept her two daughters under lock and key.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:49.000 --> 02:29:55.760
She must have feared that the legacy that she and&nbsp;
Raden Vaga had built was about to be destroyed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:56.880 --> 02:30:00.080
But she refused to let that happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:02.360 --> 02:30:08.000
to help her free her daughters, reclaim&nbsp;
the kingdom, and put it back on course.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:08.560 --> 02:30:15.241
This royal matriarch would enlist the help of the&nbsp;
man who had recently been rising in prominence&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:15.240 --> 02:30:23.400
in the kingdom of Majapet. The raging elephant&nbsp;
who had become indispensable to the feckless king.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:24.480 --> 02:30:28.919
The soldier and statesman who&nbsp;
went by the name Gaja Mada.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:30.920 --> 02:30:44.640
In the year 1328, it seems they&nbsp;
took their chance. According

02:30:44.640 --> 02:30:49.401
to the official story related in&nbsp;
the royal chronicle, the paraton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:49.920 --> 02:30:54.920
that year, King Gienegra was suffering&nbsp;
from an unpleasant condition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:54.920 --> 02:31:02.920
a painful boil or swelling of some kind. And&nbsp;
when he summoned his doctor, a man named Tanka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:02.920 --> 02:31:09.800
he apparently forgot that he had previously&nbsp;
insulted or taken advantage of this man's wife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:11.120 --> 02:31:18.680
Enraged at this, the doctor Tanka took&nbsp;
his revenge. As the paraton records,

02:31:26.840 --> 02:31:35.000
Tanka's wife spread word that the king had treated&nbsp;
her dishonorably. Tanka was summoned before Gaja.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:36.760 --> 02:31:43.481
At that time, King Genegra was suffering from&nbsp;
a swelling and could not leave his chambers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:44.640 --> 02:31:49.840
Tanka was ordered to perform an operation&nbsp;
with a sharp lancet and he approached beside&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:49.840 --> 02:31:55.760
the royal bed. Tanka stabbed the king's&nbsp;
boil once, then again with the blade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:56.440 --> 02:32:02.919
but the weapon would not burst it for the king&nbsp;
wore around his neck an amulet of protection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:04.200 --> 02:32:11.800
Then the king was asked to set this aside. He&nbsp;
took off the charm and laid it beside the bed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:13.000 --> 02:32:17.760
Tanka stabbed him again and this&nbsp;
time the blade burst the boil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:19.040 --> 02:32:25.680
but Tanka continued stabbing him and he did not&nbsp;
stop until the king died there upon the bed.

02:32:39.480 --> 02:32:45.679
The doctor turned assassin Tanka&nbsp;
was swiftly killed by Gaja Mada&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:45.680 --> 02:32:51.241
which meant perhaps conveniently that his&nbsp;
side of the story could never be told.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:52.720 --> 02:32:57.960
As historians, we can never actually&nbsp;
know what happened in that room.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:57.960 --> 02:33:04.280
But the slightly fanciful story involving&nbsp;
a magical amulet and a vengeful doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:04.280 --> 02:33:11.880
has left many historians wondering if the&nbsp;
true story wasn't a simpler one that Gajamada&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:11.880 --> 02:33:20.401
and the Daaja queen Dariatry had simply plotted&nbsp;
together to kill the king framing the poor doctor.

02:33:22.880 --> 02:33:29.481
Whether they had planned the whole thing or just&nbsp;
seized the opportunity on a stroke of good luck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:30.400 --> 02:33:39.000
Gajamada and the Daaja matriarch Diaratri went on&nbsp;
to seize control of the kingdom. And it seems King&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:39.000 --> 02:33:46.760
Genegra was sufficiently disliked that few people&nbsp;
were all that concerned about what might actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:46.760 --> 02:33:55.520
have happened to him. But the royal matriarch&nbsp;
and the general prime minister faced a problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:57.400 --> 02:34:03.120
Gaja Mada himself had risen from humble&nbsp;
beginnings. And as a commoner, he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:03.120 --> 02:34:11.160
absolutely no legitimacy to rule under Javanese&nbsp;
law. And while Dioratri was officially the next in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:11.160 --> 02:34:18.480
line to the throne, she had already taken a vow to&nbsp;
become a Buddhist nun, a position that prevented&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:18.480 --> 02:34:26.759
her from holding any positions of earthly power.&nbsp;
And she had no sons who might take the throne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:28.840 --> 02:34:33.840
In fact, the histories record that she&nbsp;
had only given birth to her two daughters&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:34.840 --> 02:34:39.800
who were now allowed to leave the&nbsp;
palace for the first time in years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:41.280 --> 02:34:46.880
The Desha Varana describes these&nbsp;
women in conventionally poetic terms.

02:34:54.080 --> 02:35:00.640
And then the king giant Nagara was left&nbsp;
behind as king of Maja with his princesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:00.640 --> 02:35:06.441
his youngest sisters who were the daughters&nbsp;
of the worthy Raja Patney the incomparable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:07.400 --> 02:35:10.839
They were both of the greatest beauty&nbsp;
surpassing the women of heaven.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:11.840 --> 02:35:16.000
The elder was princess of Jana and&nbsp;
the younger ruled as princess of Da.

02:35:25.160 --> 02:35:34.840
The Raja soon appointed her young daughter&nbsp;
Diar Git to rule as queen in her place.

02:35:40.760 --> 02:35:44.800
Verily in the shaka year moon arrows two form&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:44.800 --> 02:35:51.000
the princess of jwana succeeded in maja pahit&nbsp;
as queen. It was the disposition of the great&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:51.000 --> 02:35:56.640
raja patney that she should be the auspicious&nbsp;
leader of the world distinguished from others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:57.520 --> 02:36:00.800
It was she who made her ruler&nbsp;
and watched over all her affairs.

02:36:09.840 --> 02:36:15.080
The princess soon took to the throne&nbsp;
and with her mother watching over her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:15.640 --> 02:36:24.680
she would be a far more capable ruler than her&nbsp;
feckless brother. She would take a royal name that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:24.680 --> 02:36:34.520
meant the exalted goddess of the three worlds who&nbsp;
the glory of Vishnu radiates. Tribuana vija tuna

02:36:45.640 --> 02:36:51.120
tribuana was her mother's daughter.&nbsp;
She was a natural stateswoman&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:51.120 --> 02:36:56.720
and was able to stabilize the kingdom after the&nbsp;
period of poor leadership that had preceded her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:58.040 --> 02:37:02.800
and she seems to have learned from the&nbsp;
mistakes of both her father and brother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:03.800 --> 02:37:09.520
Early in her reign, she faced a rebellion&nbsp;
by two men named Sardang and Keta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:10.080 --> 02:37:16.440
and a conflict arose in her court over which of&nbsp;
her generals would lead her army into battle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:17.920 --> 02:37:22.680
These kinds of political disputes&nbsp;
had plagued the last two kings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:23.280 --> 02:37:32.000
but Tribuana resolved it in a simple way. Rather&nbsp;
than show favor to one general over the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:32.000 --> 02:37:37.440
she simply led the army&nbsp;
herself. As the desana describes

02:37:44.960 --> 02:37:49.720
in Shaka fire's arrow sons,&nbsp;
their enemies were destroyed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:50.320 --> 02:37:56.920
Sadeng and Kita were defeated, overcome by her&nbsp;
personal retinue. Further in the Shaka year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:56.920 --> 02:38:03.800
arrows, seasons, eyes, naval. The king of Bali&nbsp;
was evil and base. He was attacked with an army,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:03.800 --> 02:38:10.160
broken and completely crushed. Every kind of&nbsp;
evildoer was fearful and made off quickly.

02:38:18.040 --> 02:38:24.200
Tribuana's early successes on the&nbsp;
battlefield gave her a taste for conquest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:24.960 --> 02:38:31.920
And over her rule, this queen of Java would&nbsp;
embark on a massive expansion of Majapahit&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:32.440 --> 02:38:36.400
to spread its influence all over the archipelago.

02:38:38.640 --> 02:38:45.640
To embark on this mission, she appointed as her&nbsp;
Maha Party or prime minister the general who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:45.640 --> 02:38:52.201
done more than perhaps anyone else to keep the&nbsp;
young kingdom together and who had worked with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:52.200 --> 02:39:00.679
her mother to depose the previous king. This&nbsp;
was the rampaging elephant himself, Gaja Mada.

02:39:05.360 --> 02:39:12.960
By all accounts, Gajamada rose to this&nbsp;
challenge. Spurred on by his warlike queen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:12.960 --> 02:39:20.840
the Paraton records him swearing a remarkable&nbsp;
oath of conquest before the entire royal court.

02:39:25.440 --> 02:39:34.400
Gaja Mada swore, "Until I have conquered the&nbsp;
entire Nusantara archipelago, I will eat no spices&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:34.400 --> 02:39:40.599
nor take any rest. If I have conquered&nbsp;
the Gorum Islands and the island of Saram,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:40.600 --> 02:39:48.520
the kingdom of Tanjang Pura, the kingdoms&nbsp;
of Haru and Pahang, the Don Pu regency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:48.520 --> 02:39:56.041
the island of Bali, the lands of Sunda along&nbsp;
with the Tumi and Palem Bang of Sumatra.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:56.040 --> 02:40:06.400
Then and only then will I eat&nbsp;
spices and take my rest. Over

02:40:06.400 --> 02:40:12.920
the next three decades, Gaja would lead&nbsp;
the armies of Majapit across the seas&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:13.680 --> 02:40:18.720
and would bring ever more lands under&nbsp;
the influence and control of the empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:20.160 --> 02:40:27.480
The Italian traveler Ludovviko Deva describes&nbsp;
the weapons of the Javanese that he saw when&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:27.480 --> 02:40:37.000
he visited sometime later around 1505. They carry&nbsp;
bows and the greater part darts of cane. Some also&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:37.000 --> 02:40:42.600
use blowpipes with which they throw poison darts&nbsp;
and they throw them with the mouth and however&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:42.600 --> 02:40:51.600
little they draw blood, the wounded person dies.&nbsp;
The 12th century Javanese poem, the Bulman Taka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:52.120 --> 02:40:59.000
describes an idealized army marching to battle and&nbsp;
gives a sense for what it would have been like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:59.000 --> 02:41:06.960
see the forces of Gajamada landing on the shores&nbsp;
of each island in turn and preparing for war.

02:41:09.920 --> 02:41:17.320
Their weapons were spears and lances, and there&nbsp;
were also thousands of bows. Then came the riding&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:17.320 --> 02:41:24.639
elephants, and their weapons were spears, lances,&nbsp;
javelins, and clubs. Many heroes were mounted on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:24.640 --> 02:41:32.441
horses. Skilled riders in horseback combat. The&nbsp;
spears and very sharp knives when worldled glowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:32.440 --> 02:41:40.759
brightly. Men came bearing lances and shields,&nbsp;
and others with slingshots. Their casings shone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:40.760 --> 02:41:47.240
wrapped in silk and gilded, and their various&nbsp;
jackets were like clouds traveling over the earth.

02:41:55.240 --> 02:42:02.960
The Majapet forces of Queen Tribuana Vijay&nbsp;
Tungadevi crossed the sea to the east&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:02.960 --> 02:42:11.720
and reconquered the small island of Bali&nbsp;
in 1343. After that they went northwest&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:11.720 --> 02:42:18.600
and absorbed most of the coastal regions of the&nbsp;
larger island of Sumatra into her growing empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:20.880 --> 02:42:26.720
Other kingdoms were incorporated peacefully&nbsp;
into the empire as tributary subjects&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:27.240 --> 02:42:32.679
such as the large island of Sulai&nbsp;
whose name means iron island&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:32.680 --> 02:42:38.201
on account of the high quality iron it&nbsp;
produced and exported around the region.

02:42:41.920 --> 02:42:48.240
The later poem, the Desha Varna, lists&nbsp;
at length all of the island territories&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:48.240 --> 02:42:54.480
that were either conquered by Maja Pahit or&nbsp;
agreed to pay it tribute without a fight.

02:42:57.360 --> 02:43:02.480
The islands and lesser islands in the first place&nbsp;
coming under the territory of the Malay islands&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:02.480 --> 02:43:07.799
are Jambi and Palamang, Keritang,&nbsp;
Tiba and Dharmasa. Following&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:08.600 --> 02:43:17.481
Candice, Kawas, Manang, Kabwa, Sak, Rekan,&nbsp;
Campa and Pane, Campe, Haru and Mandahiling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:17.480 --> 02:43:24.519
Tumihang, Pac and Barat, Luas and Samudra,&nbsp;
as well as Lamuri, Batan, Lampong and Barus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:24.520 --> 02:43:30.000
Those are the main ones among the Malay islands.&nbsp;
All these countries are subject and obedient.

02:43:33.800 --> 02:43:40.120
The great queen Tribuana Vijay&nbsp;
Tungadevi would rule for 22 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:40.120 --> 02:43:48.080
and her reign saw the rise of Majapay&nbsp;
to a true or an empire of the sea.

02:43:51.640 --> 02:43:55.800
But she must have known that&nbsp;
her reign couldn't last forever.

02:44:08.960 --> 02:44:15.481
In 1350, her mother, the great&nbsp;
royal matriarch Diar Guyry died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:16.280 --> 02:44:21.679
most likely in her forest monastery&nbsp;
after a long and accomplished life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:23.680 --> 02:44:29.080
Her daughter Tribuana had been ruling&nbsp;
ostensibly on behalf of her mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:29.920 --> 02:44:36.441
and it seems that once the old woman died,&nbsp;
her situation would also become untenable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:37.880 --> 02:44:44.560
But luckily, by this time she had had a son&nbsp;
who was now the first in the royal line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:45.240 --> 02:44:53.480
and he had just come of age at 16&nbsp;
years old. His name was Hyam Wuruk.

02:45:00.000 --> 02:45:04.240
When this young prince Hayam&nbsp;
Wuruk came to the throne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:04.240 --> 02:45:08.639
he would have been crowned with&nbsp;
enormous ceremony and celebration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:09.960 --> 02:45:17.560
The historian Herald Vanderinda describes the&nbsp;
rituals associated with a Javanese coronation.

02:45:20.120 --> 02:45:26.120
At sunset on the eve of the first day of the&nbsp;
first month of the year, priests entered the inner&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:26.120 --> 02:45:34.160
pavilion of the royal palace followed by acolytes&nbsp;
chanting sacred Sanskrit texts. King Haim Wuruk&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:34.160 --> 02:45:41.000
sat on a throne in the amazing pavilion where&nbsp;
mattedhaired rishi hermits and religious leaders&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:41.000 --> 02:45:49.200
sprinkled holy water over him to wash away the&nbsp;
evils of the past. As darkness descended, oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:49.200 --> 02:45:57.960
lamps were lit around the palace. A spectacular&nbsp;
sight. At the striking of a gong, Gaja Mada&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:57.960 --> 02:46:04.360
led the senior ministers to a large pavilion in&nbsp;
front of the palace gates before a growing crowd.

02:46:12.880 --> 02:46:20.720
Hayamuruk was a multi-talented king. He was&nbsp;
apparently an accomplished singer and dancer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:20.720 --> 02:46:28.960
a lover of shadow puppet plays. The poet of the&nbsp;
Desha Wanana describes the auspicious omens that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:28.960 --> 02:46:36.320
took place upon the birth of this prince as the&nbsp;
island's great volcanoes rumbled their approval.

02:46:39.200 --> 02:46:45.400
At his birth in the Shaki year, season's arrow&nbsp;
sons, the prince was already destined to be king.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:46.160 --> 02:46:51.559
The signs of his miraculous and extraordinary&nbsp;
nature were that the earth quaked and rumbled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:51.560 --> 02:46:57.600
There was a rain of ash. Thunder and lightning&nbsp;
zigzagged through the sky. Mount Kampud erupted&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:57.600 --> 02:47:03.560
and the wretched evildoers around it were&nbsp;
annihilated and died without a gasp. Such is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:03.560 --> 02:47:09.600
nature of his reign that the whole of the land of&nbsp;
Java has been humbled and bows down in submission.

02:47:13.400 --> 02:47:15.240
Like her mother before her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:15.240 --> 02:47:21.040
Tribuana did not completely vanish from political&nbsp;
life when she stepped away from her throne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:22.320 --> 02:47:29.480
Throughout the rest of her life, she would act as&nbsp;
a counselor and advisor for her son, and together&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:29.480 --> 02:47:36.400
they would usher in what is widely regarded&nbsp;
as the golden age of the empire of Majapet.

02:47:38.720 --> 02:47:46.160
When Hyam Wooduk came to the throne, he inherited&nbsp;
a capital city of no little magnificence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:47.560 --> 02:47:53.880
Over the preceding decades, it had been the site&nbsp;
of constant construction and now bloomed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:53.880 --> 02:48:01.680
palaces and temples complete with pillared&nbsp;
audience halls and pavilions. In this era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:01.680 --> 02:48:09.160
all roads led to Majapahit and its status as&nbsp;
a crossroads of Java wasn't just a metaphor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:10.400 --> 02:48:15.080
The epic poem Desa Vanana&nbsp;
describes the royal palace.

02:48:18.520 --> 02:48:22.401
Let us describe the layout of&nbsp;
the all inspiring royal palace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:22.400 --> 02:48:27.080
Its wall is of red brick. It goes&nbsp;
right around and is thick and high.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:27.080 --> 02:48:31.640
On the western side, the palace mouth faces&nbsp;
onto the great square in the midst of deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:31.640 --> 02:48:36.880
encircling with booty trees in rows and&nbsp;
neatly shaped, all thick with festoons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:37.400 --> 02:48:43.519
On the north is the ceremonial gate, splendid&nbsp;
and extraordinary. Its doors of iron adorned&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:43.520 --> 02:48:49.680
with countless designs. To the south of this is&nbsp;
the crossroads, otherworldly in its elegance.

02:48:53.280 --> 02:49:00.400
From these crossroads, great highways snaked&nbsp;
off into the country in all directions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:01.880 --> 02:49:07.960
It was part of the usual ceremony of kingship&nbsp;
that the king of Majapahit would routinely&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:07.960 --> 02:49:15.840
make journeys around the whole of Java, meeting&nbsp;
his citizens and seeing the sites of the land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:16.440 --> 02:49:24.400
visiting villages, holy shrines, and reminding&nbsp;
vassal kingdoms about who was in charge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:26.520 --> 02:49:33.241
It was on one of these journeys that&nbsp;
King Hayam Wuruk brought his court poet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:33.760 --> 02:49:40.321
a man named Propanka who would go on to&nbsp;
be the author of the poem Desavanana.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:41.920 --> 02:49:48.481
Propanka speaks of the honor in his poem&nbsp;
describing himself in the third person.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:50.120 --> 02:49:55.880
In the Shaka moon snake son in the month&nbsp;
of Badra Pada at the fullness of the moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:55.880 --> 02:50:00.920
King Hayamuruk went on a journey to Lama&nbsp;
to take note of all there was to see.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:01.520 --> 02:50:07.441
All the princes of Java with their wives again&nbsp;
went with the king with servants and vehicles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:07.440 --> 02:50:10.000
Officials and officers from the whole of Majapahit&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:10.760 --> 02:50:15.720
and in particular the king's priests and court&nbsp;
poets accompanied him. So this man by the pen&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:15.720 --> 02:50:20.441
name of Propanka joined in the pursuit of&nbsp;
beauty attending at the feet of the king.

02:50:24.240 --> 02:50:30.320
According to his own account, Propanka&nbsp;
seems to have embarked on writing the desana&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:30.880 --> 02:50:37.840
because he felt unable to write the kind of poetry&nbsp;
that the more senior court poets were producing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:38.560 --> 02:50:45.560
usually heroic epics about the Hindu gods&nbsp;
as he describes in the following extract.

02:50:47.880 --> 02:50:52.960
His motive was after all to follow the king&nbsp;
wherever he went in the hope of being favored.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:52.960 --> 02:50:56.520
But he still does not know how to&nbsp;
achieve poetic effects and is most&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:56.520 --> 02:51:01.760
unlikely to succeed in composing a song on the&nbsp;
writing tablet. And this is the reason why he&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:01.760 --> 02:51:06.281
describes all the places passed through and&nbsp;
sets out their names in order along the road.

02:51:09.920 --> 02:51:16.720
Ironically, the Dash of Varna is today one of&nbsp;
the most famous pieces of literature to survive&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:16.720 --> 02:51:25.560
from the Javanese golden era and it is by far the&nbsp;
most useful to historians. Today, the poem is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:25.560 --> 02:51:33.960
remarkable source that captures a snapshot of life&nbsp;
on Java during the reign of Hyam Wuruk. And for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:33.960 --> 02:51:43.640
all of this, we have the poet Propanka's apparent&nbsp;
inferiority complex to thank. Along their journey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:43.640 --> 02:51:50.401
Propanka watched the king carefully and noted&nbsp;
down the beauty of the land they passed through.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:52.440 --> 02:51:58.559
At Dampar and at Patanjungan, the king relaxed&nbsp;
and made his way along the beach. To the east&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:58.560 --> 02:52:02.960
his path followed the level sands, which was so&nbsp;
firm that the carriages could drive over them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:03.520 --> 02:52:08.880
He made a halt on the banks of a lake thick with&nbsp;
white and red lotuses in flour, and was absorbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:08.880 --> 02:52:13.800
in watching the giant shrimps moving about in&nbsp;
the limpid water, clearly visible in the depths.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:15.840 --> 02:52:19.320
But the royal journey wasn't always an easy one.

02:52:23.280 --> 02:52:30.040
In his text, we get a sense for the challenges&nbsp;
of moving over land during the rainy season.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:32.200 --> 02:52:35.720
The road just at the point where&nbsp;
it runs northward from the sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:35.720 --> 02:52:41.600
over its whole length was rough and narrow. On&nbsp;
top of this, it rained so that the steep places&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:41.600 --> 02:52:45.720
became slippery and some of the carriages&nbsp;
came loose and collided with each other.

02:52:48.840 --> 02:52:54.920
Due to these challenges, travel by road was&nbsp;
largely only possible during the dry season.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:55.920 --> 02:52:59.481
For the rest of the year, goods&nbsp;
were transported by river,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:00.080 --> 02:53:09.760
taking rice and other export crops downstream&nbsp;
to the great port cities on barges. These boats&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:09.760 --> 02:53:16.800
would then sail back up the rivers with luxury&nbsp;
goods imported from China, India, and elsewhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:18.280 --> 02:53:24.800
These port cities were the beating heart of&nbsp;
the Majapahit Empire. And the large numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:24.800 --> 02:53:32.200
of Chinese coins found in their archaeological&nbsp;
sites testify to the booming trade that went on.

02:53:35.640 --> 02:53:40.441
At each of these ports, the sales of&nbsp;
hundreds of ships would be visible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:41.120 --> 02:53:48.480
coming and going with countless trade goods. And&nbsp;
among these, the most lucrative were the spices&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:48.480 --> 02:53:54.519
grown on the islands of the archipelago&nbsp;
that were prized all around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:54.520 --> 02:54:03.520
such as pepper, cardamom, and ginger. But&nbsp;
the rarest of all were cloves and nutmeg.

02:54:12.520 --> 02:54:19.760
Cloves and nutmeg were brought to Majapahit on&nbsp;
large ships that sailed from a small group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:19.760 --> 02:54:27.840
islands which are called the islands of Maluku&nbsp;
and would come to be known as the spice islands.

02:54:31.480 --> 02:54:38.599
While cloves were grown all over these islands,&nbsp;
nutmeg was extremely limited in cultivation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:39.240 --> 02:54:44.240
occurring only on 10 islands in&nbsp;
the tiny archipelago of Banda.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:45.760 --> 02:54:51.600
Wild nutmeg had long been harvested by&nbsp;
the peoples native to the Bander Islands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:52.200 --> 02:54:55.760
but it was a particularly&nbsp;
difficult plant to cultivate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:57.080 --> 02:55:02.640
Nutmeg seeds will fail to germinate if&nbsp;
they aren't planted in soil within a week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:03.200 --> 02:55:09.519
making them difficult to transport by sea.&nbsp;
And the particular environment of volcanic&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:09.520 --> 02:55:15.600
soil that they evolved in means that they are&nbsp;
fragile and difficult to nurture to full size.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:17.840 --> 02:55:22.280
As a result of these difficulties,&nbsp;
the Bander Islanders claimed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:22.280 --> 02:55:28.360
nutmeg was simply a gift of the gods and&nbsp;
could not be cultivated by human hands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:29.480 --> 02:55:34.440
Instead, they would travel to the parts of&nbsp;
the forest where it grew wild to collect&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:34.440 --> 02:55:41.240
its prized seeds. And they kept the location&nbsp;
of these groves a carefully guarded secret.

02:55:43.960 --> 02:55:50.400
But despite its extreme rarity, the fragrant&nbsp;
nutmeg seeds that these islanders harvested&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:50.400 --> 02:55:58.120
traveled all around the world. The spice would&nbsp;
reach Constantinople in the sixth century&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:58.120 --> 02:56:02.599
and instantly became a sought&nbsp;
after commodity and mark of luxury.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:03.600 --> 02:56:11.000
Nutmeg would even be mentioned as a spice to&nbsp;
flavor English ale in Chaucer's Canterbury Tales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:11.520 --> 02:56:17.481
which was written during the time&nbsp;
of the Majapahit Empire. At times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:17.480 --> 02:56:23.559
nutmeg would sell on the other side of the world&nbsp;
for more than an equivalent weight of gold.

02:56:29.200 --> 02:56:34.120
As a result of this global trade,&nbsp;
the rulers of the Bander Islands&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:34.120 --> 02:56:39.760
would come to be known around the&nbsp;
region as the Orurang Kaya or rich men.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:40.560 --> 02:56:46.201
And the entirety of this trade passed&nbsp;
through the major ports of Majapahit.

02:56:48.800 --> 02:56:52.361
To transport these goods&nbsp;
across the seas and oceans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:52.880 --> 02:57:02.800
the sailors of Majapahit voyaged in large vessels&nbsp;
known as Jongs. These were the descendants of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:02.800 --> 02:57:09.401
the Bora Budor ships that had sailed to Madagascar&nbsp;
and Eastern Africa in the preceding centuries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:10.200 --> 02:57:16.120
And by this time, some of these ships had&nbsp;
reportedly grown to phenomenal proportions.

02:57:19.040 --> 02:57:26.000
Reports on the true size of these vessels&nbsp;
vary, but some sources record jongs that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:26.000 --> 02:57:34.320
reached a size of 80 m long. They were built with&nbsp;
layered hulls that made them exceptionally strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:34.880 --> 02:57:41.160
and were able to transport a thousand&nbsp;
soldiers and more than a,000 tons of cargo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:42.280 --> 02:57:47.519
The sight of these massive jongs&nbsp;
astonished European explorers when&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:47.520 --> 02:57:56.241
they arrived in the archipelago in the 16th&nbsp;
century. One Portuguese admiral and explorer&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:56.240 --> 02:58:03.400
Alphonso de Alba wrote about encountering&nbsp;
one of these enormous vessels in 1511.

02:58:06.000 --> 02:58:10.720
The whole fre went back along the coast&nbsp;
in the same order as they had first come.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:11.640 --> 02:58:14.760
And when they were in the&nbsp;
latitude of the powder island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:14.760 --> 02:58:19.080
they cighted two very large&nbsp;
junks and gave chase to them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:19.720 --> 02:58:26.361
One of these, which was from India, surrendered&nbsp;
immediately. But the other junk would not do so.

02:58:31.240 --> 02:58:38.760
The Portuguese gave chase to the massive Javanese&nbsp;
ship and made several attempts to board it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:39.360 --> 02:58:47.839
but these were all unsuccessful. The Europeans&nbsp;
even opened fire on the Jong with their cannons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:47.840 --> 02:58:54.401
but found that the three or four layers of wood&nbsp;
with which the Javanese built their ships simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:54.400 --> 02:59:03.799
absorbed the cannon fire and the vast vessel&nbsp;
sailed on. In a letter written sometime later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:04.320 --> 02:59:12.559
one of the Portuguese captains, Ferno Perez de&nbsp;
Andrada, describes the massive Javanese ship.

02:59:16.120 --> 02:59:22.840
The junk was the largest seen by men of these&nbsp;
parts so far. It carried a thousand fighting men&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:22.840 --> 02:59:29.080
on board. And your lordship can believe me that&nbsp;
it was an amazing thing to see because beside it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:29.080 --> 02:59:35.840
our own vessel, the Anuniada, did not look like&nbsp;
a ship at all. We attacked it with bombards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:35.840 --> 02:59:39.800
but even the shots of the largest cannon&nbsp;
did not pierce it below the waterline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:40.680 --> 02:59:44.640
It was so monstrous that no&nbsp;
man had ever seen the like.

02:59:48.720 --> 02:59:55.720
Eventually, the smaller and more maneuverable&nbsp;
Portuguese ships were able to disable the larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:55.720 --> 03:00:04.080
Jong by breaking its two massive rudders. This&nbsp;
finally brought the men on board to surrender.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:05.320 --> 03:00:10.200
The Europeans were so impressed with&nbsp;
the size of these Javanese Jongs&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:10.200 --> 03:00:16.920
that they sailed one of them all the way back home&nbsp;
where it was used for a time as a warship in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:16.920 --> 03:00:25.880
fleet guarding the Gibralta Strait. Although&nbsp;
we have little reliable data on when the ships&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:25.880 --> 03:00:32.441
of the Javanese reached such a massive scale, it&nbsp;
is reasonable to assume that they were at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:32.440 --> 03:00:40.480
approaching that size during the reign of Hyam&nbsp;
Wuruk when the power of Majapahit was at its peak.

03:00:43.600 --> 03:00:49.560
These enormous ships would have been the&nbsp;
crucial backbone of the Majapahit Empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:50.080 --> 03:00:57.320
with their enormous capacity for cargo and&nbsp;
their ability to transport thousands of men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:57.320 --> 03:01:05.440
horses, and even elephants from island to island&nbsp;
wherever they were needed. And of course they&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:05.440 --> 03:01:13.440
would have made a powerful statement about the&nbsp;
power and prestige of the empire of Majapahit.

03:01:16.160 --> 03:01:22.680
By now, the Majapahit sphere of influence&nbsp;
stretched over the sea for thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:22.680 --> 03:01:29.600
kilometers, and they had monopolized the&nbsp;
lucrative spice routes from the Malaku Islands&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:29.600 --> 03:01:34.361
so that their wealth would have swelled&nbsp;
to previously unimagined heights.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:36.360 --> 03:01:44.320
Over this period, Majapahit began to take on the&nbsp;
form of what has been called a Mandala Empire.

03:01:52.960 --> 03:02:00.481
In Sanskrit, the word mandala means circle&nbsp;
and it has come to describe a style of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:00.480 --> 03:02:06.240
traditional geometric pattern used to&nbsp;
represent a kind of map of the universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:07.760 --> 03:02:14.321
reflected in the shapes of the Buddhist temples&nbsp;
at Seu and Borau Budur. These are designed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:14.320 --> 03:02:23.120
help an adherent achieve a state of meditation.&nbsp;
Although the forms of these mandalas differ, many&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:23.120 --> 03:02:30.160
show the sacred mountain meu at the center of the&nbsp;
world, sometimes surrounded by the world ocean.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:32.280 --> 03:02:39.240
Around this is a square palace with four gates&nbsp;
and then a circle representing the continents&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:39.240 --> 03:02:45.080
outside it. The heavens and the world of&nbsp;
the gods forming the outside of the pattern.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:46.760 --> 03:02:53.361
The mandala presents the vision of a world&nbsp;
ordered in a strict hierarchy radiating out from&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:53.360 --> 03:03:00.599
the center. And so it's no surprise it has long&nbsp;
been used as a way of visualizing royal power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:02.160 --> 03:03:10.120
As early as the 4th century BC, the Indian writer&nbsp;
Killilia in his work the Arth Shastra described&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:10.120 --> 03:03:17.640
what he called the Raja Mandala or Mandala&nbsp;
of kings in which a powerful ruler will bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:17.640 --> 03:03:26.161
other less powerful ones under his control. This&nbsp;
idea has become popular among modern historians&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:26.160 --> 03:03:31.840
seeking to understand how Southeast Asian&nbsp;
states of this time were organized and there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:31.840 --> 03:03:37.120
some evidence that this is also how the Javanese&nbsp;
of the time would have thought of their world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:39.080 --> 03:03:47.040
The epic poem the desavan describes the structure&nbsp;
of the Majapayah Empire like the solar system&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:47.040 --> 03:03:51.680
with power radiating out from the&nbsp;
center like the rays of the sun.

03:03:59.720 --> 03:04:05.281
The royal compound at Maja the incomparable is&nbsp;
as sun and the moon and the courtyards of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:05.280 --> 03:04:10.240
many quarters each with its own particular&nbsp;
charm are rays of light in relation to it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:10.800 --> 03:04:16.560
Like the stars and planets are the various&nbsp;
remaining cities. Daha the foremost and the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:16.560 --> 03:04:21.760
islands and all the surrounding countries which&nbsp;
seek refuge and in numbers come into the presence.

03:04:31.680 --> 03:04:39.241
It's still an open question exactly how much&nbsp;
power Majapahit exerted over its vassal states.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:40.800 --> 03:04:49.120
Some empires like the Roman Empire ruled with&nbsp;
direct control. When they conquered a new area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:49.120 --> 03:04:56.000
they erased previous forms of government and&nbsp;
social structures. and imposed their own on&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:56.000 --> 03:05:02.920
absorbed territories. But we don't see much&nbsp;
evidence of that in the case of Majapahit.

03:05:04.880 --> 03:05:10.401
Subordinate kings and rulers on the&nbsp;
outer islands certainly paid symbolic&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:10.400 --> 03:05:18.480
homage to the king in Majapah. They likely&nbsp;
provided an agreed upon tribute each year&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:18.480 --> 03:05:24.799
and a certain number of soldiers and ships for&nbsp;
any military action undertaken by the empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:25.840 --> 03:05:31.640
But beyond that, it's likely that the lords of&nbsp;
each island and tributary kingdom continued to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:31.640 --> 03:05:40.201
exert more or less total control over their own&nbsp;
affairs. This arrangement suited everyone well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:41.800 --> 03:05:48.600
Cultural identities on each individual island&nbsp;
were relatively distinct and deeply entrenched.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:49.560 --> 03:05:55.720
Local languages and customs were different on each&nbsp;
island, and few people around the region wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:55.720 --> 03:06:03.281
to be directly ruled by a Javanese governor or to&nbsp;
have Javanese troops stationed in their hometowns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:04.760 --> 03:06:10.680
And for the Majapayah, appointing governors and&nbsp;
stationing troops to put down rebellions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:10.680 --> 03:06:19.120
uprisings would have been an unwelcome expense.&nbsp;
The Mandala structure was cheap and efficient.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:20.360 --> 03:06:26.679
While times were good and the empire was strong,&nbsp;
it ensured a maximum level of compliance and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:26.680 --> 03:06:34.241
cooperation from subjects with the lightest&nbsp;
possible touch and allowed everyone to profit&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:34.240 --> 03:06:39.240
from the great traffic of trade that&nbsp;
flowed along the great arteries of the sea.

03:06:42.760 --> 03:06:49.481
From the center of the mandala in Majapahit,&nbsp;
fleets of Jong ships sailed back and forth&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:49.480 --> 03:06:55.080
among the islands, bringing trade and&nbsp;
tribute back to its booming ports.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:57.160 --> 03:07:06.599
But as with any empire, sometimes its subjects&nbsp;
had to be kept in line, as the Deshavan describes.

03:07:15.840 --> 03:07:20.640
The other countries, all that were under the&nbsp;
care of Java, obeyed the king's commands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:20.640 --> 03:07:26.600
and each was true in what it did. Furthermore,&nbsp;
any that transgressed his commands was attacked&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:26.600 --> 03:07:30.720
and wiped out completely by the squadrons&nbsp;
of the various valiant naval officers.

03:07:47.480 --> 03:07:55.959
One such example occurred in the year 1375&nbsp;
when one of the subject kings of the empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:55.960 --> 03:07:59.600
seemed to get ideas above his station.

03:08:09.880 --> 03:08:15.760
The troublesome king in question was a&nbsp;
man named Aditya Vamman who had been put&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:15.760 --> 03:08:23.160
in power by Majapahit on the large island of&nbsp;
Sumatra, a short hop over the sea from Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:25.040 --> 03:08:32.960
Aditioan was something of a narcissist. He was&nbsp;
fond of commissioning massive statues of himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:32.960 --> 03:08:37.040
larger than any that have been&nbsp;
uncovered of other kings in the region,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:37.640 --> 03:08:43.120
and over the preceding years he had&nbsp;
begun to act in an insubordinate manner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:45.080 --> 03:08:50.920
At one point, he had a stone tablet&nbsp;
inscribed that showed a list of kings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:51.600 --> 03:08:59.441
and he had the insulence to place&nbsp;
his own name above King Hyam Woods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:59.440 --> 03:09:05.240
This all would have caused rumbles of&nbsp;
constonnation at the court of Majapahed.

03:09:07.280 --> 03:09:15.120
But Aditio would go too far when he sent his&nbsp;
own envoys to the court of the emperor of China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:15.760 --> 03:09:23.640
bypassing his king completely. One&nbsp;
Javanese epic poem, the Parata Yuda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:24.160 --> 03:09:30.559
describes the rage of the demon king Ravena,&nbsp;
upon getting news of the death of his sons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:31.120 --> 03:09:38.040
But we might imagine it a fitting description of&nbsp;
Hayam Wuruk's reaction when he heard that Aditya&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:38.040 --> 03:09:44.080
had been going over his head and making&nbsp;
overtures to China without consulting him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:46.360 --> 03:09:53.599
The monarch was furiously enraged. He gnored&nbsp;
his mustaches, his countenance was inflamed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:53.600 --> 03:09:57.201
and his bosom red as the waari flower.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:57.760 --> 03:10:05.321
Sweat exuded from every paw. The angles of his&nbsp;
mouth trembled. His eyelashes stuck together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:05.320 --> 03:10:11.200
He vaunted. He called aloud. He challenged&nbsp;
all his enemies to meet him at once.

03:10:14.680 --> 03:10:19.120
Hayam Wuruk summoned the&nbsp;
Chinese ambassador to Sumatra&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:19.120 --> 03:10:26.400
and after lambasting him apparently had the man&nbsp;
beheaded and his head sent back to China in a bag.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:28.800 --> 03:10:36.240
In perhaps a sign of the power of Majapahit at&nbsp;
this time the Chinese didn't respond in kind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:37.440 --> 03:10:42.559
Instead, they bashfully apologized for&nbsp;
their mistake and would eventually send&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:42.560 --> 03:10:49.840
a gift of fine fabrics and a batch of paper&nbsp;
money to the Javanese court to make amends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:51.080 --> 03:10:56.559
But from the vain king Aditioan,&nbsp;
no apology would suffice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:58.080 --> 03:11:06.040
King Hayam Wuruk amassed his army, as many as&nbsp;
300 warships and according to the chronicles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:06.040 --> 03:11:14.640
200,000 soldiers to crush this upstart king.&nbsp;
And although we don't hear what happened to him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:14.640 --> 03:11:20.880
Aditioan never appears in any other&nbsp;
written records or inscriptions again.

03:11:24.280 --> 03:11:32.480
Now Hayam Wuruk was on the war path. After&nbsp;
crushing Aditioan, the Majapahit fleet&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:32.480 --> 03:11:40.679
rounded on another problematic vassel. This&nbsp;
man ruled the walled settlement of Singapura,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:41.240 --> 03:11:50.519
now the modern city of Singapore, and his&nbsp;
name was Paramevara. After a month-long siege,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:50.520 --> 03:11:56.121
the Majapahed forces broke through the&nbsp;
walls and slaughtered the inhabitants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:57.840 --> 03:12:04.441
To this day, legend has it that the&nbsp;
ironrich reddish clay soil of Singapore&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:04.440 --> 03:12:09.440
was stained that color by the&nbsp;
blood spilled during this massacre.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:11.280 --> 03:12:18.040
Paramea, the king of Singapura, fled&nbsp;
north to the area of the Malaca Strait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:19.120 --> 03:12:25.040
but he would remain a thorn in the&nbsp;
side of Majapah for some time to come.

03:12:41.280 --> 03:12:48.960
The Aditia incident shows that the empire of&nbsp;
Majapahit was held together by trade and diplomacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:49.560 --> 03:12:53.321
and the promise of mutual peace and prosperity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:54.160 --> 03:13:02.120
But it was also very much backed by&nbsp;
the threat of force. Over this period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:02.120 --> 03:13:10.120
the royal capital would flourish. But this&nbsp;
golden age of Majapahit could not last forever&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:10.720 --> 03:13:16.800
and its end would come about after the&nbsp;
death of its last great king Hayam Wuruk.

03:13:31.320 --> 03:13:36.280
King Hayam Wuruk died likely in the year 1389&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:36.840 --> 03:13:41.880
and with him the light began&nbsp;
to leave the court of Majapah.

03:13:44.840 --> 03:13:51.441
The great statesman and general&nbsp;
Gajamada had died 25 years earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:51.440 --> 03:13:59.480
and Hayam Wuruk's mother queen Tribuana Vijay&nbsp;
Tungadevi had also died a decade or so after that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:01.280 --> 03:14:07.840
There now seems to have been something of&nbsp;
a vacuum of talent in the Majapahit court.

03:14:10.680 --> 03:14:16.520
Exacerbating the problem was the&nbsp;
complexity of Javanese succession laws&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:16.520 --> 03:14:22.560
which had developed to deal with the fact that&nbsp;
kings of this time always had multiple wives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:23.560 --> 03:14:30.600
In ideal circumstances, the law would&nbsp;
unambiguously favor one successor over another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:30.600 --> 03:14:34.241
and there would be no confusion&nbsp;
about who would take the throne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:35.360 --> 03:14:41.160
But fate had dealt Hyamuruk a bad&nbsp;
hand when it came to his succession.

03:14:48.240 --> 03:14:53.120
He had failed to have a son by his&nbsp;
principal wife and so his closest&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:53.120 --> 03:15:00.360
legal heir was therefore a woman named Kusumov&nbsp;
Vardini, his daughter by a lower ranking wife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:01.640 --> 03:15:09.880
She had married a man named Vikramordina and so&nbsp;
it seemed likely that he would become king despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:09.880 --> 03:15:18.000
having only married into the royal family. The&nbsp;
Dashaner describes this couple in glowing terms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:20.320 --> 03:15:25.240
They have as daughter Princess Kusuma&nbsp;
Wadani, a blameless young princess&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:25.240 --> 03:15:29.320
who's paid homage in the royal compound&nbsp;
of Cabalan and is of the utmost charm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:30.080 --> 03:15:34.200
Prince Wikramama Wadana will be her proper&nbsp;
match as everyone says they will be like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:34.200 --> 03:15:38.599
god and goddess when they marry later,&nbsp;
bringing happiness to people's hearts.

03:15:41.880 --> 03:15:48.441
But Hayam Wuruk did also have a son&nbsp;
by a secondary less senior wife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:49.600 --> 03:15:57.121
In the histories, he is known only by the title&nbsp;
Bra Vabumi or the lord of a place called Virumi.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:58.960 --> 03:16:06.400
Being a man and the direct descendant of Hyam&nbsp;
Wuruk also gave him a strong claim to the throne&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:06.400 --> 03:16:11.080
despite his mother being of a&nbsp;
lower rank than Kusumo Vines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:12.400 --> 03:16:20.200
And this situation of two people having a&nbsp;
comparable claim was a recipe for disaster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:21.880 --> 03:16:29.840
Hyam Muruk seems to have sensed the danger while&nbsp;
he was still alive. In an attempt to remedy the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:29.840 --> 03:16:36.441
situation and cool off the rivalry between&nbsp;
these two men, he divided the empire into&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:36.440 --> 03:16:44.679
the western court, the capital of Majapet, and the&nbsp;
eastern court, where the lord of Virumi held sway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:46.600 --> 03:16:52.880
The exact details of this arrangement are&nbsp;
unknown, but it was a clear and obvious rift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:52.880 --> 03:17:01.720
now splitting the kingdom's Javanese heartlands&nbsp;
in two. The situation seems to have gotten so bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:01.720 --> 03:17:08.441
that at one point the Chinese chronicle, the&nbsp;
Ming Shilu, notes that the two different courts&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:08.440 --> 03:17:13.759
were each sending their envoys to meet&nbsp;
independently with the emperor's ambassadors.

03:17:16.000 --> 03:17:22.800
Like the rumblings and earthquakes before&nbsp;
a great eruption, the signs of trouble&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:22.800 --> 03:17:29.080
must have been obvious to anyone who was paying&nbsp;
attention. And in the years that followed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:29.080 --> 03:17:35.480
the rift would tear apart the land&nbsp;
of Java and the empire it ruled.

03:17:48.120 --> 03:17:55.000
The name for the conflicts that follows&nbsp;
is found in the chronicle of the Paraton.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:56.240 --> 03:18:03.519
There it is called the regreg, a word&nbsp;
that in Javanese means bit by bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:03.520 --> 03:18:08.680
and can be used to describe something&nbsp;
falling apart piece by piece.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:10.080 --> 03:18:18.080
As we will see, this was a fitting name as the&nbsp;
conflict would bring about the steady coming apart&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:18.080 --> 03:18:24.800
of the Mandala Empire of Majapet that&nbsp;
had held together over the last century.

03:18:27.040 --> 03:18:31.360
The paraton describes the conflict that unfolded.

03:18:38.160 --> 03:18:43.440
They came to blows. There was&nbsp;
division and men died in the division.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:44.760 --> 03:18:51.800
After 3 years, there then arose again the&nbsp;
regreg war. Both sides prepared themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:52.560 --> 03:18:59.760
All the lords asked themselves, "Whom&nbsp;
shall I follow?" Then the fighting began.

03:19:07.000 --> 03:19:15.600
The regreg war would rage for 2 years and over&nbsp;
this time neither side could gain the upper hand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:16.960 --> 03:19:22.200
The para raton describes the back&nbsp;
and forth nature of the fighting.

03:19:26.360 --> 03:19:34.120
First the western palace was defeated. Their lords&nbsp;
were hardpressed. In distress they wished to flee.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:34.840 --> 03:19:42.200
Then they said, "Do not flee yet. Let us fight&nbsp;
them." Then the eastern palace was defeated.

03:19:46.480 --> 03:19:53.879
The strength of the Mandala Empire system was&nbsp;
in how light a hold it exerted on its vassels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:55.080 --> 03:19:59.000
It was a cheap and efficient&nbsp;
way to run an imperial system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:59.520 --> 03:20:06.321
But this strength in times of stability&nbsp;
became a weakness when times got hard.

03:20:11.120 --> 03:20:18.480
For the kings who swore allegiance to Majapay, it&nbsp;
was all too easy when the empire was distracted&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:18.480 --> 03:20:25.759
to simply turn away and quietly stop&nbsp;
paying its fees or sending it troops.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:27.320 --> 03:20:34.759
With the power of Majapahit's fleet and army now&nbsp;
focused entirely on the war between its western&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:34.760 --> 03:20:42.680
and eastern courts, many of its formerly most&nbsp;
loyal subjects simply slipped out of its grasp.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:44.400 --> 03:20:51.040
One of these subjects was in the region&nbsp;
of Malayu, which during the Regreg War&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:51.040 --> 03:20:57.640
drifted under the sovereignty of the powerful&nbsp;
kingdom of Aayutaya in what is now Thailand.

03:21:02.640 --> 03:21:11.000
On the island of Sumatra, a Chinese pirate named&nbsp;
Chen Zooi from Guangdong even took advantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:11.000 --> 03:21:18.680
of the chaos caused by the regreg war to seize&nbsp;
control of the wealthy port city of Palumbang.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:19.720 --> 03:21:27.321
This man would run Palumbang as a pirate fortress&nbsp;
for the better part of a decade, using it as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:27.320 --> 03:21:33.960
base to raid the wealthy shipping that passed&nbsp;
through the Malaca Strait and using forged&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:33.960 --> 03:21:43.640
bronze seals to create counterfeit licenses and&nbsp;
aid smuggling into and out of China. The Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:43.640 --> 03:21:51.080
Muslim writer Mahan wrote about the situation&nbsp;
in his work, A Survey of the Ocean Shores.

03:21:53.640 --> 03:22:00.640
Chanzui plundered ships and prayed on both&nbsp;
native and foreign merchants for several years.

03:22:04.840 --> 03:22:10.640
On the northern coast of Borneo, the&nbsp;
Brune Kingdom had also begun to ignore&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:10.640 --> 03:22:21.720
the overlordship of Majapah. And by 1405, Western&nbsp;
Borneo had also drifted away. These small kingdoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:21.720 --> 03:22:28.880
would instead seek protection under the influence&nbsp;
of another power that was now flexing its muscles&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:28.880 --> 03:22:36.920
with increasing confidence on the waves.&nbsp;
That was the rising Ming dynasty of China.

03:22:54.680 --> 03:23:01.680
By this time, the Mongol Yuan dynasty had&nbsp;
collapsed and the Mongols had been forcibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:01.680 --> 03:23:09.201
thrown out of China. Now, a new dynasty&nbsp;
known as the Ming had taken its place.

03:23:11.960 --> 03:23:17.760
The Ming ruled over a devastated land that&nbsp;
had suffered immensely over the years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:17.760 --> 03:23:26.281
Mongol conquest and misrule by the Yuan emperors,&nbsp;
exacerbated by the ravages of the Black Death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:27.840 --> 03:23:30.640
But now, China was rebuilding.

03:23:33.800 --> 03:23:40.200
Ultimately, the Ming Dynasty has been remembered&nbsp;
as a paranoid and inward-looking state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:40.200 --> 03:23:46.480
which virtually bankrupted itself with the&nbsp;
construction of its vast Great Wall in the north,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:47.040 --> 03:23:49.480
sealing itself off from the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:50.840 --> 03:23:58.840
But at the start of the 15th century, Ming China&nbsp;
looked quite different. This was partly due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:58.840 --> 03:24:05.560
reign of an ambitious and outward-looking&nbsp;
ruler known as the Yong Lur Emperor.

03:24:24.880 --> 03:24:32.600
The Yonglur emperor was a patron of learning and&nbsp;
sponsored the writing of a great encyclopedia&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:32.600 --> 03:24:40.080
made up of 23,000 written scrolls in an&nbsp;
attempt to record all the world's knowledge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:41.640 --> 03:24:47.040
He was fascinated by everything and&nbsp;
especially the world outside of China&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:47.680 --> 03:24:56.600
as he recorded in his work Ming Taong Baoun&nbsp;
or the valuable teachings of Emperor Yong Lur.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:58.400 --> 03:25:04.400
Whenever I can find the time, I read history&nbsp;
books and the classics so as to avoid idol living.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:05.400 --> 03:25:11.000
I constantly remind myself that the world&nbsp;
is so vast and state affairs so important&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:11.560 --> 03:25:16.281
that I cannot succumb to laziness&nbsp;
and complacency for even a moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:17.240 --> 03:25:23.559
Once one has succumbed to laziness and&nbsp;
complacency, everything will become stagnant.

03:25:29.680 --> 03:25:36.361
As a result of this fascination with the outer&nbsp;
world, throughout his reign, he sent out a flurry&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:36.360 --> 03:25:44.160
of diplomatic missions to all corners of Asia,&nbsp;
letting everyone know that China was once again&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:44.160 --> 03:25:51.240
open for business. And as part of this effort,&nbsp;
he ordered the construction of a grand fleet&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:51.800 --> 03:25:59.200
designed to sail around the region and remind&nbsp;
the people of every land of the might of China.

03:26:08.680 --> 03:26:15.321
To build his fleet, the Yonglur Emperor&nbsp;
ordered the construction of a vast shipyard&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:15.320 --> 03:26:24.559
at a place called Liang or Dragon River.&nbsp;
This sprawling industrial complex was built&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:24.560 --> 03:26:32.040
at the point where the Yangti River is met by the&nbsp;
smaller Chin Wai in the Ming capital of Nanjing.

03:26:34.200 --> 03:26:38.280
And it would be the largest&nbsp;
shipyard anywhere in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:39.880 --> 03:26:46.720
On its seven long large docks, at least&nbsp;
100 ships could be built at any one time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:48.480 --> 03:26:56.320
To help in his mammoth effort, the Yonglur emperor&nbsp;
invited more than 20,000 craftsmen to work here&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:56.320 --> 03:27:03.280
on his ships. And timbers of elm, cedar, and&nbsp;
campfor were brought in enormous quantities&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:03.280 --> 03:27:10.639
to the city of Nanjing, transported from inland&nbsp;
ports down the rivers on flatbottomed barges.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:11.840 --> 03:27:18.481
The noise of the shipwrights shouting and calling.&nbsp;
The thump of the massive beams being lifted into&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:18.480 --> 03:27:24.120
place. The beating of chisels and hammers on&nbsp;
nails. All of this would have sounded over&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:24.120 --> 03:27:31.320
the rooftops of Nanjing in the early years of&nbsp;
the 15th century. For China, it was the sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:31.320 --> 03:27:38.920
of industry and progress. But for the power&nbsp;
of Majapahit far across the sea in the south,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:38.920 --> 03:27:44.520
it might as well have been the sound&nbsp;
of nails being driven into its coffin.

03:27:50.360 --> 03:27:57.400
By the year 1405, as the regreg&nbsp;
civil war dragged on in Majapay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:57.400 --> 03:28:05.799
the Yonglur emperor had completed his grand fleet.&nbsp;
And now all he needed was an admiral to lead it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:07.360 --> 03:28:12.799
The man he chose was a unic, a giant named Jungh.

03:28:17.760 --> 03:28:27.361
In some ways, Jungha was an unlikely candidate to&nbsp;
become China's most famous sailor and admiral. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:27.360 --> 03:28:34.240
one thing, he had barely any sailing experience.&nbsp;
But he had spent much of his life serving the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:34.240 --> 03:28:41.040
Yonglur Emperor as a military commander and&nbsp;
had proved himself a capable and loyal general.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:43.120 --> 03:28:50.000
Jungha was a Muslim born into a devout&nbsp;
family in the southwest of China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:50.000 --> 03:28:56.520
but he had been captured during the wars of&nbsp;
the preceding decades and had been castrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:56.520 --> 03:29:01.041
and brought up a unic as a kind of military slave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:03.240 --> 03:29:11.639
One writer named Yuan Jong describes his&nbsp;
towering stature with perhaps some exaggeration.

03:29:14.600 --> 03:29:23.321
He was 7 ft tall and had a waist about 5 ft in&nbsp;
circumference. His cheeks and forehead were high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:23.320 --> 03:29:30.799
but his nose was small. He had glaring eyes,&nbsp;
teeth as white and well-shaped as shells, and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:30.800 --> 03:29:38.760
voice as loud as a huge bell. He knew a great deal&nbsp;
about warfare, and was well accustomed to battle.

03:29:44.280 --> 03:29:53.120
On the 11th of July 1405, under the command&nbsp;
of Admiral Jung Ha, this vast fleet set sail&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:55.360 --> 03:29:58.400
and it would have made an awe inspiring site.

03:30:01.240 --> 03:30:07.480
It comprised a total of 317&nbsp;
vessels of the latest design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:07.480 --> 03:30:13.519
including at least 60 vast treasure ships&nbsp;
that were among the largest in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:15.160 --> 03:30:22.519
By some sources, these could have measured&nbsp;
more than 120 m in length and 50 in breadth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:23.200 --> 03:30:29.160
making them still among the largest wooden&nbsp;
sailing ships built anywhere in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:30.440 --> 03:30:38.559
These vessels were each fitted with 24 cast bronze&nbsp;
cannons and were beautifully decorated too with&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:38.560 --> 03:30:47.600
their prows carved with animal heads, dragons,&nbsp;
phoenixes and eagles all painted in bright colors.

03:30:53.880 --> 03:31:00.680
Like the ancient Greeks, Egyptians, Phoenicians,&nbsp;
and Romans, the Chinese painted eyes on the fronts&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:00.680 --> 03:31:08.160
of their vessels so that the ships seemed to take&nbsp;
on a life of their own and see their way through&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:08.160 --> 03:31:18.400
mists and stormy seas. On board these hundreds&nbsp;
of ships were a total of 28,000 men, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:18.400 --> 03:31:26.480
sailors and soldiers, but also diplomats,&nbsp;
astronomers, linguists, interpreters, and doctors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:27.120 --> 03:31:33.640
as well as carpenters, and ship builders for&nbsp;
if the vessels needed repairing while at sea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:35.080 --> 03:31:43.120
On one stone tablet that the admiral later&nbsp;
erected in Fujian, he describes this great voyage.

03:31:45.880 --> 03:31:53.240
We have traversed more than 100,000 lee of&nbsp;
immense water spaces and have beheld in the ocean&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:53.240 --> 03:32:02.360
huge waves like mountains rising sky high. And we&nbsp;
have set eyes on barbarian regions far away hidden&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:02.360 --> 03:32:11.679
in a blue transparency of light vapors. While our&nbsp;
sails loftily unfurled like clouds day and night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:11.680 --> 03:32:18.120
continued their course as rapidly as&nbsp;
a star traversing those savage waves.

03:32:23.800 --> 03:32:29.520
Although outfitted with cannons, the&nbsp;
treasure ships were not primarily warships.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:30.200 --> 03:32:37.440
They were in fact vast cargo vessels. Their&nbsp;
holds filled to the brim with Chinese silks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:37.440 --> 03:32:45.360
cotton, tea, wine, oil, iron, and&nbsp;
candles along with other luxuries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:45.360 --> 03:32:51.120
All intended for trade with the strange&nbsp;
lands they would encounter along the way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:52.440 --> 03:33:00.960
From these lands they brought back spices,&nbsp;
gemstones and even in the year 1414 a live giraffe&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:00.960 --> 03:33:06.000
which had been brought to Bengal by&nbsp;
some African delegates from Malindi.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:07.520 --> 03:33:10.840
Impressed at the sight of the&nbsp;
treasure fleet, these men had&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:10.840 --> 03:33:16.520
gifted one of these animals to the Chinese&nbsp;
who brought it home to no little amazement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:18.520 --> 03:33:25.800
The Yonglur emperor was no stranger to receiving&nbsp;
exotic animals as gifts. And there was even a part&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:25.800 --> 03:33:32.880
of his palace devoted to keeping and caring for&nbsp;
them. Elephants and rhinoceroses from Champa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:33.440 --> 03:33:40.200
bears from Siam, parrots and peacocks&nbsp;
from Java, and ostriches from Aiden.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:41.320 --> 03:33:47.759
But of all these, it was the giraffe&nbsp;
that he asked his court artists to paint.

03:33:52.880 --> 03:33:59.160
Alongside the vast treasure ships sailed a&nbsp;
countless array of different support vessels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:59.720 --> 03:34:08.720
some carrying horses and troops, others warships&nbsp;
for defense. Other boats were floating water&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:08.720 --> 03:34:15.920
tankers, ensuring the thousands of personnel&nbsp;
wouldn't go thirsty on long voyages between ports.

03:34:18.400 --> 03:34:26.360
The ships communicated between one another using&nbsp;
large flags, signal bells, a drum, and gongs&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:27.640 --> 03:34:36.080
along with 10 lanterns that could signal at night.&nbsp;
They even carried carrier pigeons to communicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:36.080 --> 03:34:43.960
over longer distances. The ultimate destination&nbsp;
of the treasure fleet was the city of Kolicode&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:43.960 --> 03:34:50.720
in the modern Indian state of Kerala. But the&nbsp;
fleet was ordered to stop at every large port&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:50.720 --> 03:35:00.401
and kingdom on the way to demonstrate the might&nbsp;
of the Ming Emperor. [laughter] It was in 1406&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:00.400 --> 03:35:08.719
that the Ming fleet arrived in Java while the&nbsp;
regreg civil war was reaching its bloody height

03:35:22.800 --> 03:35:29.880
when the Chinese treasure fleet arrived in Java.&nbsp;
They might have expected to encounter the scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:29.880 --> 03:35:37.600
of wealth and prosperity that had been recorded&nbsp;
by previous visitors, but they would find instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:37.600 --> 03:35:45.800
a troubled land devastated by civil war.&nbsp;
The treasure ships anchored off the coast&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:45.800 --> 03:35:53.080
and a group of Chinese soldiers and merchants&nbsp;
came ashore in western Java hoping to trade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:53.920 --> 03:36:01.680
But the western king Vikramina, the husband of&nbsp;
Haim Wuruk's daughter, believed that his rival&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:01.680 --> 03:36:09.441
in the east had brought the Chinese to intervene&nbsp;
in the civil war. While the Chinese visitors were&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:09.440 --> 03:36:16.440
trading in the marketplace, King Vikrama's&nbsp;
soldiers swept down on them from all sides.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:17.440 --> 03:36:22.559
The cries of the Javanese warriors and&nbsp;
the screams of the dying would have joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:22.560 --> 03:36:29.121
the pops and bangs of cannons that would have&nbsp;
sounded all around the marketplace. [cheering]&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:29.120 --> 03:36:36.280
The acrid smell of gunpowder and blood would&nbsp;
have lingered on the air. When the smoke cleared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:36.280 --> 03:36:44.320
170 Chinese merchants and sailors were dead,&nbsp;
and the rest had fled back to their ships.

03:36:47.320 --> 03:36:53.639
Admiral Jungha had enough soldiers with him&nbsp;
that he could have invaded Java in response.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:54.200 --> 03:36:58.160
And perhaps like the Mongols&nbsp;
more than a 100red years earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:58.160 --> 03:37:02.160
he could have decisively tipped&nbsp;
the balance in this civil war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:03.280 --> 03:37:11.880
But he was a pragmatic man, and he decided to&nbsp;
tread a more considered path. He treated his&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:11.880 --> 03:37:19.680
wounded on board the ships and sent envoys back&nbsp;
to the Yonglur emperor asking him what to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:21.240 --> 03:37:29.040
In the time of Hayam Wuruk 30 years earlier, China&nbsp;
had steered clear of conflict with the powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:29.040 --> 03:37:38.600
Majapah Empire, sending money and goods in order&nbsp;
to avoid a war. But now Majapah was weakened and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:38.600 --> 03:37:46.920
divided. This time it was King Vikramma who would&nbsp;
send a graveling apology to the Chinese court.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:47.800 --> 03:37:55.640
And the Ming Shilu or Records of the Ming&nbsp;
dynasty records what the emperor said in reply.

03:37:58.640 --> 03:38:05.201
There is no doubt as to your guilt. You&nbsp;
acted on the basis of greed and anger&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:05.200 --> 03:38:10.200
and you have acted against heaven. Is&nbsp;
there anything more serious than this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:10.840 --> 03:38:15.520
Just as I was about to raise an army&nbsp;
to punish you, I have seen that you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:15.520 --> 03:38:23.041
capable of repenting your mistakes, and I have&nbsp;
thus leniently stopped the dispatch of troops.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:23.680 --> 03:38:30.321
But what of the 170 people who died&nbsp;
innocently? You must immediately pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:30.320 --> 03:38:37.080
60,000 leang of gold as compensation for&nbsp;
their lives and to atone for your crime.

03:38:43.960 --> 03:38:51.240
A year later, a Javanese ship arrived in China&nbsp;
with only a sixth of the sum of gold that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:51.240 --> 03:38:57.719
Chinese emperor had demanded along with a&nbsp;
letter explaining that the bankrupt kingdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:57.720 --> 03:39:05.000
could not afford to pay anymore. The&nbsp;
Chinese emperor responded magnanimously.

03:39:07.960 --> 03:39:13.600
What I want from those people who live far&nbsp;
away is that they acknowledge their guilt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:14.160 --> 03:39:21.440
But I do not want to enrich myself with&nbsp;
their gold. The point had been made&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:22.040 --> 03:39:29.720
and the humiliation of Majapah was total.&nbsp;
Everyone in the region took notice.

03:39:32.240 --> 03:39:41.040
In 1424, Chinese records show that when one&nbsp;
king in Sumatra died, his son sent out a letter&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:41.040 --> 03:39:49.440
asking permission to succeed him as king. And&nbsp;
he sent it not to the kings of Majapah in Java,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:50.080 --> 03:39:53.960
but to the Chinese emperor in the Ming court.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:55.320 --> 03:40:04.799
This shows just how far the star of Majapahit had&nbsp;
fallen and how the might of China was on the rise.

03:40:25.600 --> 03:40:32.080
Although Admiral Jungh had declined to fight on&nbsp;
this occasion, the treasure fleet was more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:32.080 --> 03:40:40.240
capable of doing battle on the seas. And alongside&nbsp;
their main diplomatic and merkantile mission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:40.240 --> 03:40:45.840
its admiral had been given some less&nbsp;
peaceful tasks to complete on their voyage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:47.520 --> 03:40:54.560
Chief among these was a mission to destroy&nbsp;
the pirate fortress that still held out at&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:54.560 --> 03:41:02.481
the Sumatran port of Palumbang which had risen&nbsp;
up due to the weakness of the Majapahit Empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:03.600 --> 03:41:10.040
The Mingshi or history of Ming&nbsp;
describes how the pirate chief Chen Zooi&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:10.040 --> 03:41:16.519
had been playing a clever double game,&nbsp;
attempting to mllify the Ming Emperor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:18.000 --> 03:41:24.480
In the fourth year of the Yong Lay reign, the&nbsp;
pirate chieftain of the old port, Chenhu Yi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:24.480 --> 03:41:30.040
sent his son to the Ming court. However,&nbsp;
although he sent tributes to the court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:30.040 --> 03:41:36.519
he was plundering the seas and thus obstructing&nbsp;
the comingings and goings of the tributes envoys.

03:41:39.200 --> 03:41:46.080
On its way home through the straits of Malaca,&nbsp;
the enormous treasure fleet took its chance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:47.600 --> 03:41:55.121
They sailed along the mangrove crusted coast&nbsp;
of Sumatra towards Palumbang which the Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:55.120 --> 03:42:03.400
called the old port. We can only imagine the&nbsp;
site that greeted the pirates of Palamang&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:03.920 --> 03:42:08.360
as the hundreds of colorful ships&nbsp;
bore down on them on the horizon.

03:42:11.120 --> 03:42:17.840
When they arrived at the port city, they demanded&nbsp;
the surrender of the pirate chief Chen Zooi.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:18.600 --> 03:42:24.800
According to the Ming Shilu or records of the&nbsp;
Ming dynasty, the pirate chief attempted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:24.800 --> 03:42:32.441
use deception to gain the upper hand. When&nbsp;
he arrived at the old port of Palimang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:32.440 --> 03:42:39.360
he came across Ju Yi and others and sent people&nbsp;
to bring them to negotiate their pacification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:39.360 --> 03:42:45.759
Juu Yi and the others feigned surrender but&nbsp;
secretly plotted to attack the Imperial army.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:45.760 --> 03:42:52.720
Jeng Hur and the others found out about this and&nbsp;
marshalling the troops prepared their defenses.

03:42:56.880 --> 03:43:02.720
The ensuing battle would be the first test&nbsp;
of the power of the Yonglur Emperor's fleet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:04.360 --> 03:43:11.360
The Ming ships would have rammed into the pirate&nbsp;
vessels, hurled gunpowder bombs from catapults,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:11.920 --> 03:43:20.360
and fired special kinds of fireworks filled with&nbsp;
scraps of paper designed to spread fire and terror&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:20.360 --> 03:43:28.000
and ignite enemy sails. They would have&nbsp;
also sent volleys of flaming arrows&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:28.000 --> 03:43:37.080
flying towards the pirate ships. The result was&nbsp;
a resounding victory for the Ming treasure fleet.

03:43:42.320 --> 03:43:49.960
The pirate Jui suffered a great defeat. Over 5,000&nbsp;
of the bandit gang were killed while 10 of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:49.960 --> 03:43:57.400
bandit ships were burnt and seven were captured.&nbsp;
Further, two counterfeit bronze seals were seized&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:57.400 --> 03:44:04.719
and three prisoners, including Ju Yi, were taken&nbsp;
alive. When they arrived at the capital in chains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:44:04.720 --> 03:44:08.640
it was ordered that all of the&nbsp;
prisoners were to be beheaded.

03:44:12.240 --> 03:44:17.320
With this pirate threat crushed,&nbsp;
the Ming Chinese quickly moved to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:44:17.320 --> 03:44:22.759
establish a governor of the area to&nbsp;
prevent a resurgence of any piracy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:44:24.200 --> 03:44:32.760
The man they chose, a Muslim named Xi Jinqing,&nbsp;
had once been an official of the Majapet Empire.

03:44:35.360 --> 03:44:42.080
The old port pacification superintendency&nbsp;
was established and it was ordered that Xi&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:44:42.080 --> 03:44:48.960
Jinqing be appointed as the pacification&nbsp;
superintendent. A seal, a title patent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:44:48.960 --> 03:44:55.040
headwear and a belt, patented fine silks&nbsp;
and silk gauses were conferred upon him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:44:57.520 --> 03:45:03.321
While the rulers of Majapay had their&nbsp;
backs turned, fighting amongst themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:03.320 --> 03:45:10.360
the Ming Chinese had swept in and stamped&nbsp;
their seal of authority on the Malaca Strait.

03:45:21.360 --> 03:45:25.960
To strengthen this grip over&nbsp;
the narrow and crucial waterway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:25.960 --> 03:45:31.160
the Chinese would also bring under&nbsp;
their wing the young city of Malaca,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:31.720 --> 03:45:35.401
which had recently been growing&nbsp;
in wealth and importance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:36.360 --> 03:45:44.120
Malaca was a hub for another tide of change&nbsp;
that was now sweeping through the archipelago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:46.080 --> 03:45:51.320
That was the steady spread&nbsp;
of the religion of Islam.

03:46:04.720 --> 03:46:12.160
Islam had long had a presence in the Indonesian&nbsp;
archipelago and had arrived with traders who&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:46:12.160 --> 03:46:19.440
traveled back and forth across the Indian Ocean,&nbsp;
bringing nutmeg and cloves to the markets of Egypt&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:46:19.440 --> 03:46:26.559
and Arabia. But now this young religion&nbsp;
began to make advances in all directions.

03:46:28.840 --> 03:46:36.640
The European traveler Marco Polo remarked on the&nbsp;
change already underway on the island of Sumatra&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:46:36.640 --> 03:46:41.080
when he passed through the town of Perlac in 1292.

03:46:44.760 --> 03:46:51.000
The people of Pearlac used to be idoltors,&nbsp;
but owing to contact with Sariss merchants&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:46:51.000 --> 03:46:56.960
who continually resort here in their ships, they&nbsp;
have all been converted to the law of Muhammad.

03:47:05.480 --> 03:47:12.080
Part of the reason for the spread of Islam on&nbsp;
the archipelago was the prestige and protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:12.080 --> 03:47:20.400
that the religion brought. One example of&nbsp;
this in action is the king Parameasara,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:20.400 --> 03:47:27.160
the ruler of Singapura, who had been besieged&nbsp;
by Majapahit under the reign of Hayamuruk&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:27.720 --> 03:47:36.281
and had fled north to the coast of the Malaka&nbsp;
Strait. There he had founded the city of Malaca.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:37.120 --> 03:47:44.000
And in order to add an heir of legitimacy to&nbsp;
his new rule, Paramasara seems to have ditched&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:44.000 --> 03:47:52.120
his original Hindu Buddhist name and adopted a&nbsp;
new Persian one, calling himself Sultan Iscander&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:52.120 --> 03:48:01.000
Sha instead. Muslim traders preferred where&nbsp;
possible to trade with other Muslims who were&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:01.000 --> 03:48:08.320
more likely to share their languages of Arabic or&nbsp;
Persian, keep the same trading and prayer hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:08.320 --> 03:48:16.000
and shared a common moral code. And so this&nbsp;
connection helped Malaca to rapidly expand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:17.360 --> 03:48:23.080
The new city's strategic location, right&nbsp;
on the narrow sea corridor, meant the Ming&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:23.080 --> 03:48:30.040
Chinese were also very interested to bring its&nbsp;
rulers under their patronage and protection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:31.640 --> 03:48:37.600
When Admiral Jung Hur arrived at the&nbsp;
port town of Malaka, he left a stone&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:37.600 --> 03:48:44.441
tablet inscribed with a poem that officially&nbsp;
declared it under the protection of China.

03:48:48.840 --> 03:48:56.600
The vast southwestern sea reaches unto the middle&nbsp;
kingdom. Its waves cresting high as the heavens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:56.600 --> 03:49:04.640
watering the earth the same way for countless&nbsp;
eons. Washed by sunlight, bathed in the moonlight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:04.640 --> 03:49:13.000
rain swept cracks and dewy rocks in its midst&nbsp;
luxuriate with vegetation. And in such a place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:13.000 --> 03:49:19.400
people live in harmony. Its righteous king,&nbsp;
paying his respects to imperial power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:19.400 --> 03:49:25.719
wishes his country to be treated as one of our&nbsp;
imperial domains and to follow the Chinese way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:26.280 --> 03:49:35.160
So here, writ large on everlasting stone, we&nbsp;
do proclaim your loyalty. Posterity will smile&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:35.160 --> 03:49:42.440
upon your deed as time passes, and your sons&nbsp;
and grandsons will have limitless good fortune.

03:49:46.320 --> 03:49:53.759
In this, the Chinese were right. The port town&nbsp;
of Malaca would grow under Ming protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:53.760 --> 03:49:57.441
to become the largest and most&nbsp;
important in the whole region.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:59.200 --> 03:50:05.599
When Jungh returned a few years later, he&nbsp;
would build a fort at Malaca to protect it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:50:06.320 --> 03:50:12.200
And in return, the people of the city would ensure&nbsp;
that this crucial waterway would be controlled&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:50:12.200 --> 03:50:21.040
by China and China alone. Wealth and trade that&nbsp;
would have once flowed through the ports of Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:50:21.600 --> 03:50:26.121
now passed through the new&nbsp;
trading hub of Mala instead.

03:50:28.080 --> 03:50:34.920
From this hub of commerce, the influence of&nbsp;
Islam would spread all across the islands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:50:36.960 --> 03:50:42.440
For the kings of Java who ruled according to&nbsp;
legitimacy derived from their connection to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:50:42.440 --> 03:50:49.240
the Buddha and the god Shiva, each new&nbsp;
convert reduced their overall power.

03:50:52.520 --> 03:51:00.041
Subsequent voyages by Jung Hur's treasure fleets&nbsp;
would sail as far west as the straight of Hormuz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:00.840 --> 03:51:06.720
the city of Mecca and Moadishu and&nbsp;
Mombasa on the East African coast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:07.960 --> 03:51:13.640
But eventually the Ming would turn&nbsp;
away from oceangoing exploration.

03:51:24.400 --> 03:51:32.240
When the adventurous Yong Lur emperor died, he was&nbsp;
replaced by more inward-looking rulers who focused&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:32.240 --> 03:51:40.040
instead on the construction of the Great Wall in&nbsp;
the north. At one point, the Ming even outlawed&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:40.040 --> 03:51:47.720
the construction of large ships, dismantling some&nbsp;
and perhaps even burning some vessels in harbor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:49.280 --> 03:51:56.200
But the decades when the vast treasure fleets&nbsp;
had sailed the seas left a lasting impression&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:56.200 --> 03:52:05.639
on every culture that they touched, and the damage&nbsp;
done to the image of Javanese power was immense.

03:52:11.040 --> 03:52:20.000
Eventually, the regreg civil war would come to an&nbsp;
end in Java and the western king Vikramina Haim&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:52:20.000 --> 03:52:28.120
Wuruk's son-in-law would be victorious. Despite&nbsp;
his humiliating and bankrupting experience of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:52:28.120 --> 03:52:35.040
angering the Chinese emperor, he managed to close&nbsp;
in on his enemies in the eastern court. And in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:52:35.040 --> 03:52:42.080
final bloody confrontation, the western forces&nbsp;
broke through the walls of the eastern palace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:52:43.440 --> 03:52:48.599
The lord of Fraabumi fled in&nbsp;
the night and escaped by boat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:52:49.760 --> 03:52:57.640
He was hunted down and beheaded and his severed&nbsp;
head was brought back to the capital of Majapahit.

03:52:59.800 --> 03:53:04.680
The civil war was over and the&nbsp;
kingdom was united once more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:53:05.720 --> 03:53:14.080
But the conflict had exacted a terrible cost.&nbsp;
In only 2 years of fighting, the manpower of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:53:14.080 --> 03:53:22.360
Majapahit had been severely depleted. Its treasury&nbsp;
was on the verge of bankruptcy and its influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:53:22.360 --> 03:53:28.719
had been drastically curtailed right at the point&nbsp;
when powerful rivals were rising in the north&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:53:29.320 --> 03:53:33.360
and the religion of Islam was&nbsp;
spreading from port to port.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:53:35.000 --> 03:53:41.600
The center of the mandala of power had&nbsp;
now shifted irrevocably away from Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:53:42.360 --> 03:53:49.759
And the age of Majapahit's Empire of the&nbsp;
Islands would slowly but surely come to an end.

03:53:59.760 --> 03:54:06.760
While Majapahit lost much of its overseas empire&nbsp;
through the beginning of the 15th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:07.400 --> 03:54:11.719
it still remained the preeeminent&nbsp;
power on the island of Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:12.280 --> 03:54:20.519
But steadily, its loss of regional status began&nbsp;
to erode its power even in its heartlands.

03:54:23.360 --> 03:54:33.559
When King Vikram Vodina died in 1429, his daughter&nbsp;
Suittita took to the throne. She was prepared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:33.560 --> 03:54:41.800
rule in the tradition of the great Javanese queens&nbsp;
who had preceded her a century earlier. Diaragatry&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:41.800 --> 03:54:50.401
and Tribuano Vijaya Tungadevi. But for a number&nbsp;
of reasons, her rule would be beset by problems.

03:54:53.120 --> 03:55:01.080
Chief among these was a terrible famine&nbsp;
that descended on Java around the year 1426&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:01.080 --> 03:55:08.720
caused by a drought. But the only source&nbsp;
for this is the chronicle of the Paraton&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:09.680 --> 03:55:16.720
and by this point its reports are becoming&nbsp;
sparse and scarce and contain little detail.

03:55:25.040 --> 03:55:29.640
Next, there was a period of&nbsp;
food shortage that was very long&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:29.640 --> 03:55:32.000
in the year of the serpents biting people.

03:55:34.600 --> 03:55:42.040
Unfortunately for historians, the decline of&nbsp;
royal power in Java also resulted in a disruption&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:42.040 --> 03:55:51.040
of the major sources that we have relied on up&nbsp;
to this point. Narrative texts like the paraton&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:51.040 --> 03:55:57.000
or book of kings were crucial sources&nbsp;
during the golden age of Majapahit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:57.600 --> 03:56:04.920
But as we enter the 15th century, the text becomes&nbsp;
increasingly obscure and difficult to understand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:06.000 --> 03:56:12.960
The chronicles notes get shorter concerning only&nbsp;
earthquakes and eruptions and the deaths of kings&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:13.560 --> 03:56:16.321
and they come further and further apart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:18.080 --> 03:56:23.760
The final three pages of the Javanese&nbsp;
Book of Kings are just a few scraps&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:23.760 --> 03:56:32.880
of seemingly unrelated information separated&nbsp;
by decades with its final note coming in 1481.

03:56:41.040 --> 03:56:43.920
Then a volcanic eruption occurred&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:43.920 --> 03:56:48.800
in the wake of Watu Ganung in the&nbsp;
Saka action of the sky sea echore

03:56:51.560 --> 03:56:58.361
perhaps in an attempt to alleviate the effects&nbsp;
of the drought. Suitta had constructed a vast&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:58.360 --> 03:57:04.719
reservoir named Kolam Seagaran just&nbsp;
to the north of the city of Majapahit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:05.600 --> 03:57:13.800
This great pool was 800 m by 500 and&nbsp;
ringed all around by banks of red brick.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:15.120 --> 03:57:21.040
Whether or not this alleviated the famine,&nbsp;
we will never know. But it shows the drastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:21.040 --> 03:57:27.320
situation that the empire was facing around&nbsp;
this time under pressure from the environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:27.960 --> 03:57:38.360
from internal divisions, and increasingly from&nbsp;
powerful rivals. When Suhita died in 1447,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:38.360 --> 03:57:46.960
her brother Ka Vagaya ruled for only four&nbsp;
years. He was followed by a further four kings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:46.960 --> 03:57:53.481
all of whom ruled for only a short time, and&nbsp;
the relationships between them are unclear.

03:57:56.680 --> 03:58:04.401
The kingdom erupted in civil war again&nbsp;
in the 1470s. And in 1478, one pretender&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:04.400 --> 03:58:10.480
broke through the defenses of Majapahit and&nbsp;
killed his brother who sat on the throne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:12.280 --> 03:58:20.080
That year in Java would be given a name that seems&nbsp;
to convey the mood in the kingdom at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:20.080 --> 03:58:28.400
Sir Elanging Bumi, which has been translated&nbsp;
as vanished and lost is the order of the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:30.560 --> 03:58:36.920
Upon his victory, this new king had&nbsp;
inscribed a series of stone inscriptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:36.920 --> 03:58:43.560
that made dire warnings of the fate that would&nbsp;
befall any who failed to support his rule.

03:58:47.840 --> 03:58:52.280
They will end up in the realm&nbsp;
of Yama, the king of the dead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:53.520 --> 03:59:00.121
They will be beaten by Yama's army for years&nbsp;
innumerable. They will fall in the cauldron of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:00.120 --> 03:59:07.559
hell. They will not be reborn. So, not only will&nbsp;
they end up in hell, but they will stay there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:08.360 --> 03:59:13.679
And even if they are reborn, they will&nbsp;
be reborn under miserable circumstances.

03:59:17.360 --> 03:59:24.519
It seems clear from these inscriptions that the&nbsp;
new king didn't feel at all secure on the throne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:26.360 --> 03:59:32.000
Throughout this period of political&nbsp;
instability, the situation for Majapay&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:32.000 --> 03:59:39.000
would be made increasingly worse by the rise&nbsp;
of a new kind of power on the coasts of Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:40.240 --> 03:59:45.120
That is the everinccreasing&nbsp;
influence of Muslim traders

04:00:07.200 --> 04:00:12.720
with the powerful rival of Mal.&nbsp;
Laka now exerting its influence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:13.400 --> 04:00:19.960
Muslim merchants had become wealthier and&nbsp;
more influential in Java than ever before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:21.200 --> 04:00:28.880
And in the vacuum left by royal power, these&nbsp;
wealthy merchants became politically powerful too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:30.640 --> 04:00:37.120
Now more and more people on the coasts all&nbsp;
around the archipelago began to flirt with&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:37.120 --> 04:00:46.320
the idea of this new religion. The Portuguese&nbsp;
traveler Tom Perez describes the steady advance&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:46.320 --> 04:00:53.799
of Islamic power in Java at the expense of&nbsp;
the Hindu Buddhists who he calls heathens.

04:00:57.840 --> 04:01:05.200
At the time when there were heathens along the sea&nbsp;
coast of Jaba, many merchants used to come. Paris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:05.200 --> 04:01:12.840
Arabs, Gujaratis, Bengales, Malaise and other&nbsp;
nationalities. There being many Moors among them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:13.680 --> 04:01:20.760
They began to trade in the country and to grow&nbsp;
rich. They succeeded in way of making mosques&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:20.760 --> 04:01:28.840
and mulas came from outside. In some places the&nbsp;
heathen Japanese lords themselves turned muhamadan&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:28.840 --> 04:01:32.120
and these merchant moors took&nbsp;
possession of these places.

04:01:36.800 --> 04:01:44.441
The growing influence of these coastal traders&nbsp;
would culminate in 1475 with the foundation of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:44.440 --> 04:01:53.080
true Muslim kingdom on the island of Java. A power&nbsp;
that would call itself the sultenate of Denmark.

04:02:01.640 --> 04:02:09.401
A port town looking north into the Java Sea,&nbsp;
Denmark had been founded in the late 15th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:09.400 --> 04:02:14.839
possibly by Chinese merchants, and its&nbsp;
people had built a grand mosque there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:14.840 --> 04:02:19.320
combining elements of Muslim&nbsp;
and Javanese architecture.

04:02:21.960 --> 04:02:30.280
The ruler of Damak was a man named Raden Pata who&nbsp;
was a vassel swearing allegiance to Majapahit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:31.320 --> 04:02:36.759
But he soon declared independence&nbsp;
and claimed the title of Sultan.

04:02:39.760 --> 04:02:47.080
As Majapahed declined, the Sultanate&nbsp;
of Demach would expand. It conquered&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:47.080 --> 04:02:56.680
the coastal region of Tuban by 1527 and was&nbsp;
spreading in land as far as Malang by 1546.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:58.160 --> 04:03:03.599
For the dwindling power of Majapet,&nbsp;
this was the final death nail.

04:03:18.240 --> 04:03:22.639
Due to the slow petering out&nbsp;
of royal chronicles in Java,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:22.640 --> 04:03:31.281
we have few details about what exactly happened to&nbsp;
lead to the eventual destruction of Majapahit. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:31.280 --> 04:03:38.360
at this point, we are able to turn to the writings&nbsp;
of some outside visitors to the island of Java.

04:03:44.120 --> 04:03:50.720
Around 1513, about 100 years&nbsp;
after the end of the Regreg War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:50.720 --> 04:03:54.640
the Portuguese traveler Tom Perez visited Java.

04:03:57.880 --> 04:04:05.401
He wrote in glowing terms about the beauty of the&nbsp;
landscape and the abundance of the island. But he&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:05.400 --> 04:04:14.000
found the power of Majapahit already a shadow of&nbsp;
its former glory. As he recounts in his memoirs,

04:04:16.560 --> 04:04:23.000
they say that the island of Jaba used to rule&nbsp;
as far as the spice islands on the eastern side&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:23.000 --> 04:04:29.360
and over a great part of the west. and that it&nbsp;
had almost all the islands of Somatra under its&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:29.360 --> 04:04:38.320
dominion and all the islands known to the Japanese&nbsp;
and that it had all this for a long time passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:38.320 --> 04:04:45.719
until about a 100 years ago when its power began&nbsp;
to diminish and it came to its present state.

04:04:49.560 --> 04:04:54.880
Perez also alludes to some degree&nbsp;
of political instability in Java.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:55.800 --> 04:05:01.920
According to his account, the power of its kings&nbsp;
had diminished so that they were now little more&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:01.920 --> 04:05:08.640
than a figurehead with the Mahapati or prime&nbsp;
minister appearing to wield greater power.

04:05:11.640 --> 04:05:16.840
Because the Japanese have lost a great part&nbsp;
of their lands, the kings do not command,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:16.840 --> 04:05:22.000
nor are they taken into account, but&nbsp;
only the viceroy and chief captain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:22.640 --> 04:05:30.120
This man is known and honored like the real king.&nbsp;
All the lords of Java obey him. Him they honor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:31.000 --> 04:05:36.360
This governor commands in everything.&nbsp;
He holds the king of Java in his hands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:36.360 --> 04:05:45.040
He orders him to be given food. The king has no&nbsp;
voice in anything, nor is he of any importance.

04:05:48.480 --> 04:05:55.080
Alongside this weakening of royal authority&nbsp;
came the fragmentation of the kingdom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:56.120 --> 04:06:01.880
Local lords became steadily more&nbsp;
powerful with some even breaking away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:02.480 --> 04:06:07.879
And now the Muslim sultenates&nbsp;
began to taste blood in the water.

04:06:21.000 --> 04:06:28.880
The sultan of the city of Demach, Raden Pa,&nbsp;
knew that only one thing stood between him&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:28.880 --> 04:06:37.240
and ultimate power on the island of Java.&nbsp;
That was the lingering remnants of Majapahit.

04:06:39.320 --> 04:06:46.280
After only a few years, he gathered his&nbsp;
forces and marched on the now isolated city.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:48.320 --> 04:06:54.759
The later Muslim epic verse chronicle, the&nbsp;
Babad Tana Jawi, recounts what happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:54.760 --> 04:07:02.720
next in heroic terms as the Muslim forces&nbsp;
bore down on the once great capital city.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:07:05.080 --> 04:07:12.120
Then they agree to gather together all Muslims&nbsp;
with their arms. Then they set out to Majapahed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:07:12.640 --> 04:07:17.960
The number of troops was immeasurable.&nbsp;
The city of Majapahed was encircled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:07:18.560 --> 04:07:24.481
Many inhabitants submitted to Raden&nbsp;
Pa. Nobody dared to meet him in battle.

04:07:27.640 --> 04:07:34.201
Marching under blue and yellow banners,&nbsp;
they soon arrived at the walls of Majapahit.

04:07:36.280 --> 04:07:42.679
The later Muslim chronicles imitated&nbsp;
elements of previous Javanese legends&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:07:42.680 --> 04:07:50.321
and claimed that the last king of Majapahit was so&nbsp;
aed at the sight of the army approaching the city&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:07:50.320 --> 04:07:57.360
that he simply ascended to heaven right there on&nbsp;
the spot and the city was captured peacefully.

04:07:59.560 --> 04:08:04.560
At the moment Prabhu Bra&nbsp;
with Jaya ascended to heaven,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:04.560 --> 04:08:10.840
something like a fireball was seen coming&nbsp;
out of the palace of Maja, it flashed&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:10.840 --> 04:08:17.880
like a bolt of lightning and sounded like a&nbsp;
terrifying thunderclap. It fell down in Binda.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:18.600 --> 04:08:24.720
Radan Pa entered the palace. He was astonished&nbsp;
that he did not encounter a single person.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:25.520 --> 04:08:32.201
He wept in his heart, then left the palace&nbsp;
and returned to Bintara with his troops.

04:08:36.400 --> 04:08:43.160
But we can assume that the end of Majapahit&nbsp;
was not as peaceful as these later chronicers&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:43.160 --> 04:08:52.559
make out. The historian Herald Vanderinda imagines&nbsp;
an extreme version of the scenes that unfolded.

04:08:55.200 --> 04:09:01.519
The soldiers met almost no resistance and within&nbsp;
an hour they had taken control of the royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:09:01.520 --> 04:09:10.640
palace. It was a massacre. Priests were drowned&nbsp;
in lotus ponds. Stable boys were stabbed as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:09:10.640 --> 04:09:17.401
tried to protect the king's horses. Everything&nbsp;
was burned to the ground. The royal residences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:09:17.400 --> 04:09:23.320
the watchtower, the pavilion with its&nbsp;
intricate wood carvings, the army barracks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:09:23.320 --> 04:09:32.120
the elephant stalls, and the library. By noon, all&nbsp;
that was left of Maja Pahit was a heap of rubble.

04:09:37.040 --> 04:09:42.360
However, there is little evidence of&nbsp;
widespread burning at the site of the city.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:09:43.000 --> 04:09:50.480
and no heaps of bodies or mass graves&nbsp;
have ever been found. But what's clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:09:50.480 --> 04:09:57.839
is that at this point, human habitation at&nbsp;
the city of Majapahit largely came to an end.

04:10:03.440 --> 04:10:08.240
Its people fled and all of&nbsp;
its houses and workshops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:10:08.240 --> 04:10:13.440
its temples and palaces were&nbsp;
left to fade and crumble.

04:10:16.040 --> 04:10:19.960
For many ordinary people, life simply went on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:10:20.960 --> 04:10:25.200
But it must have been a time of&nbsp;
great upheaval and confusion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:10:26.760 --> 04:10:34.920
The Javanese text, the Niti Shastra, records one&nbsp;
vision of the end of the world that must have&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:10:34.920 --> 04:10:43.960
captured the mood of many on the island of Java&nbsp;
as their world came to an end and another began.

04:10:47.040 --> 04:10:53.720
The signs of the approaching end of this world&nbsp;
will be all kinds of depravity among mankind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:10:54.280 --> 04:11:00.599
That is to say, the wise will turn&nbsp;
foolish. The holy men will become worldly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:11:01.160 --> 04:11:08.559
Children will abandon their parents.&nbsp;
Princes will lose their empires. The little&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:11:08.560 --> 04:11:17.040
will become great and commit depradations.&nbsp;
In short, everything will be in confusion.

04:11:31.600 --> 04:11:39.000
From this moment on, the Sultanate of Demach would&nbsp;
be the most powerful force on the island of Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:11:39.840 --> 04:11:45.160
and the age of the Hindu Buddhist kingdoms&nbsp;
on the island would come to an end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:11:47.120 --> 04:11:54.920
Most remaining Hindus and Buddhists would flee&nbsp;
east and took refuge on the small island of Bali&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:11:55.640 --> 04:11:59.241
which became a sanctuary&nbsp;
for their ancient culture.

04:12:02.320 --> 04:12:09.480
Epic Hindu poems like the Bulman Taka were&nbsp;
largely forgotten on the mainland of Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:12:10.120 --> 04:12:20.240
but in Bali they were preserved. One verse in this&nbsp;
poem describes the destruction wrought by a demon&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:12:20.240 --> 04:12:27.200
king on the religious sanctuaries of a kingdom.&nbsp;
And we might find it a fitting description&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:12:27.200 --> 04:12:32.720
for the final days of Java's&nbsp;
last Hindu Buddhist sanctuaries.

04:12:35.640 --> 04:12:43.720
Heaven is being destroyed and hermitage is swept&nbsp;
away because of the swarming demons. The hermits&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:12:43.720 --> 04:12:52.760
have left, have abandoned all and fled far away,&nbsp;
constantly pursued the miserable wretches. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:12:52.760 --> 04:12:59.600
bodies are lying about in the monasteries with&nbsp;
their bark clothing and woven bags flung away.

04:13:03.160 --> 04:13:07.400
With its people gone, the city of Majapay&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:13:07.400 --> 04:13:12.480
would have been swiftly reclaimed by&nbsp;
the fast growing forest around it.

04:13:16.240 --> 04:13:22.400
The sophisticated drainage system that its&nbsp;
engineers had spent generations perfecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:13:22.400 --> 04:13:29.120
that channels the slooes and great reservoirs&nbsp;
would have begun to silt up within a decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:13:29.120 --> 04:13:36.240
without maintenance. The water with nowhere&nbsp;
left to go would have pulled and spread,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:13:36.240 --> 04:13:41.160
turning streets into marshland&nbsp;
and marshland into swamp.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:13:42.400 --> 04:13:49.360
The red bricks that gave the ruins of Majapahit&nbsp;
its distinctive character were fired from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:13:49.360 --> 04:13:57.360
volcanic clay of the Bras River plane. And in&nbsp;
the wet heat of Java, they didn't last long.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:13:59.080 --> 04:14:08.280
Mosses would take hold first, then lychans&nbsp;
and ferns. The mortar softened and dissolved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:14:08.280 --> 04:14:15.519
The strangler figs and banyan trees would move&nbsp;
in next, clambering over the red brick courtyards&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:14:15.520 --> 04:14:24.321
and ceremonial gateways of the Majapahit capital.&nbsp;
their roots clenching the walls in their grasp and&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:14:24.320 --> 04:14:33.840
steadily but surely pulverizing the brick. Slower&nbsp;
growing teak and mahogany trees would arrive next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:14:34.360 --> 04:14:41.759
shading the crumbling walls of Majapahit&nbsp;
beneath their canopies. As all this went on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:14:42.280 --> 04:14:49.200
the great rumblings of Java's volcanoes would&nbsp;
have conspired to hide the ruins even further.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:14:51.080 --> 04:14:58.720
More than 10 major eruptions of the nearby&nbsp;
Arjuno Wellerang and Anjas Moro volcanoes&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:14:58.720 --> 04:15:06.120
took place in the centuries that followed. Each&nbsp;
one burying the site of Majapahit in ever more ash&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:15:06.720 --> 04:15:12.920
until some of its structures lay as much&nbsp;
as 4 m beneath the surface of the earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:15:14.160 --> 04:15:19.639
In the year 1849, a Dutch traveler&nbsp;
called Walter Robert Van Hovl&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:15:20.160 --> 04:15:28.040
passed through the site of the old city and&nbsp;
described the overgrown ruins that still remained.

04:15:31.800 --> 04:15:38.401
The entire ruins were shrouded in a dark forest&nbsp;
and covered with shrs and trees. Majestic Banyan&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:15:38.400 --> 04:15:44.280
trees have rooted their roots between the stones&nbsp;
and pushed walls apart, raising their crowns high&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:15:44.280 --> 04:15:52.440
above the ruins. That is why nowadays the Javanese&nbsp;
also call the place Waning Lang or Banyan Gate.

04:16:02.760 --> 04:16:10.840
The Muslim sultenates that destroyed Majapahit&nbsp;
would not remain the most powerful force in Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:16:10.840 --> 04:16:19.920
for long. The next century would see the arrival&nbsp;
of other seaborn powers that would soon form&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:16:19.920 --> 04:16:29.640
their ownthaloscracies or empires of the sea.&nbsp;
These were the powers of European colonialism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:16:31.320 --> 04:16:37.080
In 1511, the port town of Malaka&nbsp;
was seized by the Portuguese&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:16:37.680 --> 04:16:42.520
and from there their influence would&nbsp;
spread all over the archipelago.

04:16:44.760 --> 04:16:50.760
Soon other European powers would arrive&nbsp;
and they would all begin to influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:16:50.760 --> 04:16:58.281
the fracturous political life of the island of&nbsp;
Java and introduce the beginnings of an ideology&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:16:58.280 --> 04:17:04.759
more seductive and powerful than any&nbsp;
religion, the force of capitalism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:17:06.560 --> 04:17:13.560
The Sultanates of Java would soon come under&nbsp;
the sway of a massive international conglomerate&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:17:13.560 --> 04:17:20.281
and one of the first joint stock companies&nbsp;
in the world, the Dutch East India Company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:17:21.600 --> 04:17:27.361
In 1598, a Dutch fleet of eight&nbsp;
ships under Jacob Van Neck&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:17:27.360 --> 04:17:32.599
became the first from the Netherlands&nbsp;
to reach the spice islands of Maluku.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:17:34.240 --> 04:17:41.360
In doing so, they bypassed the Javanese&nbsp;
merchants who had long dominated the trade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:17:42.840 --> 04:17:52.080
When these Dutch ships returned to Europe in the&nbsp;
year 1599, laden with cloves, nutmeg, and mace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:17:52.080 --> 04:18:01.400
the expedition yielded an enormous&nbsp;
profit of around 400%. One Dutch observer&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:18:01.400 --> 04:18:07.480
would reportedly make the following remark&nbsp;
upon seeing the arrival of this fleet.

04:18:10.040 --> 04:18:16.560
As long as Holland has been Holland, there have&nbsp;
never arrived ships as richly laden as these.

04:18:19.400 --> 04:18:25.000
The age of European colonialism&nbsp;
in Southeast Asia had now begun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:18:27.040 --> 04:18:36.560
Between6602 and 1796, the Dutch East India Company&nbsp;
dispatched almost a million Europeans to Asia&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:18:36.560 --> 04:18:44.520
aboard nearly 5,000 ships, dwarfing the traffic of&nbsp;
its nearest rival, the British East India Company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:18:45.080 --> 04:18:52.559
returning with more than 2.5 million&nbsp;
tons of Asian goods and enslaved people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:18:55.680 --> 04:19:02.960
Throughout this colonial period, which would&nbsp;
ultimately see Java become a European colony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:19:02.960 --> 04:19:10.720
the legacy of the ancient Javanese kingdoms&nbsp;
was largely ignored or intentionally obscured.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:19:11.760 --> 04:19:17.361
The Dutch in particular had no desire&nbsp;
to see the people of their new colonies&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:19:17.360 --> 04:19:24.599
rally around the memory of any ancient kingdoms&nbsp;
and mostly declined to investigate the ruins&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:19:24.600 --> 04:19:34.040
that could be found dotted around Java. The erasia&nbsp;
was so extensive that in his history of the Indian&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:19:34.040 --> 04:19:42.400
archipelago published in 1820, the British writer&nbsp;
John Crawford was able to write the following.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:19:44.040 --> 04:19:48.080
The theory of a great monarchy and of&nbsp;
an antecedent state of high civilization&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:19:48.080 --> 04:19:52.440
and improvement has been forged by&nbsp;
the national vanity of the Javanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:19:52.440 --> 04:19:57.960
unsupported by a shadow of proof and&nbsp;
contradicted by unquestionable internal evidence.

04:20:02.240 --> 04:20:11.679
But some memory of Majapahit's glory lived on&nbsp;
in the minds of Java's people in their songs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:20:11.680 --> 04:20:19.720
their poetry, their shadow puppet plays, and in&nbsp;
the manuscripts they kept in their libraries.

04:20:24.280 --> 04:20:33.080
In 1894, in a typical expansion of European power&nbsp;
in East Asia, the Dutch colonialist government&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:20:33.080 --> 04:20:39.320
intervened on the island of Lombok to&nbsp;
the west of Bali to depose its king,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:20:39.840 --> 04:20:47.520
a man named Aung Ketut Karangasam, who&nbsp;
was a renowned collector of ancient texts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:20:49.160 --> 04:20:54.120
After a protracted war in which&nbsp;
the local people resisted fiercely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:20:54.120 --> 04:21:02.080
the Maharaja's palace was finally stormed by&nbsp;
Dutch marines on the 19th of November 1894.

04:21:03.920 --> 04:21:10.880
The Dutch poured into the palace and began&nbsp;
a slaughter in which 2,000 were massacred.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:21:12.360 --> 04:21:18.719
The Maharaja was captured and died a&nbsp;
few months later in a colonial prison.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:21:19.720 --> 04:21:25.160
During the fighting, Dutch artillery&nbsp;
set fire to the Maharaja's palace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:21:25.160 --> 04:21:32.200
And while its towers burned and its roofs fell&nbsp;
in, the colonial troops looted the king's treasury&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:21:32.760 --> 04:21:42.361
and carried away more than 130 kg of gold,&nbsp;
3,000 of silver, and countless works of art.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:21:44.120 --> 04:21:52.240
This would become known as the Lombok treasure.&nbsp;
But among the lutters there was also a Dutchman&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:21:52.240 --> 04:22:01.440
named Yan Lawrence Andre Brandes an expert&nbsp;
in Sanskrit and old Javanese. This man had&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:22:01.440 --> 04:22:09.120
heard that the Maharaja Karangasam was an avid&nbsp;
scholar and a collector of rare manuscripts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:22:11.240 --> 04:22:19.080
As the Maharaja's palace burned, this man ran&nbsp;
to the ruler's library and saved what he could.

04:22:23.120 --> 04:22:29.880
While others ran with gold heaped in their arms,&nbsp;
Brandis frantically gathered all of the ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:22:29.880 --> 04:22:39.360
books he could carry and fled before the roof of&nbsp;
the palace could collapse. Much of the Maharaja's&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:22:39.360 --> 04:22:45.360
collection went up in flames, and we will never&nbsp;
be able to read what was contained within it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:22:46.240 --> 04:22:52.800
But what Brandes was able to save was packed&nbsp;
into a box and sent back to the colonial capital.

04:22:57.360 --> 04:23:04.200
Inside he would later find the only&nbsp;
existing copy of the poem the desavana&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:23:04.200 --> 04:23:10.120
written by the self- aacing scribe&nbsp;
propanka some 500 years earlier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:23:11.680 --> 04:23:17.361
In the pages of that work the voice of&nbsp;
the poet could once more bring to life&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:23:17.360 --> 04:23:24.480
the description of its great king on his journey&nbsp;
around the island. the lives and concerns of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:23:24.480 --> 04:23:34.280
people of medieval Java. And at least in words,&nbsp;
the kingdom of Majapet could live once more.

04:23:59.840 --> 04:24:08.401
I'd like to end this episode with an extract from&nbsp;
an old Javanese poem called the Shiva Ratri Kalpa&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:24:08.960 --> 04:24:17.320
or the night ritual of Shiva written during the&nbsp;
years of the decline of the Majapahit kingdom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:24:18.760 --> 04:24:25.640
In this passage, a hunter named Lugdaka&nbsp;
stumbles upon the ruins of an ancient temple&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:24:26.280 --> 04:24:32.719
and like Thomas Stamford Raffles centuries&nbsp;
later, would be struck by the strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:24:32.720 --> 04:24:41.200
melancholy of these places where once so&nbsp;
many lives passed, now overgrown with jungle.

04:24:47.280 --> 04:24:53.040
As you listen, imagine how it would&nbsp;
feel to watch the palaces and temples&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:24:53.040 --> 04:25:01.640
of the kingdom of Majapah crumbling into the earth&nbsp;
as its people left and its reservoirs ran dry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:25:03.160 --> 04:25:07.040
Imagine wandering beneath&nbsp;
the shade of that teak forest&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:25:07.040 --> 04:25:14.240
and watching the roots of banyan and silk cotton&nbsp;
trees slowly bind their way over the ruins&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:25:15.160 --> 04:25:24.760
as the layers of ash fell from the sky season&nbsp;
after season and covered the works of man. Imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:25:24.760 --> 04:25:34.080
turning away and setting off into the forest&nbsp;
as the sun finally set on Java's golden age.

04:25:40.720 --> 04:25:46.680
A great temple complex from ancient&nbsp;
times rose near a mountain stream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:25:47.400 --> 04:25:55.040
and the path there was lonely. The curved trunks&nbsp;
of the water elephants had fallen and crumbled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:25:55.040 --> 04:25:58.440
and for lack of care, its&nbsp;
wall had almost tumbled down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:25:59.480 --> 04:26:05.040
The monster heads seemed to be weeping as their&nbsp;
covered faces were overgrown with creepers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:26:05.680 --> 04:26:11.160
and as though sad and weary, the temple&nbsp;
guardians lay rolled over on the ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:26:12.640 --> 04:26:18.201
The pavilions in its courtyard were in ruins.&nbsp;
Some of the buildings were now only wreckage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:26:18.720 --> 04:26:23.520
while others were rotting away. Their&nbsp;
roofs were broken and had fallen in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:26:24.200 --> 04:26:28.960
and beyond repair their pillars&nbsp;
stood a skew, swaying back and forth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:26:30.360 --> 04:26:38.080
Heartending was the spectacle of the reliefs.&nbsp;
Young maidens in stone stood, gazing skywards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:26:38.640 --> 04:26:43.441
full of sorrow, because now no&nbsp;
wandering poets ever came to visit them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:26:44.640 --> 04:26:50.560
The tower temple soared on high and its&nbsp;
pinnacles served as a gathering place for weeds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:26:51.520 --> 04:26:57.361
The sides were cracked, overgrown by a&nbsp;
shady fig tree which spread luxurantly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:26:58.640 --> 04:27:05.401
All the statues were cracked by a fearsome&nbsp;
tangle of vines, and only the main deity within&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:27:05.400 --> 04:27:10.160
was immovably in place, standing&nbsp;
firm in the center of its pedestal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:27:11.160 --> 04:27:17.400
Pandan trees hung down over the rocks with&nbsp;
an air of sadness quite wrapped in the mist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:27:18.720 --> 04:27:24.000
How they longed to be visited by the thunder&nbsp;
which would bring with it the rushing of the rain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:27:25.440 --> 04:27:28.320
As if in sorrow they let their petals droop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:27:29.200 --> 04:27:33.519
Petals on which someone had jotted down&nbsp;
some words in a moment of inspiration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:27:34.880 --> 04:27:40.401
For is this not an admirable place for&nbsp;
a wandering poet to weave his verses.

